incompatibility of the two communication strategies: a factual statistical argument and an
emotional narrative designed to trigger selective recall involve different rhetorical modes and
are difficult to combine effectively within a single message.","6 Conclusion
This paper studies persuasion that works not by supplying evidence but by changing which
evidence comes to mind. A Bayesian decision-maker who understands that recall is ma-
nipulated corrects for it, and this correction disciplines what manipulation can achieve:
manipulation cannot move the average posterior, and it backfires on individuals with few
memories, whose sparse recall reads as evidence against the favored state. But the cor-
rection stays bounded while recalled evidence accumulates, so manipulation is effective for
large memories, thickens the right tail of beliefs and, under Poisson memory, lowers its hazard
21
rate. In the first application, a monopolist selling an expensive experience good manipu-
lates consumers’ recall and, because manipulation makes demand less elastic at high prices,
charges a higher price. In the second, a politician facing a demanding voter withholds all
but his strongest evidence and manipulates recall instead; when voters choose whether to
listen, only those whose own impressions already favor him do, creating an echo chamber.
Given the growing evidence that memory shapes belief formation, the model is a first step
toward understanding communication that works through recall, and we encourage further
theoretical and empirical work on this channel.",cues
