2610.29804v1,Conservation in Every Quantum Measurement Outcome?,"We also argue that the proposed extension to several noncommuting conserved quantities is problematic. Second, independently of these justificatory concerns, we examine whether the mechanism actually establishes the stronger form of conservation claimed.","4 
Conclusion




We have examined the proposal of 
[
2
]
 according to which conservation laws can be restored at the level of individual quantum measurement outcomes by including the physical system responsible for preparing the measured state. The proposal highlights an important and often neglected point: the preparation history of a quantum state may matter when questions of conservation are considered. Nevertheless, we have argued that the stronger conclusions drawn from this observation are not established.






At the level of justification, the framework relies on substantive assumptions that do not follow from the reference-frame considerations invoked in their support. In particular, the necessity of a physical reference system and of a reference-frame-type preparation interaction is assumed rather than demonstrated, while recursive application of the same reasoning generates further difficulties and the extension to several noncommuting conserved quantities remains problematic. Moreover, even granting the framework, the resulting mechanism appears to preserve only statistical properties of the relevant conserved quantity rather than establishing conservation of a definite quantity in the stronger, non-statistical sense claimed. The claim of locality is correspondingly weakened, and in the case of energy the interaction term required for the preparation process prevents the total energy from being identified simply with the sum of the subsystem energies. We therefore conclude that the proposal does not establish conservation in every quantum measurement outcome in the general sense claimed.",cues
