Conclusion




6.1 
Limitations and future work




Our evidence rests on two synthetic families of nets, and it is worth being precise about what that supports. The two benchmarks measure different things: the supervised recovery of the parameters that generated a net, and the geometry of a self-supervised latent space shared with two other views of the same process. Whether either construction helps other self-supervised objectives, or process-similarity tasks evaluated by behavior rather than by generation factors, remains to be tested.






There are two additional caveats regarding DA-PNGNN that we want to name rather than bury. The first is that both benchmarks have unit arc weights, so the multiplicity distinction of Figure 
1
, which motivated the kernels, is never tested explicitly in our experiments. The kernels are computed from the weighted incidence matrix and would register that distinction, but whether doing so helps a model remains untested. The second caveat concerns scaling. Pair kernels need 
