6.2 
Concluding remarks




We asked whether the structure that Petri net theory makes explicit can improve learned representations of these formal process models. To this end, we exposed two kinds of structure to GNNs. The first captures where each node sits relative to directed cycles, through PNRWPE, and the second how the incidence matrix balances token flow, through the diffusion kernels of DA-PNGNN. The kernels improved results on both benchmarks: the supervised recovery of generation factors on PDC 2023, and the self-supervised multimodal objective of ProcRosetta, where the configuration chosen on PDC was used without retuning. A control that zeroes the kernels while keeping every parameter and every same-type connection lost the entire gain, so the improvement comes from the kernel values rather than from added capacity or connectivity. The walk encoding helped most where nets contain directed cycles and, as its derivation predicts, carried almost no information on acyclic nets. We see this as an instance of a broader pattern: when data arrives with its own established symmetries, useful inductive biases are already encoded in its geometry, and the work is to find an operator that carries them inside the learning model. Using this modern geometric deep learning approach for learning on Petri nets is, in our view, a promising and still largely unexplored direction at the intersection of machine learning and process mining.",cues
