Conclusions




In this paper, we have investigated the statistical accuracy of model-based quantile distributional policy evaluation as the quantile resolution increases. Although a finer quantile representation introduces more parameters and increasingly extreme quantile levels, it does not necessarily lead to greater estimation error. Under uniformly positive reward densities, root-
n
n
 estimation remains possible uniformly over all resolutions in every Wasserstein distance. Even when reward densities vanish at the support endpoints, this resolution-independent rate persists through 
W
2
W_{2}
 without any quantitative restriction on boundary decay. In contrast, higher-order Wasserstein distances are more sensitive to the estimation errors of extreme quantiles, leading to a potentially stronger dependence on the resolution.






Beyond these non-asymptotic guarantees, we have characterized the exact local asymptotic minimax risk at each fixed resolution and established its attainability by the quantile fixed point estimator. This characterization provides a statistical benchmark for examining the resolution dependence of the estimation error. Our fixed-instance analysis shows that the optimal local risk can remain bounded across resolutions, while under endpoint-vanishing reward densities it can also exhibit the polynomial growth predicted by our non-asymptotic bounds, up to logarithmic factors. Thus, the resolution dependence of the estimator reflects an intrinsic statistical difficulty at a fixed MDP.",cues
