2610.29506v1,"Asynchronous, Generation-Free ABC: Streaming AMIS Reweighting for Heterogeneous Simulator Workloads on HPC","In our experiments, the method achieves well-calibrated posterior estimates where a pyABC baseline with the same kernel and bandwidth schedule under-covers. From the asynchronous run’s timing alone, we predict how much a synchronization barrier would slow the sampler.","9 
Conclusion




Generation-based ABC can leave workers idle while slow simulations finish. We quantify this synchronization penalty using simulation times recorded during an asynchronous run and validate the prediction against a synchronized variant on synthetic runtime distributions and a tissue simulator. The generation-free sampler removes the barrier and, wherever a simulation costs more than a few milliseconds, converts the simulations it gains into a tighter tolerance. The full-history posterior estimator can be reconstructed from the simulation log. Our analysis characterizes its potential deviation from the smooth-ABC posterior through a fidelity ratio that is only partly measured. The estimator is well calibrated in the one-dimensional tests but conservative for some parameters of the four-dimensional benchmark. What it delivers is bounded by the starting bandwidth and the archive size, and §
7
 says how to set them.






Broader Impact Statement




This work is methodology for likelihood-free inference on parallel machines. Its direct effect is on compute: it removes the idle time a generation barrier imposes on heterogeneous simulator workloads, and the prediction of §
6.1
 lets a practitioner estimate that saving from their own timing data before spending an allocation. The method does not introduce an application-specific risk beyond those associated with the models and inferences to which it is applied.








Reproducibility




The code is available at 
https://github.com/ju-he/async-abc
. It contains the implementation (the asynchronous sampler as a Propulate propagator, the matched-kernel pyABC baseline, the benchmarks and runners), every run configuration, the cluster launchers and the test suite, together with the summary data behind every figure and table, the scripts that regenerate each, and the committed output of every diagnostic quoted in the text, so every figure, table and quoted number can be regenerated from the repository alone. The raw per-particle records (
≈
30
{\approx}30
 GB for the Gaussian-mean experiment alone) and the per-worker timing logs will be released publicly upon publication.








