2610.29083v1,Rare-Feature-Adaptive Subsampling for Big Data Regression,"Although
simple, it ignores differences among individual observations, and we use it as a baseline RC
design. We aim to derive optimal RC designs in the sense of minimizing the variance of the
subsample estimator or its transformation.","8 Discussion
This paper studies subsampling for regression models with rare features. We show that the
estimators of coefficients associated with rare features have convergence rates determined
by the number of observations expressing the corresponding features, rather than by the
full-data sample size. This finding explains why rare features remain difficult even in mas-
sive datasets and why subsampling can further aggravate the problem when rare-feature
30
observations are underrepresented. Motivated by this insight, we develop an RA subsam-
pling framework. We propose optimal RC designs, an inverse-prevalence modification of
Lopt, and a balanced design. We also develop a union-sample aggregation procedure that
incorporates pilot information while accounting for overlap between pilot and second-step
samples. Simulation studies and applications to real datasets show that the proposed meth-
ods reduce identifiability failure, improve estimation efficiency, and provide more accurate
covariance estimation than existing subsampling approaches.
These results should be interpreted within the modeling framework studied in this paper.
In particular, the estimating equations are assumed to be correctly specified, and the dimen-
sion of features is fixed. The rare features are binary covariates, including dummy-coded
categorical features; highly correlated rare-feature structures are not considered.
Several avenues remain for future research. First, since the challenges associated with
rare features stem directly from the covariate structure, they warrant investigation beyond
the model framework considered here. A natural direction is to extend the proposed meth-
ods to broader statistical settings, such as clustered, longitudinal, and survival data models.
Second, the impact of rare features on inferential tasks beyond point and interval estima-
tion, including hypothesis testing, model selection, and variable screening, remains to be
explored. Third, high-dimensional regimes, particularly settings where the number of rare
features increases as the sample size grows, will require new theory. Fourth, extending these
scaling and sampling schemes to non-parametric regression or tree-based machine learning
algorithms, where rare features are easily neglected by default splitting rules, is another
direction. Finally, the effects of oversampling observations carrying rare features warrant
further study. A systematic comparison of estimation efficiency between such oversampling
designs and our proposed subsampling methods remains an open question.",cues
