We presented a benchmark for differentially private quantum learning on clinical and wearable health data. The framework protects each clinical record or wearable participant and compares models at matched input dimensionality. The QNN exceeded the matched MLP on clinical datasets by up to 0.104 average ROC AUC across privacy budgets; however, it performed worse on wearable data, while LR remained competitive. No model difference was significant after Holm correction. The gradient bound showed that avoiding clipping can increase noise, and circuit ablations showed that design choices affect performance under DP. Hardware predictions closely matched exact simulation, with a mean absolute probability error of 0.046 for the private checkpoint. These findings support feasible inference on quantum hardware; however, they do not establish quantum advantage. Future work should evaluate larger cohorts under preregistered analyses, investigate alternative private training mechanisms, and tighten sensitivity bounds. Hardware evaluations should be repeated across devices and sessions with recorded physical-qubit layouts.",cues
