7.2 
A transition in clump formation mechanisms




Taken together, our measurements and the simulations of 
Mandelker et al. (2017)
 and 
Nakazato et al. (2024)
 suggest a transition in the dominant clump formation pathway with both cosmic time and stellar mass, broadly consistent with recent observational studies 
(
de la Vega et al., 2026
; 
Sok et al., 2026b
)
. At lower redshifts and higher stellar masses, especially around cosmic noon, the high values of 
f
clumpy
f_{\rm clumpy}
 are difficult to explain through major mergers alone, although interactions have long been considered a possible origin of clumpy morphologies 
(
Puech, 2010
; 
Ribeiro et al., 2017
)
. In Section 
5
, we showed that the merger-driven fraction inferred from the major merger rates of 
Puskás et al. (2025)
 generally falls below the observed 
f
clumpy
f_{\rm clumpy}
 in the higher-mass bins, even when assuming longer clump observability timescales. This implies that additional formation channels beyond major mergers are required. Minor mergers may also contribute through accreted satellites or interaction-induced star formation, as discussed by 
Mandelker et al. (2014)
 and 
Guo et al. (2015)
, and more recently by 
de la Vega and Mobasher (2026)
. Nevertheless, the most natural interpretation for the high 
f
clumpy
f_{\rm clumpy}
 in massive galaxies is 
in situ
 gravitational fragmentation in gas-rich discs, as predicted by the VDI framework of 
Dekel et al. (2009)
 and demonstrated in numerical simulations 
(
Agertz et al., 2009
; 
Ceverino et al., 2010
; 
Bournaud et al., 2014
; 
Inoue et al., 2016
; 
Mandelker et al., 2017
)
. Recent analytical models by 
Ferrara et al. (2026)
 similarly predict a broad intrinsic clump mass spectrum and mass–size relations from gravitational instability, with subsequent evolution influenced by dynamical friction and feedback. This interpretation is also supported by the companion JADES study of 
Zhu et al. (2026)
, who find clump mass functions and radial trends consistent with formation in turbulent discs, followed by migration, disruption, or structural evolution, as explored in earlier theoretical studies 
(
Ceverino et al., 2012
; 
Mandelker et al., 2014
; 
Dekel et al., 2022
)
.






At the low-mass and high-redshift end, the interpretation changes. In our lowest stellar-mass bin, 
8
≤
log
10
⁡
(
