<
8.5
8\leq\log_{10}(M_{\star}/{\rm M_{\odot}})<8.5
, the clumpy fraction remains comparatively low and nearly flat with redshift. However, as shown in Section 
5
, the merger-driven fraction inferred from the major merger rates of 
Puskás et al. (2025)
 can become comparable to the observed clumpy fraction at high redshift, suggesting that major mergers could account for a substantial fraction of these systems. This remains sensitive to the assumed pair observability timescales 
(
Lotz et al., 2010
; 
Snyder et al., 2017
; 
Duncan et al., 2019
)
, as well as the duration of the detectable clumpy phase and the assumption that every merger produces detectable clumps. This interpretation is qualitatively consistent with 
Nakazato et al. (2024)
, who find predominantly merger-induced clumps at 
z
≈
6
−
9
z\approx 6-9
, although their simulations target galaxies selected to reach higher stellar masses. Minor mergers may also contribute by triggering off-centre star formation 
(
Renaud et al., 2015
; 
Calabrò et al., 2019
)
, as recently illustrated by 
de la Vega and Mobasher (2026)
.






The intermediate-mass regime, 
9
