, is likely where the transition becomes most apparent. In this mass range, our clumpy fractions are substantially higher than in the lowest-mass bin, while the comparison with the major merger rates of 
Puskás et al. (2025)
 indicates that mergers alone are increasingly insufficient towards lower redshifts. At higher redshifts, however, merger-induced clump formation could still contribute substantially, consistent with the FirstLight simulations of 
Nakazato et al. (2024)
. We therefore interpret this as a mixed regime, in which both merger-related processes and 
in situ
 gravitational fragmentation contribute, with the latter becoming increasingly important as galaxies assemble more massive, gas-rich discs, as suggested by 
Mandelker et al. (2017)
. This picture is broadly consistent with 
de la Vega et al. (2026)
, who argue that disc fragmentation dominates at lower redshifts and 
log
