, while mergers become more important at higher redshifts and intermediate stellar masses.








7.3 
Gravitational instabilities and the role of gas




It is important, however, not to interpret disc fragmentation and VDI too narrowly. Classical VDI is often framed in terms of fragmentation in a marginally stable disc with Toomre parameter 
Q
≈
1
Q\!\approx\!1
 
(
Toomre, 1964
; 
Dekel et al., 2009
)
, but high-redshift galaxies are not necessarily isolated, axisymmetric systems. Using cosmological simulations, 
Inoue et al. (2016)
 showed that clumps can form in highly perturbed, non-linear discs even when the smoothed pre-collapse regions have 
Q
Q
 values of order unity to a few. They discuss several possible mechanisms, including non-axisymmetric instabilities, rapidly decaying turbulence, and externally stimulated instabilities associated with minor mergers or counter-rotating gas streams. Stellar feedback can further regulate clump formation and survival, with simulations demonstrating a strong dependence on feedback prescriptions 
(
Mayer et al., 2016
; 
Oklopčić et al., 2017
; 
Mandelker et al., 2017
)
. Therefore, when we refer to a disc fragmentation or VDI-like origin, we mean 
in situ
 gravitational fragmentation in gas-rich, turbulent systems, including externally stimulated instabilities, rather than only the idealised linear Toomre instability of an isolated smooth disc.






This broader 
in situ
 interpretation is physically motivated by the conditions of high-redshift galaxies. Observations show that molecular gas fractions increase strongly with redshift 
(
Tacconi et al., 2010
; 
Tacconi et al., 2020
; 
Wang et al., 2022
)
, while 
JWST
 imaging reveals substantial disc-like populations even at early epochs 
(
Ferreira et al., 2023
; 
Kartaltepe et al., 2023
)
. Kinematic studies further indicate that rotationally supported, often turbulent systems are already present at high redshift 
(
Förster Schreiber et al., 2018
; 
Wisnioski et al., 2019
; 
Übler et al., 2019
; 
Parlanti et al., 2023
; 
Danhaive et al., 2025
; 
Lee et al., 2025
)
. In sufficiently massive discs, higher gas surface densities can reduce the Toomre stability parameter and increase the characteristic fragmentation scale, promoting the formation of massive star-forming clumps 
(
Dekel et al., 2009
; 
Ceverino et al., 2010
; 
Mandelker et al., 2017
; 
Krumholz et al., 2018
)
. At the same time, simulations by 
Di Matteo et al. (2008)
 and 
Renaud et al. (2015)
 show how gas-rich mergers can trigger enhanced star formation through tidal compression, while 
Nakazato et al. (2024)
 demonstrate the formation of merger-induced clumps at high redshift. Therefore, high gas content does not uniquely favour one channel: in massive discs it promotes 
in situ
 fragmentation, while in low-mass disturbed systems it can amplify the clump-forming effect of galaxy interactions.






Our results therefore support a gradual, mass- and redshift-dependent transition in clump origin, rather than a simple division between merger-driven clumps at high redshift and fragmentation-driven clumps at later times. At fixed redshift, interactions and accreted companions may contribute more strongly towards lower stellar masses 
(
Ribeiro et al., 2017
; 
Zanella et al., 2019
; 
Nakazato et al., 2024
)
, whereas sufficiently massive, gas-rich discs may undergo 
in situ
 fragmentation even at early epochs, as demonstrated in the simulations of 
Mayer et al. (2025)
. This is also compatible with the model of 
Krumholz et al. (2018)
, in which turbulence in high-redshift discs is driven predominantly by gravitational instabilities and radial transport, with stellar feedback becoming increasingly important at later times. The contrasting evolutionary trends in our sample, from nearly constant clumpy fractions at low stellar masses to a pronounced increase towards cosmic noon in more massive galaxies, are also consistent with this picture. Our comparison with the major merger rates of 
Puskás et al. (2025)
 is central to this interpretation: where the inferred merger-driven fraction is comparable to 
f
clumpy
f_{\rm clumpy}
, a merger origin is plausible, whereas a substantial excess of 
f
clumpy
f_{\rm clumpy}
 over the merger expectation requires additional formation channels, most naturally 
in situ
 fragmentation.
Figure 
17
 therefore supports an increasingly important contribution from gravitational fragmentation at higher stellar masses and later cosmic times, broadly consistent with 
de la Vega et al. (2026)
; 
Sok et al. (2026b)
. However, minor mergers remain unconstrained by our comparison, the highest-mass regime at 
z
≳
5
z\gtrsim 5
 is poorly sampled, and the merger-rate measurements do not extend below 
z
=
3
z=3
.










