, the observed fractions substantially exceed merger-driven expectations, even for the longest assumed observability timescales.




(v)




Our results favour a gradual, mass- and redshift-dependent transition between 
ex situ
 and 
in situ
 clump formation. Mergers and accreted companions may contribute more prominently in low-mass galaxies at high redshift, while gravitational fragmentation in gas-rich, turbulent discs becomes increasingly important in more massive systems towards cosmic noon. These mechanisms are not mutually exclusive, as interactions may themselves trigger internal gravitational instabilities.










Our findings support a gradual, mass- and redshift-dependent transition in the relative importance of 
in situ
 and 
ex situ
 clump formation. External interactions and accretion may contribute more prominently in low-mass galaxies at early epochs, while gravitational instabilities in gas-rich discs become increasingly important in more massive systems. This suggests that clumpy morphologies arise from an evolving interplay between internal and external processes, rather than a single dominant formation mechanism across cosmic time.






Important questions remain regarding the physical origins, survival, and evolution of individual clumps, as well as their contribution to galaxy growth. Future studies combining deeper and wider 
JWST
 surveys with high-resolution imaging using adaptive optics and spatially resolved spectroscopy from next-generation 30-metre-class telescopes will help address these questions. In particular, measurements of clump kinematics, stellar populations, and chemical abundances will provide stronger constraints on their formation mechanisms and their role in the structural evolution of galaxies.








