Conclusion




This paper established a rigorous approximation framework for continuous-time controlled diffusions under partial information. By constructing a discrete-time Euler–Maruyama approximation, we proved that the resulting partially observable model has several regularity properties. Specifically, we established the total variation continuity of the observation kernel and the weak continuity of the state transition kernel, which together guarantee the weak Feller property of the corresponding non-linear filter.






These results justify the quantization of the action, belief, and observation spaces. Through a step-by-step error analysis, we demonstrated that this sequential chain of approximations preserves near optimality: the approximation errors introduced by time discretization and quantization vanish asymptotically, and the Euler–Maruyama substitution error admits an explicit 
O
⁡
(
h
)
O(\sqrt{h})
 bound, uniformly over policies. Consequently, an optimal policy derived from the resulting fully finite, computable POMDP is asymptotically near optimal for the original continuous-time system. This establishes a complete, theoretically verified path from intractable continuous-time stochastic control problems to implementable numerical methods.",cues
