2610.28327v2,Observation Assimilation Transformer: Probabilistic Weather Analysis from Sparse Station Observations,"Observations serve as training target, and if the learned model does not rely on station-specific embeddings, it can be transferred to arbitrary locations, under the assumption that topographic features alone are sufficient to describe the local processes. We aim to combine elements from each paradigm into a data-driven framework that can correct NWP biases, generate higher resolution fields, and condition probabilistic predictions on observations.","5 
Discussion




Inspired by classical statistical post-processing and recent advances in neural processes 
(
Ashman et al., 2024a
; 
Ashman et al., 2024b
)
, we introduced OAT, a novel architecture that refines NWP fields by leveraging high-resolution topographic information and sparse station observations.
Benchmarked against several strong baselines, OAT achieves competitive predictive performance on both a small and a large scale dataset, with the latter demonstrating the scalability of our approach (see Appendix 
F
 for detailed analysis). The model is highly flexible with respect to the choice of output distributions and handles missing observations in a straightforward manner. A particularly useful feature of OAT is its ability to inspect predictions before and after observations are assimilated, making it possible to quantify the contribution of the observational input. We further showed that OAT generalizes to regions not seen during training, enabling spatial transferability, while its performance is limited at the tails of the training distribution (e.g., precipitation at high altitudes). Finally, in case studies of extreme events, OAT appropriately increases its predictive uncertainty, indicating robust uncertainty quantification in these scenarios.






Despite these findings, OAT has some limitations. First, the model only captures the marginal predictive distribution at each location, rather than the correlations and joint distribution across locations. Consequently, samples at different locations are conditionally independent: although the marginals are well calibrated, a field composed of independent samples at each location does not represent a physically meaningful precipitation scenario. Second, OAT does not currently incorporate dense radar observation products, which, from a meteorological perspective, are essential for obtaining spatially coherent precipitation fields. Third, OAT provides no straightforward way to distinguish measurement noise from model uncertainty, a separation that arises naturally in the Bayesian GP framework.






Several directions could address these limitations. Modeling correlated noise with neural processes, as proposed by 
Bruinsma et al. (2021)
, would be a natural extension of OAT, as would the inclusion of additional dense observational inputs. Additionally, spatial coherence could be encouraged through a multivariate scoring rule such as an energy score, a multivariate generalization of the CRPS, evaluated jointly across locations. Alternatively, OAT’s architecture could be embedded within conditional generative denoising diffusion 
(
Ho et al., 2020
)
 or flow matching frameworks 
(
Lipman et al., 2023
)
.






In this work, we focused on the instantaneous refinement of NWP fields. Incorporating past and future observations is a promising direction for further improving predictions. In OAT, this can be achieved naturally by treating time as an additional coordinate alongside location and elevation. OAT could then even be used for forecasting, trained to predict future observations conditioned on past ones, which would make it directly comparable to the models by 
Miralles et al. (2026)
 and 
Yang et al. (2024)
.






Beyond the setting considered here, we believe OAT is applicable to a broader range of problems. One example is the refinement of sea surface temperature or ocean current fields using observations from floating surface drifters 
(
Botvynko et al., 2025
)
, a challenging task due to the continuously changing positions of the measurements.






In conclusion, OAT is a versatile model that demonstrated strong performance on the NWP refinement task considered here, offering multiple avenues for future extensions.








