Continuous composite endpoints provide a coherent way to incorporate clinically meaningful 
intercurrent events , such as death , directly into the endpoint definition, ensuring that the 
occurrence of the event itself is reflected when post- event outcomes are non-existent or no 
longer meaningful. These endpoints retain a continuous measure of treatment effect. 
However, when the estimand is defined using a mean summary measure, the choice of failure 
value becomes a critical and consequential design decision. We derived a formula showing 
that the relative variance of these endpoints depends on the frequency of the ICEs and in 
particular the extremity of the failure value. Highly penalising values, although clinically 
intuitive, can substantially increase variance and reduce power. In extreme cases these values 
cause the endpoint to collapse into a pseudo -binary measure, that is difficult to interpret 
clinically.  
This paper demonstrates that the failure value should not be treated as a secondary 
implementation detail, but as a core component of the estimand that requires explicit clinical 
justification and statistical evaluation. Distribution- based failure value de finitions such as a 
quantile from a control- arm population distribution offer a pragmatic option in situations 
where there is no clear alternative, generating some clinical interpretability while allowing 
control over statistical operating characteristics. Where no acceptable failure value can 
balance the above considerations, alternative summary measures such as the median should 
be considered.  Further research is needed on both the estimand definition (including 
multidisciplinary consensus-building) and its estimation. 
 
