2610.28040v1,Using Letter Positional Probabilities to Assess French Word Complexity,"We discuss possible explanations as to how complexity could be encoded in orthography. Keywords:

French; word complexity; letter positional probability; linguistics
1 
Introduction




This paper examines French word complexity using letter positional probabilities (LPPs).","7 
Conclusion




This study has examined the role of LPPs in carrying a simplicity/complexity signal in French words. We examined the distribution of LPPs in simple as opposed to complex words. We found that there were 145 LPPs that were significant out of a potential cohort of 441. Because the average length of simple words was 6.69, we focused on the first 6 positions because we wished to reduce the confounding effect of length of LPPs in positions >6. We also found that several structural variables were associated with complexity, with length being particularly predictive of complexity in our sample.






We found that there were a number of phenomena associated with LPPs in relation to complexity. We found that 5 out of 6 of the significant LPPs containing an accented letter were associated with lower complexity, while 6 out of 6 of the significant LPPs containing an unaccented letter were associated with higher complexity. The overall finding was that there are significant differences in the orthography of simple as opposed to complex words.






We went on to create classifiers using LPPs and structural data as features to predict binary simple and complex words. We found that length alone achieves an accuracy of 77.27%. Additional structural variables added little to classification accuracy. The best classifier achieved an accuracy of 83.95% and consisted of the first 6 LPP features plus Length. We concluded from this that both orthography and length are significant features associated with complexity.






The question we went on to examine is how orthography can encode complexity. Certainly it is conventionally accepted that word length is associated with complexity, and in the current study it was indeed the variable most closely associated with complexity. However, positing the idea that the placement of individual letters is predictive of complexity is significantly more controversial.






We considered an explanation from Zipf that more common words become simplified over time due to articulatory economy. The important point here for our purposes is that simplicity is associated with frequency in that words that are repeated often become simplified.






We observed that the question of the association between vowel/consonant patterns and complexity may be worth considering in subsequent work, as is the possibility that there may be a difficulty in disentangling the difference between word complexity and word frequency.








Funding




This research received no external funding.








Conflict of Interest




The author declares no conflict of interest.








Data Availability




The Python scripts used to generate the data used in this study can be obtained from the author at admin@michaeldalvean.com.",cues
