. Building on this framework, we investigate whether these sampling stages preserve distinctions between parameter values.","Conclusion




We established conditions for local and global parameter identifiability from distributions of recorded crossings under a fixed MT sampler. The ranks of distribution derivatives cannot increase through crossing, timing, and selection. Under the respective regularity, rank, and topological assumptions, local identifiability is characterized by a nonsingular Jacobian of finitely many event probabilities, and global identifiability by properness of the parameter-to-distribution map onto its image.






For the specified nonhomogeneous Poisson signal model with four intensity parameters, a fixed known single-photon response, and deterministic MT sampling with time quantization, a single sufficiently small positive threshold ensures local and global identifiability under their respective conditions. The construction uses four cumulative probabilities of the first recorded crossing time and attains the minimum number of thresholds for this model and sampler. Numerical experiments reveal weak sensitivity and substantial finite-sample errors when estimating shared parameters from the corresponding five categories. The minimum threshold count establishes uniqueness under the stated conditions, while accurate estimation also requires attention to parameter sensitivity.",cues
2610.27648v1,Speak vs. Know: Balancing Behavior and Knowledge Distributions of Large Language Models,"Thus, neither expression method alone can serve as a faithful simulator. In this study, we aim to construct a distribution that preserves the shape of the knowledge distribution while following the most likely answer of the behavior distribution.","8 
Conclusion




In this paper, we distinguished what an LLM knows about a group’s opinions, captured by its verbalized distribution, from how it would actually behave at deployment, captured by its log-probability distribution.
Because the two disagreed on the most likely answer for a large fraction of questions, a high verbalized alignment score did not describe what a practitioner observed once the model was sampled, and a high alignment score did not, by itself, indicate that the distribution reflected the model.
Motivated by this, we proposed a learnable, behavior-constrained balanced distribution that preserves the shape of the knowledge distribution while anchoring its mode to the model’s behavior, controlled by a single hyperparameter.
Across six models and three datasets, the balanced distribution consistently achieved higher net utility on average than the baseline distributions.
We recommend that future work on LLM opinion simulation report behavioral consistency alongside distributional alignment.








