This work provides a simple example of how the abstract mathematical structure underlying a given quantum information task can be used to directly inform the design of a quantum experiment. In particular, the optimal four-copy detector is first identified from the quartic structure of the hyperdeterminant. Then, from a representation-theoretic perspective, the same measurement can be understood through the local 
J
=
0
J=0
 sectors and the projection onto the trivial irreduciple representation of the relevant four-copy representation. This observation allowed us to decompose the corresponding local 
J
=
0
J=0
 projectors into elementary gates and thus turn an abstract optimality result into a concrete circuit.






More broadly, our results suggest that finding a direct circuit for a nonlinear quantum quantity should not be viewed as the end of the problem. Once such a measurement is known, one can still ask how much of the available signal it captures and whether another measurement can extract the same information with fewer state preparations. Indeed, identifying the optimal measurement before compiling it into a circuit therefore provides a route for connecting the mathematical structure of quantum information with the practical resource requirements of quantum experiments.






Finally, the circuits introduced here may also be useful as test cases for early fault-tolerant devices. They are relatively small, but already combine transversal Clifford operations with non-Clifford rotations implemented through magic-state injection or STAR. Since the ideal click probabilities are known exactly, one can directly study how logical errors modify the statistical advantage of the different detectors. In particular, the 
