Discussion




We have presented a criterion to decide which overlapping signals must be
reanalysed jointly when one wants to test a different model for some target source than the
one used in an initial global fit so that any extra effect in the new model is not absorbed and masked by other nuisance signals. This criterion is obtained by computing the overlap between the directions that a group of nuisance sources spans in data space and the
direction of an extra effect absent from the first model.
The absorption is captured by two numbers. The masking ratio 
ℛ
\mathcal{R}

of Sec. 
6
 is the factor multiplying the detection
significance when an effect is present, so that a deviation the global fit would
establish at 
n
σ
n_{\sigma}
 is reported by post-processing at

ℛ
​
n
σ
\mathcal{R}\,n_{\sigma}
. The null ratio 
ℛ
0
\mathcal{R}_{0}
 quantifies the
miscalibration when no effect is present. It can be interpreted as the factor by which the
posterior shrinks,
so the bounds that post-processing reports are tighter
than those from the full global fit by 
ℛ
0
\sqrt{\mathcal{R}_{0}}
. For an effect of unknown size, the loss lies between the two, so
the smaller of

ℛ
\mathcal{R}
 and 
ℛ
0
\sqrt{\mathcal{R}_{0}}
 serves as a single,
conservative
measure. For a single beyond-vacuum parameter,
the two
coincide.
Both can be computed by reusing the posterior samples of the initial vacuum global fit. We also
construct an algorithm that ranks the nuisance sources by how strongly they mask a
specific beyond-vacuum effect in a given target source. The rationale behind this is to keep the
smallest subset that reproduces the inference of the full global fit, and to
refit only those sources jointly with the target in a down-scaled reanalysis.






The linear-signal predictions for the uncertainties, the ratios,
the recovered values and the down-scaled bounds were all confirmed by full MCMC
analyses on a toy model that included one target source with a possible deviation and 20 nuisance sources.






Our methods are directly applicable to LISA data-analysis pipelines. The LISA
Science Ground Segment currently plans to release catalogues of sources
characterised with a global fit using vacuum GR only. The
method described here could be used to assess for which sources and which
types of beyond-vacuum GR deviations robust constraints can be obtained by
post-processing only the target source, or the target source together with a
small number of nuisance sources. We only need the overlaps of
Eq. (
74
) between the beyond-vacuum direction of the target and
the templates at the posterior samples of the vacuum global fit. For a set of standard
beyond-vacuum (e.g. scalar-tensor theories, environmental effects), these could be computed during the first inference and
released alongside the catalogue, and for any other model they can be computed
from the released posterior samples, so that any user can compute the masking
of their own beyond-vacuum model without rerunning the global fit. It will be
possible to test this using forthcoming mock datasets. The LISA Distributed Data
Processing Centre (DDPC) has generated a 
Mojito
 dataset, which contains
the major LISA source classes together with non-stationary noise. This dataset
will be analysed with the global-fit pipelines currently under development by
the DDPC and the NASA Ground Segment, and the resulting posteriors will be
released. The 
Mojito

dataset contains no beyond-vacuum GR physics, so an analysis of such effects
falls precisely into our null case, and the quantities to compute are

ℛ
0
\mathcal{R}_{0}
 and the per-source rankings. Once the vacuum pipelines are
robust and their results available, our estimator can be applied to assess
whether post-processing is a viable strategy for the scientific interpretation
of the public data products, for which sources a down-scaled reanalysis is
required instead, and under what circumstances the only option is to redo the
entire global fit. Through this experience, and the analysis of future common
datasets, we will learn how to obtain robust constraints on beyond-vacuum GR
effects in a computationally efficient way from the real LISA data.






Although our toy model did not include systematic errors, the formalism accounts
for them. Waveform model error enters both strategies through the same effective
parameter 
𝜽
eff
