, is then a requirement not on the overall
accuracy of the waveform model but on its projection onto the beyond-vacuum
direction. This is an important topic to explore further, but will be sensitive to the assumed
waveform error model, and in particular its projection onto the beyond-vacuum-GR space, and so it
must be done carefully.






One of the main limitations of our method is the use of the linear-signal
approximation 
[
37
]
, which enters both in approximating the
vacuum manifold by its tangent plane and in characterising the first fit by a
point estimate at the maximum of the likelihood. Curvature, priors, and
subtraction at a posterior point estimate rather than at a maximum all degrade
the exact relation between the two strategies, and we already saw in
Sec. 
8.2
 that the ranking itself depends on the expansion point
once the effect is large enough. Sweeping the expansion point, or updating it
iteratively at the value of the effect recovered by each refit
accounts for this, and in the toy model both
recover the right sources even though the null-point ranking alone points to
the wrong group. It is also important to note that the final reanalysis itself
does not rely on the linear-signal approximation, which only enters the
selection of the sources. If the approximation failed to identify an important
nuisance source, this could be compensated by a more stringent threshold. In
addition, estimating the masking ratio from the posterior samples themselves,
as described in Sec. 
7.1
, should make it robust to some
deviations from the linear-signal approximation. This is one of the points that
can be tested using the DDPC common datasets.






Another aspect to explore more carefully with the common datasets is when and how
to average in post-processing, i.e. to average over samples from the first fit
instead of subtracting its best fit. We discussed this strategy in
Secs. 
5.4
 and 
6
, where we showed that it
reduces the significance of a detection by at most a factor of 
2
\sqrt{2}
 (
69
), while bringing the upper limits of non-detections
closer to those of the global fit (
71
). This comes at the
price of increased computational cost (one refit per posterior draw). However,
it may have the advantage of increased robustness against the modelling
approximations we were just discussing, and of better-calibrated uncertainties
on the vacuum parameters, whose means it leaves unchanged at linear order. Using
the common dataset analyses, it will be possible to compare the simple and
averaged strategies in a realistic setting, and hence identify the right
approach to take with real data.






Our toy model included only 20 nuisance sources in total, whereas the LISA data
will contain 
𝒪
⁡
(
10
4
)
\mathcal{O}(10^{4})
 individually resolvable Galactic binaries. We
expect that a small number of sources will still dominate the masking of any
given target, in which case the greedy ranking of Sec. 
7.3
 will
identify them for a refit. It could also be that no source dominates, but the
cumulative effect of many still leads to an appreciable masking, as the 16
crossing sources of our toy model illustrate in a mild form. This
would not go unnoticed, since the total masking estimated with
Eq. (
77
) includes all sources, and the greedy algorithm
would still terminate, but the selected set could become large, making the
down-scaled reanalysis expensive. This is another point that should be tested
using the DDPC common datasets.






Finally, we also assumed stationary Gaussian noise with a known spectral density,
whereas in practice the noise will have to be inferred jointly with the
sources. Formally, the noise parameters then act as one more nuisance block,
but we expect them to matter less for the masking, because they couple in a different way to the
residual. A biased nuisance source is subtracted, so its imprint

Δ
​
h
\Delta h
 is a waveform-like piece of the residual that the second step
projects onto the beyond-vacuum direction, linearly in 
𝜽
b
\bm{\theta}^{\mathrm{b}}
. Instead, a biased noise estimate cannot be
subtracted as it enters only through the weighting 
C
n
−
1
C_{n}^{-1}
 of the inner
product, and at linear-signal order the expected Fisher cross-terms between
waveform and noise-model parameters vanish at the true parameters, because
they are proportional to the mean of the residual. What the noise sector does
see is the power of the residual, including that of the imprint left by
the biased subtraction, and this enters only at second order in the residual. It increases the
inferred spectral density in the frequency band where the residual lives, which does not
displace 
𝜽
b
\bm{\theta}^{\mathrm{b}}
 along a degeneracy but down-weights that band, lowering the
significance of every source there, including that of the effect itself. If
the inflation were frequency independent, all Fisher matrices would scale by
the same factor and the masking ratio would be unchanged. For a residual localised in frequency, the ratios have to be recomputed with the inflated spectral density,
although the fractional inflation is set by the residual’s signal-to-noise
ratio per frequency bin, and is therefore small for effects that are not too strong. The population of unresolved sources below a certain threshold
can also be included in the analysis in the same way, either as an effective bias or as extra
confusion noise 
[
35
]
.
Again, this is one more aspect we can study in more detail in realistic mock datasets to ensure that by the time LISA flies, we have a robust inference strategy to do precision tests of General Relativity.






