Conclusion




Under Gaussian surrogates the three training ingredients documented for candidate-scoring models have exact consequences. Training on teacher targets leaves a risk floor equal to the teacher’s coefficient bias, so it improves on outcome supervision only below a crossover sample size. A fixed representation trades the representation loss 
B
A
B_{A}
 against the estimation variance; it also projects the teacher bias onto the retained directions and moves the unrepresented teacher signal into the variance. The optimal teacher weight depends on the sample size, the teacher distortion and the correlation of the target errors. In the probit model, temperature scaling corrects a teacher whose coefficient has the right direction, and a residual excess, given in closed form, remains when the direction is wrong. The regression formulas assume a Gaussian design; under a multivariate-
t
t
 design the simulated risk exceeds them by up to 
35
%
35\%
 at 
n
=
12
n=12
, and the discrepancy shrinks as 
n
n
 grows. On credit card complaints the direction of each result held outside these assumptions: a student trained on a teacher stayed at the teacher’s decision cost while a student trained on outcomes went past it, recalibration left a large zero-shot model worse than a logistic regression, Turing’s calibration check accepted a constant forecast and rejected the best model, and a model reading all candidates in one context departed from the coherence condition by 17.5 decibans.






For practitioners the results suggest five checks.




1.




Training target.
 A model trained on teacher or reference probabilities estimates the teacher’s conditional distribution. Its error against outcomes includes a teacher bias that more student data does not remove (Proposition 
3.2
) and that agreement with the teacher does not reveal (
21
).




2.




Evaluation.
 Proper scores should be computed on realized outcomes. The model should be compared with a conventional classifier fitted to the same outcomes and with one fitted to the same teacher probabilities, so that the effect of the architecture is separated from the effect of the training target.




3.




Pretrained representations.
 A fixed representation reduces estimation variance. It lowers the risk only while the signal it discards is below the threshold (
10
), and the threshold decreases as the sample grows.




4.




Calibration.
 Recalibration corrects the scale of a forecast. A calibrated forecast can retain the excess risk (
20
) of a wrong coefficient direction, and calibration diagnostics do not detect it.




5.




Coherence.
 The same event should receive the same probability under different candidate sets (
23
), which can be tested by posing the event under several sets.










A model that predicts which candidate an annotator, a teacher or a past policy would select has not been shown to select the action with the best outcome. Establishing that requires a model of outcomes given actions and, when actions change outcomes, causal assumptions.",cues
