Discussion




Conclusion.
 We introduced trajectory replay, which replaces sample-level generative replay with velocity-field regression along the historical model’s own trajectories. Its optimum is the density-weighted average of the two task velocities, generating the mixture of their probability paths, so the stability-plasticity coefficient sets what the update retains rather than only trading it off, and the objective recovers a weighted forward KL divergence up to time reweighting. A matching rule for the mean velocity carries this optimum to the flow-map level. Because replay stops at intermediate times, it halves the expected simulation cost and makes replay more robust to discretization error. Empirically, trajectory replay outperforms replay and distillation baselines on class-incremental image generation, stays robust under few-step replay, and extends perturbation models to incremental experimental measurements.






Limitations.
 Trajectory replay still involves partial simulation under a historical model, and its adaptive version, though favorable on image generation, requires a training run per candidate weight at every update. The mean velocity aids sampling but requires a Jacobian-vector product whose memory cost grows with the state, which restricted its use in our microscopy experiments. Partial replay limits how solver error enters each update, but not the approximation error of the historical model itself, which every update inherits and which may compound over long task sequences, echoing forgetting bounds for continually fine-tuned Large Language Models (LLMs), while our streams span at most five tasks. Our reliance on flow matching also leaves extension to autoregressive models open. Finally, future work will explore CL updates of more efficient distillation models.










