2610.26397v1,Bridged Self-Distillation for On-Policy Learning,"We propose 
B
ridged 
S
elf-
D
istillation
 (
BSD
) to turn the reasoning of an 
external teacher
 into guidance on the student’s own rollouts (Figure 
1
, bottom). The external teacher is the model we aim to distill from, and BSD requires only its reasoning trace.","8 
Conclusion




We introduced BSD, a unified framework for distilling from any teacher that provides reasoning traces.
BSD uses the external teacher’s reasoning trace as privileged context for a self-teacher, producing compatible token-level guidance in the student’s own token space without teacher probabilities or vocabulary mappings.
Across twelve teacher–student pairs spanning shared-tokenizer, cross-tokenizer, and closed API settings, BSD improves every base model and achieves the best or tied average performance among the distillation methods in eleven pairs.
Our analyses further show that BSD supports stable training, benefits from more complete reasoning, and focuses its guidance on uncertain tokens, establishing reasoning traces as a common and effective interface for on-policy distillation.








Limitations




We evaluate BSD with shared-tokenizer, cross-tokenizer, and closed API
teachers across twelve teacher–student pairs and analyze its token-level
guidance. However, we acknowledge three main limitations.






First, due to computational constraints, our experiments are limited to
students of up to 4B parameters from one model family and to English
competition mathematics. Future work could examine whether the gains of
BSD hold for larger students, other model families, and other domains.






Second, BSD relies on a verifier to set the direction of each update, so
it applies only to tasks whose answers can be checked automatically and
not to open-ended ones. Whether the agreement between the student and the
external teacher can serve as a verifier in such tasks remains to be
explored.






Finally, our analysis relates the token-level guidance to the gains of
BSD only through correlations across students. It characterizes where the
guidance concentrates but does not establish which tokens it helps, or
why, which we leave for future work.








Ethics Statement




This work fine-tunes publicly released language models on publicly available
mathematical reasoning datasets and evaluates them with automatic verifiers.
It involves no human subjects, no personally identifiable information, and no
newly collected data.
The method transfers reasoning capability that the
teacher models already expose through their public interfaces, so we do not
identify risks beyond those general to improving open-weight reasoning
models.






The closed API teacher is queried through its official API solely to
obtain responses for non-commercial research. The students trained with
these responses serve only to study distillation methods: we do not
deploy them, offer them as a service, or use them to develop models that
compete with the API provider. We release only code, and release no
model weights and no datasets, including the teacher’s reasoning traces.






Use of AI Assistants.

The authors used AI assistants for code development, the organization of
experiments and analyses, figure preparation, and manuscript drafting and
language editing. All reported experimental results and scientific
interpretations were reviewed by the authors, who take responsibility for
the final paper.








