2610.26990v1,Euclid Bypass end-to-end simulations for spectroscopic galaxy clustering performance assessment,"Stars (
type
=
1
\texttt{type}=1
 in the EL-COSMOS catalogue) were removed from our analysis. We expect that stars will be efficiently filtered out of the catalogue using photometric and morphological criteria, and this point will be studied on real data.","7 
Discussion




The bypass simulation framework developed for the 
Euclid
 spectroscopic redshift survey encompasses two complementary tools, 
FastSpec
 and 
Pypelid
, as well as calibrated prescriptions for the survey selection function, redshift errors, and redshift interloper populations. These tools were designed to reproduce the essential characteristics of the 
Euclid
 slitless spectroscopic measurements at a computational cost that is orders of magnitude lower than full pixel-level simulations, enabling their application to the large mock catalogue ensembles required for galaxy clustering analyses.






FastSpec
 simulations applied to EL-COSMOS enable us to assess the redshift quality statistics, purity, completeness, and interloper fractions, which characterise the EWS. We demonstrated that the baseline selection applied to simulated EL-COSMOS spectra yields a redshift purity of 74% and a sample completeness of 45%. The inclusion of a photometric colour classifier based on 
Euclid
 broadband photometry modestly improves these figures to 78% and 49%, respectively. These results are broadly consistent with expectations from pre-launch performance models and provide a quantitative foundation for systematic error budget assessments. The redshift error distribution obtained from 
FastSpec
 simulations is well described by a Gaussian core with width 
σ
z
=
9.4
×
10
−
4
\sigma_{z}=9.4\times 10^{-4}
, with extended non-Gaussian wings that fall between Gaussian and Lorentzian profiles. Importantly, we found that the redshift error does not show a significant trend with redshift over the range 
0.9
<
z
<
1.8
0.9<z<1.8
, and is not proportional to 
1
+
z
1+z
, a point of relevance for the modelling assumptions made in galaxy clustering analyses. The interloper population is dominated at low S/N by noise interlopers, while line interlopers from [O 
iii
] and [S 
iii
] misidentifications contribute at the level of a few to ten per cent, depending on redshift, with a characteristic redshift dependence that can be understood from the wavelength coverage of the red grism. The conditional redshift error model 
𝒫
⁡
(
z
meas
|
z
true
)
\mathcal{P}(z_{\mathrm{meas}}|z_{\mathrm{true}})
 calibrated on those simulations provides a complementary prescription that decouples angular from line-of-sight selection effects, and was applied to assign realistic redshift errors to mock catalogues.






Pypelid
 extends this framework to the full survey footprint. It operates on catalogues, accounting for the exact dither sequence, varying noise levels, and the angular selection function of the survey. The output includes detection probabilities and redshift measurements for input mock galaxy catalogues. The detection model, parameterised as a sigmoid function of the bypass S/N, was calibrated against 
FastSpec
 + SPE simulations and reproduces the detection rate for correct redshifts and interloper classes across the relevant redshift ranges. We further constructed a tabulated model, marginalised over galaxy properties, that maps 
H
​
α
\mathrm{H}\,\alpha
 flux to detection probability. This allowed the framework to be applied to mock catalogues that contain only 
H
​
α
\mathrm{H}\,\alpha
 flux information, as in halo occupation distribution mocks.






We performed a 
Pypelid
 simulation of the DR1 survey using the Flagship mock galaxy catalogue. The resulting galaxy density map reveals the complex spatial structure of the selection function, with number density modulations driven by the dither pattern of exposures, the zodiacal background, scattered starlight, and Milky Way extinction. These features demonstrate that a realistic simulation of the 
Euclid
 selection function must account for the full spatial complexity of the observing strategy. The impact of the selection function on 2-point clustering statistics was assessed by computing the monopole and quadrupole moments of the correlation function in four redshift bins over 
0.9
<
z
<
1.8
0.9<z<1.8
. We showed that a forward-modelled random catalogue produced by running 
Pypelid
 on a set of randomly placed mock galaxies recovers the monopole and quadrupole in excellent agreement with the ground truth across all redshift bins, accurately reproducing both the BAO feature and the RSD anisotropic signature. Looking forward, the approach can be applied to real data with a calibrated bypass detection model that accurately reproduces the survey selection function to robustly recover galaxy clustering statistics.






Several simplifying assumptions are embedded in the current framework that should be borne in mind when interpreting its outputs. 
FastSpec
 assumes a spatially uniform background noise and sensitivity across the focal plane, and does not model residual contamination from overlapping spectra. Contamination in the extracted spectra from spectral overlaps and artefacts including detector persistence and 0
th
-order images led to spurious detections in early 
Euclid
 data 
(
Euclid Collaboration: Le Brun et al., 2026
)
. A dedicated study of the impact of contamination from overlapping continuum sources was presented in 
Euclid Collaboration: Passalacqua et al. (2026)
.






As we approach Euclid DR1, an important next step is to validate the bypass model predictions against measurements on real data, updating the detection model calibration where necessary. Source injection simulations that add artificial galaxies to real data will provide essential input for calibration (Euclid Collaboration: Passalacqua et al., in prep.). The use of realistic simulations that account for artefacts in the data is particularly important for characterising the detection model of the EDS where the substantially increased exposure depth leads to a different S/N distribution and interloper composition.






Systematic uncertainties and artefacts present in the 
Euclid
 data can be accounted for by adding dependencies to the bypass detection model. For instance, the detection model could account for the number of overlaps with bright sources to assign a probability of a spurious detection from residual contamination. Fundamentally, the detection model calibration will depend on the features present in the training dataset, and whether they are simulated or derived from real observations. Due to the complexity of the measurement process, it will be natural to turn to machine-learning-based methods for generalisation of the detection model.






The bypass framework built on simplified but physically motivated instrument models provides an efficient and reliable route to characterising the 
Euclid
 spectroscopic selection function at the level of precision required for galaxy clustering science. The bypass spectroscopic simulation framework, together with the associated detection model and redshift error prescriptions, constitutes an integral component of the 
Euclid
 simulation infrastructure for systematic assessments 
(
Euclid Collaboration: Monaco et al., 2026
)
, and forms the basis for ongoing preparation of the cosmological analyses that are the primary science goal of the 
Euclid
 mission.






