2610.25809v1,Do We Need Learned Targets? Classical Gradients as Supervision for 2D Gaussian Initialization,"Abstract


Learning-based Gaussian image initializers can require costly supervision obtained by per-image optimization. We investigate whether the Pseudo-PPM targets used by Instant GaussianImage can be replaced by classical image gradients. Additional training images and target-generation settings therefore require further preprocessing, limiting the scalability of this approach. We investigate whether a classical image-gradient operator can provide effective initialization supervision.","5 
Conclusion




We studied whether 2D Gaussian initialization requires supervision obtained by per-image optimization. A Sobel gradient target supports both a training-free initializer and online supervision of a neural initializer, removing the offline Pseudo-PPM generation stage. Parallel grid-based sampling reduces discretization time by 
27
×
27\times
, and online target generation allows training on 10,000 LSDIR images without storing optimized targets. The resulting initializers are competitive with Instant-GI on Kodak and DIV2K and outperform it under domain shift, by up to 1.85 dB on Manga109 and at every reported budget on Kvasir-SEG, while the convergence advantage varies with the dataset and fitting budget.






Limitations and Future Work.

Image gradients are a proxy for useful spatial detail and can also respond to high-frequency noise. Flat regions require a coverage heuristic in the analytic track. Performance varies across datasets and iteration budgets, and the comparisons evaluate the complete initialization pipelines. Future work includes noise-robust targets and initialization of other explicit representations, including video, 3D Gaussian Splatting, and Radiant Foam 
[
9
]
