2610.25603v1,When Better Opportunities Backfire: Service Worker Responses to Parallel AI Deployment in Customer Acquisition,"Abstract 
 
As AI voice agents get deployed in high-volume contact centers, firms can route low-intent leads to AI and 
allocate high-intent leads to human workers. Drawing on sequential search theory, we posit that AI-induced 
task allocation reshapes work, enriching opportunities facing human workers but also raising their reservation 
value for continuing with the lead in hand, making them less willing to persist. Much of this work 
examines AI’s direct involvement in the focal task, decision, or assignment. Our empirical strategy is designed to test this prediction by matching position-varying changes in 
opportunity exposure to position-varying changes in worker behavior. In addition, we examine whether 
conversion, reallocation, and call duration exhibit a corresponding gradient.","7. Conclusion 
The promise of AI deployment in service operations is that automating low-value work allows human 
workers to concentrate on higher-value tasks. In our customer acquisition setting, AI absorbed the weakest 
leads and enriched the opportunity pool facing human agents, yet agents responded by releasing leads more 
readily and investing less effort, with no net performance gain. This pattern suggests that AI-enabled 
allocation can affect human performance indirectly by changing workers’ expectations about future 
opportunities and the outside options against which current tasks are evaluated. More broadly, AI does not 
merely change who performs a task; it can also change how humans value, prioritize, and engage with the 
tasks that remain. As a result, the performance consequences of AI deployment depend not only on the 
allocation of work, but also on the behavioral responses that allocation induces.",cues
