In this work, we identify over-reliance on instantaneous observations as a key limitation in model-based reinforcement learning, leading to latent representations that are weakly aligned with underlying dynamics. To address this, we introduce a stochastic gating mechanism based on a Beta–Bernoulli process that induces controlled partial observability during training, effectively serving as an information bottleneck. Empirical results on tasks from the DeepMind Control Suite show that training under partial observability improves planning performance and data efficiency. Performance remains stable across different spatial masking scales, and training under diverse observability conditions proves more effective than biasing toward either sparse or dense observations alone. These results indicate that exposure to a broad range of information regimes promotes more dynamics-aligned representations. Future work includes extending evaluation to more complex environments and integrating the approach with actor–critic methods to assess its impact on end-to-end policy learning.",cues
