2610.25285v1,A discrete-continuous-time stochastic framework for attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder-related symptom modeling and pharmacological intervention via methylphenidate,"Once the unmedicated ND baseline is established, we aim to find the exact intervention required to steer the precise trajectories toward the NT baseline.","10 
Conclusion




This paper successfully established and validated a novel neurocomputational framework that bridges the theoretical rigor of stochastic measure theory with the empirical demands of clinical psychiatry. By conceptualizing the neurobiological dysfunctions of ADHD as a system of continuous-time stochastic processes, this research moved beyond descriptive statistics, offering a predictive and mathematically optimized paradigm for pharmacological intervention.






The foundational achievement of this work lies in the development of the hybrid 1D-CNN and ViT architecture. Overcoming the limitations of early baseline models, the integration of a one-dimensional convolutional front-end provided the essential temporal inductive bias required to process high-dimensional fMRI sequences. Furthermore, the engineering of the latent chunking mechanism transformed a standard classification task into a precise parameter extraction engine. By enforcing the network to orthogonally encode the four core behavioral axes of ADHD, the spatial attention weights were successfully translated into discrete, clinically relevant scalar parameters.






These extracted parameters served as the critical bridge to the mathematical domain, directly parameterizing the SDEs governing each patient’s symptomatology. Modeling executive and motivational deficits as exponential decays and motor hyperactivity as an Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process provided a mathematical representation of the unmedicated ADHD baseline. This stochastic formalization accurately captured the inherent tendency of the ND connectome to converge towards stable, yet dysfunctional behavioral regimes under the influence of physiologic Brownian noise.






Ultimately, the OROS formulation of the ER MPH PDs as a continuous control policy represented the pinnacle of the framework. By solving the SOC problem, the mathematical optimizer successfully computed patient-specific dosages, being capable of counteracting the ND-specific dysfunctional drifts. Interpreted probabilistically as a change of measure, the control signal effectively guided the ND-specific trajectories towards NT targets. The validation of these numerical outcomes against the spatial attention maps confirmed that our mathematical optimization reliably mirrors the structural normalization of the fronto-striatal networks, establishing a computational proof of concept.










