2610.24954v1,"ceci n'est pas un tagger, can an LLM flavour-tag a jet?","In this work, we investigate whether the scientific knowledge acquired by an LLM during training can be used to infer the flavour of a jet directly from its reconstructed observables.","4 
Conclusion




In this work, we probe the ability of 
Opus 5.5
 to classify the flavour of jets, a task of great interest for LHC physics that the model has learned solely through the scientific literature processed during its training.
The model is asked to assign flavour probabilities to jets from a public ATLAS dataset, stripped of all truth information and with only reconstructed quantities retained.






Any outcome of the experiment would have been interesting a priori.
What we observe is that the model achieves non-trivial discrimination between flavours, well beyond random choice but definitely not able to compete with the most refined algorithms specifically trained for this task, which are capable of also resolving the subtle differences that distinguish 
b
b
-jets from 
c
c
-jets.






The model accompanies each prediction with a short natural language description of the jet.
Whether these notes are a genuine representation of its internal reasoning or a convenient justification of the prediction is difficult to establish.
Nevertheless, the physical features invoked are coherent with the jets’ truth flavours and reproduce the characteristics verbally associated with each category.
Indirectly, this simple natural-language tagging exercise offers a unique perspective on our scientific literature, suggesting that the less detailed and distinctive descriptions of 
c
c
-jets compared with those of 
b
b
-jets may be reflected in the model’s performance, much as an imbalanced training sample can influence the outcome of supervised learning.






It is clear that this experiment comes with a list of limitations and cannot be considered a novel approach to flavour tagging.
The probabilistic nature of LLMs is what primarily makes their use in any practical application still inconceivable: a trained algorithm such as 
GN2
 is a deterministic function of its inputs, whereas the same jet presented twice to an LLM may receive different predictions.
Moreover, although the observed performance is consistent across different sessions and prompt variations, the results remain difficult to disentangle from the choice of model, reasoning effort, and agentic harness.
In this sense, we have observed an event, which is significant in its own right. Further comparisons across different configurations could provide a broader understanding of the emergent behaviours observed in this study.
We therefore encourage readers to reproduce the experiment using the instructions provided in the public repository and to share their findings.






The computational demands and inference time of this approach are orders of magnitude larger than what would be acceptable in a practical application.
In addition, our results show that the model’s probability estimates exhibit limited granularity and span a narrower range of values than those of the reference algorithms.
The model in fact tends to output human-rounded probability values, preferring for example 0.800 to 0.823 even when explicitly instructed to provide numerical outputs at a fixed precision.
This is a clear limitation coming from how the model training is optimized for textual data and not for numerical values.
An interesting avenue for addressing these limitations could be offered by newer models, adopting a decision-based approach producing granular probabilities rather than textual tokens. Whether such models can provide better discrimination for flavour tagging remains an open question to be assessed.






While the integration of LLMs into analysis workflows is already underway, the results presented in this work suggest that they may also be used directly to support quantitative inference on physical data.
Exploiting the representation of fundamental physics concepts encoded in these models to guide classically trained algorithms could provide a framework for a new generation of hybrid solutions that combine the best of both worlds.
In this perspective, the initial epoch of training would no longer necessarily correspond to a state of complete ignorance but can rely on a shared base optimized for the specific experiment, field or task.
Whether this will shape the future of conventional classification algorithms remains to be seen.
What seems nowadays clear is that LLMs will become the standard interface to scientific knowledge, with great potential to accelerate discovery.






Ceci n’est pas un tagger. What this study reveals is not a competitive new flavour-tagging algorithm, but a reflection of the scientific knowledge accumulated through decades of work by the flavour-tagging community.",cues
