2610.24788v1,Information Theory of Random Composition Trees,"Motivated by these findings, we set out to mathematically analyze the entropy of random composition trees and present three main results in this paper.","7 
Discussion




The random composition tree model is a statistical ensemble of trees that correspond to how texts are recursively split into coherent chunks, forming a hierarchical semantic structure.
In this paper, we analyzed key information-theoretic properties of this stochastic splitting model.
We showed that the entropy of the trees grows linearly with the root size, and derived an analytical expression for the entropy rate as a function of the branching factor 
k
k
.
We showed that large trees exhibit typicality, i.e., the information rate converges in probability to the entropy rate.
Moreover, we analyzed the subextensive part of the entropy, and showed that its behavior is qualitatively different from that of natural language when modeled as a stationary stochastic process.






We also showed that the entropy rate is remarkably sensitive to ’microscopic’ modifications - model changes that significantly affect small chunks but have a negligible impact on large ones.
We analyzed two model variants: the resplitting variant, which allows a child of the same size as its parent to split further, and the ’unpositioned zeros’ variant, which treats splittings differing only in the position of empty chunks as equivalent.
The original model and the resplitting variant maintain a monotonically increasing entropy rate as the branching factor 
k
k
 increases, matching the empirical trend observed in the entropy rates of texts with different branching factors 
[
14
]
.
In contrast, the unpositioned zeros variant peaks at 
k
=
4
k=4
 and decreases thereafter, demonstrating that this monotonic increasing trend is not a necessary consequence of recursive splitting.






The random composition tree model exhibits correspondence with two important informational properties of language when modeled as a stochastic process.
In the case of language as a stationary process, the entropy grows asymptotically linearly with the length of the text, and in the case of an ergodic process, the information rate converges to the entropy rate, i.e., exhibits typicality 
[
3
]
.
The random composition tree model shares these two properties despite being fundamentally different in nature.
In contrast to a discrete stochastic process that is defined as a sequence of random variables, each defined over a fixed set of possible outcomes (like an alphabet of words), our model is defined over a set of tree realizations that is distinct for every root size and expands combinatorially as the root size grows.
While the random composition tree model is stripped of any semantic or syntactic content, it may serve as a useful starting point for a model of language that incorporates the hierarchical semantic structure of texts, without the need to assume stationarity or ergodicity.








Acknowledgments




W.Z. acknowledges funding from NSF ACCESS Discover Grant CIS240836, and support from the Simons foundation and the Eric and Wendy Schmidt Membership in Biology at the Insitute for Advanced Study. M.K. is supported in part by a grant from Fran Morris Rosman and Richard Rosman. M.T. is supported by the Simons Foundation, MBZUAI-WIS Joint Program for Artificial Intelligence Research and Foundation Adelis.",cues
