Conclusions




In this paper, we have derived the leading short-time asymptotics of the ATM implied volatility skew and curvature in a model combining general stochastic volatility with a stable-like Lévy process. The result allows us to identify the mechanism generating the power law in the skew and curvature by analysing the relationship between the leading powers. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first result of this type covering both Markovian and rough volatility specifications together with such a general Lévy measure.






The proofs of the main results are obtained by a flexible methodology based on Malliavin calculus. Moreover, through an approximation argument, we extend the result to the rough Heston model despite the failure of some of the regularity assumptions of the general theorem. The numerical experiments support the theoretical asymptotics. We believe that this methodology can be further developed to obtain higher-order expansions and to study other short-time properties of the implied volatility surface.








