Conclusion




We investigated whether pretrained LLMs can identify L1 backgrounds by
scoring learner texts under prompts that specify candidate L1s. Across
three datasets, two model sizes, and all evaluated input lengths,
L1-prefix probability scoring consistently outperformed direct
classification. The results show that even the 8B model can perform
strongly with this approach, while the 70B model achieved full-text
accuracies of 96.0% on TOEFL9, 92.0% on Write & Improve 2024, and
84.1% on BULATS-L1. These results exceed the previously reported
scores in the evaluation settings considered. Results on the two recently
released datasets also suggest that the findings are not limited to
TOEFL9. Future work should investigate the linguistic and token-level
signals that drive these scores, including how candidate-L1 prefixes
change the probabilities assigned to particular parts of an essay.
Such analyses could help explain what information the models use to
distinguish L1 backgrounds. Overall, prefix-conditioned token
probabilities provide a promising approach to NLI and a useful
complement to direct LLM-based classification.








Limitations




Dataset representativeness and low-resource scenarios.




Our results are limited to English L2 texts and the L1 backgrounds
included in the three benchmarks. It remains to be seen whether the
gains from L1-prefix-probability scoring hold for other L2 languages
and for L1 backgrounds not represented in these benchmarks, especially
those that may be underrepresented in the models’ pretraining data.








Computational cost.




Prefix scoring requires evaluating the essay under each candidate-L1
prefix, whereas direct classification reads the essay once and
generates a short label. The essay-token probabilities are scored
without autoregressively generating the essay, and candidate scores
can be batched. Prefix scoring therefore involves more
candidate-conditioned computation, but this does not necessarily
translate into proportionally higher inference latency. We did not
measure runtime or resource use, so a direct efficiency comparison
remains for future work.










Ethical Considerations




Our research uses publicly available L2 English learner corpora,
including the pseudonymized Write & Improve corpus

(
Nicholls et al., 2024
)
 and TOEFL11

(
Blanchard et al., 2013
)
. TOEFL11 contains no direct personal
identifiers. We also use speech transcripts from BULATS-L1

(
Uluslu et al., 2026b
)
, which was anonymized and made available
for this study but is not publicly released. We acknowledge the broad societal implications of authorship analysis, including potential risks to the security and privacy of individuals 
Saxena et al. (2025)
. This work is intended to contribute to
educational research on L2 learner language and research on the transparency and privacy implications of automated text analysis. As detailed in our Limitations (Section 
Limitations
), we also recognize that our efforts to mitigate bias are not exhaustive, and further research is needed.








Acknowledgments




This work was supported by Innosuisse innovation project 103.188 IP-ICT (AUCH). The project also received support from Cambridge University Press & Assessment, a department of The Chancellor, Masters, and Scholars of the University of Cambridge.",cues
