SEP
 is equivalent to POVM universality outright (see Figure 
1
), so it can be seen as a measurement postulate in disguise.






Similar commitments can also enter more subtly through the assumptions used to derive formalisms that relax NRQM’s idealizations. For instance, the description of open quantum systems by the Lindblad master equation is often presented as a generalization of Schrödinger dynamics that removes the idealized assumption that the system under consideration is closed 
[
93
]
. Deriving this master equation, however, typically relies on tracing out environmental degrees of freedom 
[
94
]
. Again, if one accepts NRQM’s theory of observables, this is justifiable under appropriate conditions, because taking the partial trace does not destroy any “observable information.” Promoting this use of the partial trace to an axiom yields something like Postulate 
PT
, which, together with Postulates 
EQC
 and 
IP
, is strong enough to derive all of the foreground postulates other than Postulate 
PM
 (see Section 
4.5
). So, while the open-systems approach removes the idealized assumption of perfect isolation, it also builds contested aspects of the measurement postulates into the formalism.






Both views described above are reasonable reactions to the strong interrelationships shown in Figure 
1
. What one theorist sees as rigidity, another may see as brittleness. Some or all of these postulates may well be correct. But whichever assumptions we decide to take as foundational, we should be ready to defend them on their own terms.








5.2 
The parallelogram law as a Rorschach test




Empirical questions related to the postulates in Figure 
1
 can seem either exciting or mundane, depending on how one looks at them. Consider the arrival-time problem discussed in Section 
2.3
. On its face, this is an unremarkable scientific question about how to best describe certain empirical data. Some proposed models predict arrival-time distributions that are not given by any POVM. If these models turned out to provide the best fit to the data, would that really be a paradigm-shattering discovery? To theorists who treat the postulates in Figure 
1
 as a unified package, the suggestion of non-POVM statistics can seem to call into question “quantum mechanics itself,” or “causality itself,” or some other principle that only a radical skeptic could doubt. But the stakes need not be so dramatic. Non-POVM statistics seem to threaten principles like causality only when we bundle together assumptions from different physical theories and insist that each holds exactly and universally.






Postulate 
PL
 offers a way to make such commitments explicit. Its simplest special case, Postulate 
