. Note that the four procedures can be carried out independently of one another, perhaps on different days, or even in different laboratories. Yet their outcome probabilities are supposed to satisfy this equality 
exactly
. This is a strikingly precise and general family of empirically falsifiable claims.






This family of claims cannot simply be dismissed as interpretation-dependent. The outcome probabilities it relates are precisely those to which the Born rule applies, so a theorist who accepts that the Born rule makes testable predictions has everything needed to unambiguously interpret Postulate 
PL
. And since the Born rule, or its POVM generalization, is typically taken to hold exactly, a theorist who takes it this way is thereby committed to Postulate 
PL
 holding exactly as well.






Two attitudes toward Postulate 
PL
 are available, and both are respectable. The first is to see Postulate 
PL
 as an empirical regularity that we expect to hold to a good approximation in a certain regime, but that future theories may modify. This attitude is familiar enough to need no elaboration. The second is to see Postulate 
PL
 as an exact, universal law. The tight-package reading of Figure 
1
 can make this attitude seem natural, since Postulate 
PL
 is logically equivalent to many other standard postulates. This position nevertheless needs more explanation, since promoting an empirical regularity to the status of a physical law is a much more consequential decision than saying that a certain regularity holds, to use Bell’s terminology, “for all practical purposes” (FAPP) 
[
56
]
.






One way to argue that Postulate 
PL
 deserves law-like status would be to show that NRQM (including its measurement postulates) can be recovered as an effective limit of a deeper theory in which some sort of gauge equivalence underwrites Equation (
4
). This deeper theory would, perhaps, reveal that the two sides of this equation are different mathematical representations of the same physics. Relativistic principles might seem to supply such an equivalence. One might note, for instance, that when Alice and Bob are spacelike separated, different reference frames disagree about whether Alice’s measurement occurs first. So, simply insisting that every inertial frame tell a consistent story in which causes precede their effects seems to force Bob’s outcome statistics to be the same regardless of Alice’s choice. In the case of the bunker game, however, Bob can carry out his entire experiment inside the future light cone of Alice’s actions, in which case every frame agrees that Alice measures first. The bunker game may well be unwinnable, but if so, relativistic symmetries alone cannot explain why.






Whether Postulate 
PL
 and its logically equivalent single-system relatives are exact laws of nature or merely good approximations in certain regimes should, in my view, be considered an open question. It is, in the end, an empirical question, and theorizing alone cannot settle it. What theoretical results like those in this paper can do is help us think through our commitments before the data come in. So take a moment to ask yourself the following. Do you believe that Postulate 
PL
 holds 
exactly
? More precisely, to the extent the relevant states can be prepared and systematic errors can be eliminated, do you believe that any instance of Postulate 
PL
 will be found to hold to any desired degree of precision? Suppose experimental data showed that an instance of Equation (
4
) held to ten decimal places, but not twelve. Would that fundamentally challenge your understanding of physics?










