, in which the environment retains a memory of past interactions that influence the system’s future evolution. Here, we aim to explore these features by explicitly employing quantum process matrices to characterize quantum multi-time states.","Conclusions 




In this work, we address two major gaps in the theory of multi-time states: the lack of an axiomatic characterization of multi-time states corresponding to non-Markovian dynamics, and the absence of a general framework for spatio-temporal states beyond quantum theory itself. To bridge these gaps, we provide a set of operational principles formulated within the broader framework of operational probabilistic theories (OPTs) 
[
30
, 
31
, 
40
]
 with finite-dimensional convex state spaces that satisfy causality (i.e., a unique discarding effect) 
[
31
, 
30
]
 and local tomography 
[
60
, 
62
, 
30
, 
18
, 
40
, 
33
]
. Specifically, we have introduced three operationally motivated axioms—compositionality, reconstructibility, and spatio-temporal marginal consistency—and have shown that these axioms uniquely determine the structure of multi-time states in both Markovian and non-Markovian settings. Our results establish a connection between multi-time states and the well-established framework of multi-time processes (such as quantum combs, process tensors, process matrices in quantum theory), which provide a complete description of open system dynamics, including both Markovian and non-Markovian evolutions 
[
97
]
.






For Markovian dynamics, our structure theorem implies that a Markov compatible multi-time state is obtained by inserting broadcasting maps into the intervention slots of a Markov multi-time process: the initial physical state undergoes a broadcasting map, one output evolves through a local channel, whose output is then fed into the next broadcasting map, and the structure repeats sequentially. Given this structure, characterizing Markov compatible multi-time states reduces to identifying the admissible broadcasting maps, which are typically fixed by additional operational axioms 
[
89
]
. This establishes broadcasting maps as the fundamental building blocks of the structure of Markov compatible multi-time states, consistent with a previously obtained result in quantum theory 
[
75
, 
74
]
. Our contribution is to show that this structure holds more generally within the framework of operational probabilistic theories, without relying on any specific features of quantum theory.






Our axiomatic framework extends naturally beyond the Markovian regime. By imposing an additional axiom of spatio-temporal marginal consistency, we establish a structure theorem for general multi-time states, including those compatible with non-Markovian dynamics. Operationally, both Markovian and non-Markovian dynamics are instances of general multi-time processes, in which a joint initial system-environment state evolves through a sequence of system-environment interactions, followed by discarding the inaccessible environmental degrees of freedom at the final time step, and an intervention slot on the system between successive interactions. We show that a multi-time state compatible with such a general process can be constructed by inserting suitable broadcasting maps at each intervention slot of the underlying multi-time process, provided the multi-time state satisfies spatio-temporal marginal consistency together with compositionality and reconstructibility. These structure theorems establish, for the first time, an explicit axiomatic characterization of multi-time states arising from arbitrary system-environment dynamics. Furthermore, as in the Markovian case, our axioms are not restricted to quantum theory but apply to the broader class of operational probabilistic theories. This has foundational implications: it provides a concrete structure for multi-time states and potentially their associated Bayesian inference rules across a wide range of physical theories. Such a framework can help clarify which features of Bayesian calculus are fundamental and arise from theory-independent operational principles.






In addition to its foundational implications, our axiomatic framework also leads to several concrete applications. One of our results establishes a fundamental connection between the properties of a spatio-temporal state—arising from a joint initial physical state and a subsequent bipartite channel—and signaling through that channel. Specifically, we show that for any spatio-temporal state satisfying reconstructibility, compositionality, and spatio-temporal marginal consistency, if the reduced spatio-temporal state, obtained by discarding the relevant intermediate systems, is not a physical state of the theory, then this provides a sufficient condition for certifying signaling through the bipartite channel. In quantum theory, for Hermitian spatio-temporal states, this property translates to negativity of a particular marginal spatio-temporal state, a special case of which was investigated in the pseudo-density matrix framework 
[
82
]
. In our work, we demonstrate that this connection is not a peculiar feature of quantum theory or any particular formalism for spatio-temporal states, but rather emerges directly from the operational principles underlying our framework. Furthermore, we also show that this property can be extended to multi-time processes and used to certify memory in dynamics. This observation could lead to experimental tests to detect memory in a non-Markovian process, analogous to tests used in causal inference 
[
77
]
.






We also show that the correspondence between multi-time states and processes naturally leads to an application in causal discovery. Finding a causal model from observed correlations is a central goal in the causality community 
[
93
, 
122
, 
51
]
. Since the framework of multi-time states generalizes the notion of classical joint probability distributions in time, it is natural to ask: given a multi-time state, which underlying causal model, captured by a multi-time process 
[
7
, 
38
, 
17
]
, could explain it? In general, the inferred causal model need not be unique. As shown in Ref. 
[
109
]
, certain multi-time states can be compatible with both a bipartite state and a Markov compatible two-time state. Similarly, we have shown that the same multi-time state can be compatible with both Markovian and non-Markovian dynamics. This ambiguity is not universal, however, due to the inherent asymmetry between spacelike and timelike correlations in nonclassical theories. The goal of a causal discovery algorithm is therefore to rule out incompatible causal models, rather than to identify a unique one. We demonstrate a proof-of-principle approach to this question in the quantum case. Specifically, given a multi-time state, we ask whether there exists a multi-time process with a particular causal order of interest that could explain it. We show that this question can be addressed via a semidefinite program (SDP). Our structure theorems provide the foundation for this formulation, offering a systematic framework for quantum causal discovery from multi-time states.






Finally, to justify that our framework indeed extends beyond quantum theory, we develop, for the first time, a complete framework of multi-time states and Bayesian retrodiction in the square-bit boxworld. As a first step, we characterize the broadcasting map there using operational principles analogous to those in Ref. 
[
89
]
, which had been left unspecified in the general structure theorems. Since the square-bit boxworld satisfies the assumptions of our OPT framework, the structure theorems apply directly whenever a multi-time state satisfies reconstructibility, compositionality, and spatio-temporal marginal consistency. Moreover, the unique broadcasting map we identify yields a unique multi-time state in this theory, and all the applications discussed above extend straightforwardly to boxworld. This example concretely establishes our axiomatic framework in a Hilbert-space-free setting, without relying on the tools of quantum theory.






Our results raise several intriguing questions and open up new directions for future research, which we discuss in the following. Although we found a unique structure for multi-time states in a broad class of OPTs, the broadcasting map itself was left unspecified. In quantum theory and boxworld, however, we have concrete examples of unique broadcasting maps, both derived from similar operational axioms 
[
89
]
. This naturally raises the question: are these axioms sufficiently universal to single out a broadcasting map in any OPT? Several mathematical tools are already available to explore this question. It is known that reversible transformations in any finite-dimensional OPT with closed-convex state space form a compact Lie group 
[
50
]
, and hence admit an irreducible decomposition. This means that maps satisfying covariance conditions under reversible transformations can, in principle, be characterized. The remaining question is whether classical consistency and permutation invariance are sufficient to force a unique solution. A positive answer could lead to a unique state over time for any OPT satisfying our assumptions.






Another open question is whether our framework can be extended beyond locally tomographic OPTs. In principle, one could attempt to define multi-time states in some of these theories, such as fermionic theories, which are simply a superselection of quantum theory, by projecting quantum multi-time states onto the relevant subspaces. However, it would be more satisfying to characterize multi-time states directly within the OPT framework, without relying on an embedding into quantum theory. This remains an interesting direction for future work.






One of the main motivations for the quantum state-over-time formalism was to develop a rigorous, non-commutative Bayesian calculus within quantum theory. This effort is partly inspired by the success of Bayesian methods in classical settings, with applications in signal processing, machine learning, and state estimation 
[
102
, 
21
]
. In quantum theory, similar ideas have led to Bayesian phase estimation 
[
119
]
, quantum state smoothing 
[
113
, 
57
, 
79
, 
78
]
, and quantum belief propagation 
[
64
]
, to name a few. A systematic exploration of these applications through the unified lens of spatio-temporal states could provide a rigorous, device-independent, and theory-independent foundation for existing protocols, and may also uncover new Bayesian tools that have no classical analog, similar to Ref. 
[
80
]
.






Another interesting direction is to distinguish two types of multi-time states: those arising from a probabilistic mixture of Markovian processes (a class of classical memory processes), and those with genuine quantum memory. In the multi-time process framework, considerable progress has been made in distinguishing these 
[
52
, 
112
, 
55
]
. A natural question is whether this distinction is also visible at the level of multi-time states. One possible approach is to adapt the concept of squashed non-Markovianity 
[
48
]
, originally developed for tripartite physical states and later extended to dynamical settings 
[
23
]
. This could provide a quantitative way to distinguish different types of non-Markovianity in multi-time states.






Finally, an interesting endeavor would be to go beyond multi-time processes and consider more exotic scenarios, such as processes with indefinite causal order 
[
87
, 
28
]
 or indefinite input-output directions 
[
32
]
. In the pseudo-density matrix (PDM) framework, some initial steps have been taken to connect PDMs with indefinite causal order, showing that PDM negativity can be activated in such scenarios 
[
81
]
. An axiomatic framework could provide a deeper understanding of this phenomenon and potentially generalize such negativity activation across different physical theories.






