Conclusion




We investigated whether asymmetries in graph Dirichlet energy can
reveal the causal direction between two variables. To this end, we
proposed and analysed GDECI, a new fitting-free criterion for bivariate causal
inference that compares the graph Dirichlet energy of each variable,
viewed as a signal on a kernel similarity graph built from the other,
using the unnormalized graph Laplacian. For rank-transformed variables,
the use of the unnormalized Laplacian ensures that its decisions
are invariant under strictly monotone transformations of either
variable, which is not true for the normalized Laplacians.
In population form, GDECI criterion decomposes into a degree-weighted Bayes squared
prediction error and a non-negative smoothness term, both normalized
by the edge mass. As the bandwidth tends to zero, the weights become
uniform and the smoothness term vanishes, recovering a RECI-like
conditional-variance asymmetry in copula space. At positive bandwidth,
the degree-weighted conditional variances and the smoothness terms
of the two directions generally differ, so GDECI can reach a different
decision than a conditional-variance comparison.






Theory.
 For non-injective mechanisms, we give sufficient conditions under which GDECI selects the causal direction. For additive-noise and heteroscedastic models with bounded supports, in which the cause and noise densities are positive up to the endpoints and
the slope of the effect in the cause is positive, the smoothness term
is of smaller order in the causal direction at sufficiently small bandwidth. Quantitatively, it is at
most of order 
σ
2
\sigma^{2}
