2610.21819v1,Legitimate Reconstruction Advantage in Wiretapped Semantics-Aware Remote Monitoring,"Abstract


This paper investigates joint accuracy and security in semantics-aware remote monitoring, where source updates intended for a legitimate receiver may also be intercepted by an eavesdropper.","Conclusion




This work developed an analytical framework for legitimate reconstruction advantage in semantics-aware remote monitoring under eavesdropping. The proposed LRA metrics characterize the probability and persistence of correct reconstruction at the intended receiver coupled with incorrect reconstruction at the eavesdropper. A joint stationary analysis yielded closed-form expressions for general finite-state ergodic Markov sources, with explicit algebraic specializations enabling efficient transmission-probability optimization. The results show that achieving legitimate advantage more frequently and sustaining it can favor different source dynamics and transmission policies. For two-state sources, rare-transmission analysis reveals that greater transition asymmetry can increase persistence while reducing probability when the sum of transition probabilities is fixed. Moreover, optimizing receiver-wise objectives can select policies that are suboptimal for LRA, and improvements in legitimate reconstruction accuracy need not increase the probability of legitimate advantage. These findings emphasize that transmission decisions should account for both receivers’ reconstruction outcomes and the temporal requirements of the monitoring application. The geofencing example further translates prescribed LRA requirements into location-dependent spatial boundaries.
These conclusions hold under randomized stationary transmission and prescribed ZOH reconstruction at both receivers. An important direction for future work is to characterize how source-aware adversarial inference changes the preferred transmission policies and the resulting spatial boundaries.",cues
