V.1 
Column density distribution and observed relationships




As presented in Section 
III
, the N
2
H
+
–HCN relationship (Figure 
2
a) is super-linear using the S/N weighted fit. The super-linearity becomes more pronounced when we fit them with two slopes. This is consistent with previous findings in M51, where the overall fit without weights shows a linear trend, while the fit among data points with high HCN luminosities becomes super-linear 
[
74
]
. The radiative transfer models using both log-normal and log-normal+power-law PDFs described in Section 
IV
 can reproduce slightly super-linear trends that match the observations well. This result suggests that molecular clouds in M83 can be represented by combinations of Perseus-like clouds with different mean column densities.






Meanwhile, our individual models did not reproduce the break in the power law. Observed data have steeper slopes at higher HCN luminosity, while our models have only a slight change in slopes, and this subtle difference likely results from variations among different molecular clouds. However, if we combine models with low and high 
σ
\sigma
 with and without the power law, it should be possible to generate the break in the power law. Furthermore, in intensity modeling, we adopted the fitting results from 
Tafalla et al. [77]
, where molecular line intensities are expressed as functions of column density. However, in reality, additional environmental factors can significantly influence molecular line intensities. While Perseus is a low-mass star-forming region, our field of view toward M83 likely also contains high-mass star-forming regions. The high-mass star-forming region Orion B has about a factor of a few higher HCN intensities than Perseus does 
[
85
]
. Orion B also emits N
2
H
+
 more preferentially from higher column-density regions (i.e., lower luminosity than Perseus at lower column-density regions, higher luminosity at higher column-density regions). This difference will shift the model to the right side of the plot in Figure 
6
(a) with more super-linearity. It seems likely that we can explain the spread of the observed data points in M83 easily with the mixture of low- and high-mass star-forming regions.






There are some possible causes of differences between low-mass and high-mass star-forming regions. High-mass star-forming regions tend to have a stronger UV radiation field, and possibly higher cosmic-ray ionization rates. Kinetic temperatures are also higher in high-mass star-forming regions. A stronger UV radiation field can enhance HCN emission 
[
71
, 
66
]
, while elevated cosmic-ray ionization rates can increase the abundance of N
2
H
+
. In contrast, higher dust temperatures can suppress N
2
H
+
