VI 
Summary




In this paper, we presented 48-pc resolution observations of the dense gas tracers N
2
H
+
 and HCN, and compared them with the 1-mm and 3-mm continua and archival CO data in the disk region of the spiral galaxy M83. We have also compared the observational results with radiative transfer modeling, considering the column density probability distribution function within the observational beam. Below is a summary of our main findings.








•




The observed relationship of N
2
H
+
 intensity as a function of the HCN intensity is super-linear with the S/N weighted fit, which is more pronounced in the two-slope model (Figure 
2
a). This trend is consistent with previous findings in M51, where a largely linear relationship transitions to super-linear behavior at the high-luminosity end. A similar super-linear trend is observed in the N
2
H
+
–1 mm (dust continuum) relationship (Figure 
2
b), which closely resembles that of N
2
H
+
–HCN.




•




The observed trend can be well reproduced by radiative transfer models that incorporate column-density-dependent line intensities from Galactic observations and the column density probability distribution function (Figure 
6
). This result implies that extragalactic clouds in the observed regions of M83 can be expressed as combinations of Galactic clouds. The log-normal-only model provides a steeper super-linear N
2
H
+
-HCN relationship than the log-normal+power-law model.




•




The radiative transfer modeling demonstrates that N
2
H
+
 emission is most sensitive to the presence of a power-law tail, whereas the intensities of other transitions are less affected (Figure 
8
). However, the difference caused by the slope of the power-law is still insignificant when the peak of the column-density log-normal distribution is high (
N
peak
≳
10
21.5
N_{\mathrm{peak}}\gtrsim 10^{21.5}\,
cm
-2
).




•




The HCN–1 mm (dust) relationship resembles the N
2
H
+
–HCN relationship but exhibits less pronounced super-linearity (Figure 
2
d). This near-linear trend is well reproduced by the radiative transfer model. The HCN intensities vary only modestly (by 
≲
0.5
\lesssim 0.5\,
dex) with the addition of a power-law tail, provided that 
N
peak
≥
10
21
N_{\mathrm{peak}}\geq 10^{21}\,
cm
-2
.




•




Relationships between CO intensities and those of HCN, N
2
H
+
, and the 1 mm continuum show weaker correlations compared to the relationships among HCN, N
2
H
+
, and the 1 mm continuum (Figure 
3
). This likely reflects the fact that CO traces less dense gas and becomes optically thick even at moderate column densities. This trend is consistent with the results from the radiative transfer modeling.




•




The 3 mm continuum, which traces the star formation rate, also shows weaker correlations with other quantities. This can be explained by the breakdown of the Kennicutt-Schmidt law on spatial scales smaller than 100 pc, as previously observed in Local Group galaxies.




•




The archival data toward the center of M83 indicate that N
2
H
+
 is not a reliable tracer of dense gas in galactic center regions if the dust temperature is high (Figure 
9
).








