2610.20950v1,Congestion Price Uncertainty From Interconnection Queue by Monte Carlo Sampling,"The impact of interconnection uncertainty is starting to be explored in capacity expansion planning 
[
14
]
, but most attention remains on analyzing the long lead times. In particular, interconnection uncertainty has not been incorporated into studies on medium-term price forecasts (a few years), although we expect it to be of interest to developers of new projects and to lenders for financial risk assessment.","Conclusion




We have shown that Monte Carlo sampling yields useful congestion price distributions to represent interconnection queue uncertainty in a realistically sized system of 2000 buses.
We compared it against standard practice of selecting one or a small number of likely configurations and showed that sampling at the same expense performs at least as well, with the important benefit of quantifying errors.
We evaluated both the mean and a threshold probability and found statistical error consistent with the central limit theorem.
The results show that Monte Carlo sampling is a viable approach to estimating congestion prices and is superior to estimates based on a few scenarios.
The primary limitation of this approach is the reliability of the distribution characterization, which requires sufficient data and continuity of policies and regulations that ensure future interconnections follow the same patterns.






The study focused on a queue of 20 generation projects, as well as demonstrating the effectiveness on the full queue of 195 projects.
In practice, a subset of projects of interest can be selected or the full set can be considered.
Even though a longer queue creates exponentially more possibilities to sample, the same level of accuracy is achieved.
An outcome of significant probability will show up in more configurations, and is just as likely to be sampled.
In contrast, using a top-
k
k
 approach, a high-probability outcome (such as zero congestion) can be spread out over many lower-probability configurations and be easily missed.






While production cost modeling encompasses a long time horizon, we restricted our focus to a single-snapshot, security-constrained DCOPF.
High-load snapshots can be selected from a full time series to implement this approach.
Future work will explore the computational feasibility of the sampling approach on multi-period PCM calculations.






These results may be relevant to data center developers as well.
With the rapid pace of construction and limitations imposed by the large-load interconnection queue, sites with low congestion or excess generation are beneficial.
Data centers also benefit from grid strength, which depends on the generation fleet.
Grid strength uncertainty can be estimated by the same sampling scheme, a topic for future work.",cues
