Conclusion




We studied training-free inference-time scaling for LLM reasoning, aiming to improve answer accuracy without updating model weights or relying on external reward models. Sequence-level power sampling faces both sampler-level and target-level challenges: finite-particle SMC can suffer from path collapse, while full-sequence sharpening can suppress alternative reasoning paths that support the same answer. We proposed 
DMS
, a two-stage marginal sharpening framework with separate sampler-level and target-level designs. At the sampler level, FAA-SMC combines annealed power targets, predictive resampling, and locally fully adapted proposals. At the target level, DMS sharpens the reasoning distribution, marginalizes answer probabilities over the resulting reasoning population, and then sharpens the induced answer marginal. Separate exponents control reasoning-path concentration and answer-level consensus, allowing answers to be weighted according to their aggregate support across reasoning paths. Experiments on MATH500, GPQA-Diamond, HumanEval, and AIME24/25 across three base models show that DMS achieves the highest mean pass@1 among the compared methods in all twelve settings, including comparisons with Power-SMC and Marginal Sharpening. Future work includes improving answer-marginal estimation, reducing particle degeneracy during reasoning, and developing adaptive choices of sharpening strengths and inference-time budgets.",cues
