In this thesis, we have outlined a thorough summary of inflationary cosmology and its merits, as well as the specific models of Starobinsky and 
R
2
R^{2}
 gravity. We have also introduced the fundamentals of quantum cosmology in the context of the Wheeler-DeWitt equation, as well as Hartle and Hawking’s no boundary proposal. The premise was to use these as a starting point to make meaningful quantum calculations for plausible cosmological models using the WDW equation, and to apply Hartle-Hawking’s methods to an inflationary model of modified gravity. The end goal is to potentially determine a quantum probabilistic argument for the emergence of the Universe with initial conditions which favour inflation.






Our first contribution was to develop a rigorous mathematical formalism from first principles to handle operator ordering ambiguities in the WDW equation for minisuperspace models. We did so by introducing ambiguity functions in the canonical quantization, while noting that they appear in the path integral due to the ambiguity of choosing a measure after field redefinitions. Using an argument from Hamiltonian hermiticity, we obtained some constraints for those functions. In turn, we used them to prove the uniqueness of the quantum theory to all orders for 1d and flat arbitrary-dimensional target space manifolds. Despite that this could not be generalized to any arbitrary curved manifold, we found that universality for such cases holds at the semi-classical level. The mathematical description corroborates past known results, but also serves as a correction to errors found in the literature.






For the second contribution, we used Hartle-Hawking’s method to calculate an analogous state of minimum excitation in the case of 
R
2
R^{2}
 gravity. We did so in two ways: one directly using the Jordan frame of the modified gravity action, and one using the Einstein frame transformation into a GR model with a cosmological constant and a massless scalar matter field 
ϕ
\phi
. We emphasized that the choice of contour of integration plays a significant role. The effective sign choice that comes from Hartle and Hawking’s choice inhibits the emergence of an inflationary Universe. The opposite choice by Linde-Vilenkin instead favours it. We made an argument for the better suitability of the latter in our model, but it is not conclusive.






We expanded upon the aforementioned result by examining excited states due to non-zero kinetic energy for the field 
ϕ
\phi
. We saw that these scenarios do not correspond to the emergence of a Universe out of nothing in some geometrical configuration. Rather, they describe various transitions from a Big Bang-Big Crunch type evolution to a deSitter-like one, or the inverse. We specifically made computations for both subcritical and critical/supercritical values of the kinetic energy. By making use of the formalism from our first work, we arrived at a formula with which we can calculate transition probability amplitudes.






In future works, a natural extension would be to to see if we can find a method to prove the uniqueness of quantum theory for any type of target space manifold beyond the semi-classical level. Furthermore, it is important to construct a more robust argument from first principles such that a choice between Hartle-Hawking and Linde-Vilenkin is made. If that’s impossible and both are acceptable, then we have to prove that explicitly and explain how the Universe would most likely emerge. Finally, it would be interesting to calculate the path integral for the state solutions in the case of 
R
2
R^{2}
 gravity exactly. That potentially involves finding the path integral-specific values for the WDW ambiguity functions in that model.










