Conclusion




In this work, we established the NP-hardness of the heterogeneous allocation problem and showed that the allocations produced by the greedy 
Section
 
2
 and DORG achieve expected payouts within multiplicative factors of the optimum. While the approximation guarantee for the greedy algorithm is tight, we believe that a more refined analysis could yield improved guarantees for DORG.






Our approximation guarantees provide a foundation for adapting centralized learning algorithms, such as CUCB and potentially the Cautious Greedy algorithm of 
Richard et al. (2024)
, to the heterogeneous asynchronous setting.






Extending these results to the decentralized setting remains a major open challenge. Without a central controller, communication is no longer free, and adapting communication protocols such as those introduced by 
Boursier and Perchet (2019)
 becomes more challenging in an asynchronous environment. Heterogeneous activation probabilities introduce an additional difficulty: players no longer play symmetric roles in the communication process. Efficient protocols must therefore balance rapid information exchange between frequently active players with the ability to conduct multiple exchanges in parallel. Addressing these challenges is an important direction for future work toward designing efficient learning policies for asynchronous multi-player bandits with heterogeneous players.








