Overall, optimizing on the entropic risk objectives yields remarkably lower infidelities than training on the average fidelity objective does, with an improvement as large as four orders of magnitude in the lowest infidelity reached.
One possible interpretation is that optimizing the shared ansatz parameters for different noise trajectories may lead to conflicting effects on their fidelities, preventing them from being simultaneously maximized. While the average fidelity would favor a compromise across noise trajectories, the entropic risk objectives may instead prioritize a subset of trajectories whose fidelities can be further increased at the expense of the others.
