6 
Conclusions & Future Directions




In this work, we introduced a risk-seeking optimization framework for noisy state preparation in bosonic quantum systems.
By optimizing trajectory-wise objectives through Monte Carlo sampling, our approach provides control over the distribution of pure-state fidelities.
Furthermore, our results show that risk-seeking optimization can reduce the lowest achievable pure-state infidelity by 
up to four orders of magnitude
 compared to standard optimization of the average fidelity in the considered experimental setting.
More importantly, we show that the proposed framework provides control over the trade-off between the pure-infidelity threshold 
ϵ
\epsilon
, the 
ϵ
\epsilon
-probability
and the success rate of the optimization process.
In particular, the optimization objective and its risk coefficient 
β
\beta
 allow the operating point of the state-preparation procedure to be tailored to the requirements of a specific application.
Future work should investigate whether the observed behaviors generalize to different target states, larger Fock-space dimensions, deeper SD-ansatzes and different noise models.
Finally, comparing the quantum states prepared by our framework with those obtained by optimizing the average fidelity may reveal which state properties are favored by trajectory-wise optimization, guiding the design of alternative risk-sensitive objectives.






