Conclusion




The current body of literature on surprisal theory applies mainly to questions of sentence processing during reading rather than spoken language comprehension. Though speech language models provide a means of applying a similar research program to spoken language comprehension, obtaining surprisal from the speech signal brings several added challenges: namely, the specific psychometric signal to model, the question of which units to consider given the naturally continuous nature of speech, and the complication of modeling many variations of the same abstract unit. We address the last of these with an importance sampling estimator that approximates the surprisal a speech LM assigns to abstract units.
Among the approaches to obtaining surprisal from a speech LM that were tested, we found that our estimations of abstract, lexical surprisals better modeled our signal of processing effort, pupil size, than realization-specific word probabilities. We also found that phone and frame surprisal from speech LMs improved models of pupil size.
These findings suggest that the surprisal of abstract lexical units is a key component of processing difficulty in spoken language comprehension. Our approach to estimating surprisal for abstract units opens the door to further study the contexts in which we base our predictions of upcoming input on sub-lexical units and specific realizations.








Limitations




Our experiments focus on a single listening study 
(
Winn, 2016
)
 with 162 items from the Revised Speech in Noise corpus 
(
Bilger et al., 1984
)
. We only analyze data from normal hearing listeners, but further examination would be needed to draw conclusions about whether the results hold for people with hearing loss, who have been shown to use context differently than normal hearing listeners 
(
Winn, 2016
)
. The nature of the stimuli limits the extent to which we can study sub-lexical phenomena and the impact of prosody and indexical information in the speech signal on processing effort during listening. In stimuli designed to study listening in unfamiliar stress patterns (e.g., accented stress patterns), the relative predictive power of marginalized and unmarginalized surprisals from a speech LM may differ.
Likewise, we could imagine the following stimuli in a listening study: “I reached into my toolbox and grabbed a hamburger.” The word 
hamburger
 is surprising in this context, but because the phonetic sequence /hæm-/ is expected (e.g., as the start of the word “hammer”), phone surprisal might also be predictive of pupil size. That being the case, without stimuli designed to capture sub-lexical effects, we likely do not fully characterize the role of finer-grained units’ surprisal during speech processing. We are also limited to read (rather than spontaneous) speech in English in this study.






Another limitation of the present study is the ASR sample rejection; though this step is necessary to exclude TTS errors, it is also a point at which errors can be made. Though a fuller error analysis is not possible because we do not have ground truth transcriptions for the sampled audio, we suspect that the most likely errors would be mis-transcriptions of valid realizations of a word. This may result in a bias against audio with especially atypical prosody or speaker features. We expect that exploring larger ASR models would decrease such errors, albeit at higher computational costs. We expect that better ASR models and a higher initial sample size 
N
N
 would also contribute to higher estimator coverage overall.






We also note that the present work only studies abstract units at the level of orthographic words and does not estimate marginalized phone surprisals. A direct application of our importance sampling approach to the phone granularity would yield estimates of the probabilities speech LMs assign to maximally context-sensitive phones in our stimuli across many speakers. However, extending our approach to study less context-sensitive units at the phone level (e.g., allophones or phonemes) would require extending the method to also sample the relevant units in diverse phonological contexts.








Ethical Concerns




We use only previously studied pupillometry and speech data with explicit permission from the author 
(
Winn, 2016
)
. We do not foresee any ethical concerns from the use of this data to study human speech processing.








