In this paper, the solar panel output is modeled under the assumption that panels do not consistently operate at their maximum rated capacity.","Conclusion




This paper presented an efficient method for resource adequacy assessment in renewable-rich power systems. The proposed framework models the probabilistic behavior of conventional and renewable power plants using their respective probability distribution functions, from which statistical moments and cumulants are derived. The Gram–Charlier expansion is then used to better represent the generation distribution, particularly in situations where the normal approximation becomes inaccurate due to the limited number of generation units and the high failure rates associated with renewable energy resources. Reliability indices, including LOLE and EENS, are evaluated using the stress–strength method. The proposed approach was validated using the modified IEEE one area RTS-96 and its multi-area configurations. The results demonstrate that the method can accurately estimate adequacy indices while maintaining significantly lower computational time compared with Monte Carlo simulation. In the studied scenarios, the calculated LOLE and EENS values showed strong agreement with Monte Carlo simulation results, while the conventional normal approximation produced substantially larger errors. These results underscore the importance of improved probabilistic representations for systems with high renewable penetration. The proposed method remains effective as renewable penetration level increase and scales well to larger systems, making it suitable for future power system studies and operational planning. Future work will focus on incorporating transmission and distribution constraints and exploring data-driven approaches that estimate generation distributions directly from empirical data to further improve modeling accuracy.",cues
