To summarize, the main results of this work are:
•The derivation of a Large Deviation Principle for the empirical measure of particles sub-
mitted to L´ evy noise. In the case of independent particles, this unveils a generalized
gradient flow structure.
•The derivation of the linear fluctuating hydrodynamics equation in this setting, with
explicit expressions for the long-range correlations of the noise.
•A numerical implementation of the linear fluctuating hydrodynamics, showing very good
agreement with direct particle simulations, always in the independent case.
These results, mostly obtained in a simplified setting, open the door to many further studies.
At the theoretical level, one may wonder whether theα−stable McKean-Vlasov processes of
section 2.2 have a gradient flow structure, and what it would be. It is also noteworthy that the
structure of the LDP for the empirical measure of L´ evy particles is different from the one found
in [34] starting from a long-range interacting Ginzburg-Landau model, whose hydrodynamic
limit is a fractional diffusion. This raises the question for other microscopic models behaving at
large scales as a fractional diffusion: are their fluctuations locally Gaussian as in [34] and [35],
or ”jump-like” as in the present paper? One could think, for instance, of studying the question
for microscopic models whose kinetic formulation has been shown to converge to a fractional
diffusion in the hydrodynamic limit [56–61]. At the numerical level, the implementation of the
linear fluctuating hydrodynamics proposed in this paper should extend straightforwardly to the
mean-field interacting case. Going beyond the linear setting, to include the full LDP structure
is much more challenging.
