Although the available evidence does not allow a definitive interpretation, the two observed reaction pathways may be associated with two distinct organization mechanisms. The direct formation of a compact cluster with CN 6 along the first pathway may correspond to an early stage of nucleus formation, consistent with a more compact mode of structural organization, as expected in a CNT framework. On the other hand, formation of the large amorphous cluster prior to the reaction along the second pathway is qualitatively consistent with structural organisation through an amorphous intermediate, as often considered in non-classical, multi-step crystallisation. However, analysis of structural ordering beyond the first coordination sphere and extending to larger compact clusters is required to test this hypothesis.






Another important result is that the absence of a systematic increase in the cluster size in the transition region indicates that the formation of the first coordination sphere is predominantly associated with structural rearrangement within pre-existing clusters rather than with their progressive growth.






