2610.17936v1,PUGS: Physics-Guided Uncertainty Modeling for Underwater Dynamic 3D Gaussian Splatting,"In this paper, we posit that 
the central challenge of underwater dynamic reconstruction is not merely recovering geometry from degraded observations, but learning to detect and exploit the physical uncertainty signal embedded in the imaging process.","Conclusion




In this work, we presented PUGS, a framework that reformulates underwater dynamic scene reconstruction as probabilistic inference over Gaussian trajectories. Unlike deterministic deformation fields that conflate structural motion with optical perturbations, PUGS explicitly models physical uncertainty as a learnable distribution over per-Gaussian displacements. A variational deformation field jointly predicts motion means and position variances, enabling reparameterized stochastic sampling that escapes local minima induced by physically ambiguous observations. Dual-path rendering couples a stochastic exploratory branch with a deterministic inference branch via self-consistency distillation and progressive uncertainty annealing, while the learned variance guides adaptive Gaussian densification toward optically degraded regions. Across three benchmarks spanning 22 dynamic scenes, PUGS achieves state-of-the-art performance, including 30.85 dB PSNR on UVEB (+1.16 dB over Deformable-GS) with pronounced gains of +3.74 dB and +3.40 dB on Cave and Boat, and 34.41 dB on DRUVA (+3.99 dB over UDF). 
Limitations
 include a PSNR–LPIPS trade-off under extreme turbidity and the reliance on manually scheduled annealing, both of which motivate future work on data-driven uncertainty scheduling. Beyond underwater domains, this principled integration of physical image formation and uncertainty modeling offers a promising direction for robust reconstruction in fog, smoke, and other scattering media.",cues
