Conclusion




Fenchel–Bregman IVON (FBI) uses an exact reformulation and Bregman tracking for one-draw finite-
K
K
 posterior learning, improving candidate coverage across recursive and autoregressive reasoning.
On matched Maze-Hard, FBI reaches comparable Pass@10 to Direct-10 using 10.0% as many posterior draws and 12.3% of the measured training time per update.
On Qwen3-4B-Base, FBI attains the highest mean Pass@8 across five mathematical-reasoning benchmarks.
We also show that FB’s gains across backbones and with a structured Gaussian posterior support its applicability beyond IVON.
However, surrogate mismatch, stochastic gradient noise, or posterior drift may weaken failure tracking.
Improving surrogate calibration where applicable and using predictive or variance-reduced tracking could make that signal more reliable.
Adaptive or correlated candidate pools and selector-aware training could better match inference, while amortized estimates for unseen problems and broader evaluation across tasks and posterior families could extend applicability.








