2610.17667v1,PrT-Flow: Probability-Tractable Flow Matching for Reinforcement Learning,"However, a robot policy generates actions through finitely many
numerical steps, whose endpoint distribution may differ from
that of the continuous flow. We seek to compute the density of
each action chunk under this finite sampling procedure as the
chunk is generated.","6 
Conclusion




We introduced PrT-Flow to make the probability density of a robot’s
executed action chunk directly computable while preserving multimodal multi-step
Flow Matching generation. The central idea is to make the finite
sampling procedure itself invertible with tractable Jacobian determinants,
so that action probabilities can be computed directly from the sampling steps. JTT combines causal monotone splines and orthogonal
coordinate transformations to accumulate density changes in the
same forward pass that generates an action; CET supervises finite
displacements at the deployment step size, aligning training with
the sampling maps actually used. Proposition 
4.2

establishes that, at a fixed step size and within the same finite
map family, CET and marginal Flow Matching regression have the
same set of minimizing parameters.






The experiments show that the structural constraints needed to
track probabilities do not prevent effective robot learning:
PrT-Flow achieves 48 successes in 50 imitation-learning evaluation
episodes on Robomimic Lift and serves as the action generator in
offline-to-online SAC-style learning on OGBench Cube-Triple, also
reaching 48 successes in 50 final evaluation episodes. These results
provide evidence that a Flow Matching policy can retain multi-step
generation while explicitly evaluating the probability density of
the actions it executes. Constraining the finite sampling maps
allows a single policy to provide both capabilities.






PrT-Flow thus provides a broader basis for studying generative robot
policies: the same finite-step model generates actions from noise
and returns their explicit probability densities. Future work can
exploit this property across a wider range of policy optimization
methods. Finite-step generative policies with tractable probabilities
may thereby offer a general policy representation that connects
the expressive capacity of Flow Matching with likelihood-based
reinforcement learning.








