2610.17465v2,Optimal Feature-Based Newsvendor Policies under Censored Demand,"Both streams operate under the assumption that either the response variable or the censoring variable is known, but not both at the same time.","6 
Conclusion




Motivated by the increasing volume and dimensionality of feature data in inventory management, we study the problem of learning optimal newsvendor policies from observational data when faced with censored and feature-driven demand. Feature-driven inventory literature already offers several methods to learn asymptotically optimal feature-based newsvendor policies from observational demand data, yet in practice typically only observational sales data is available. By contrast, survival analysis literature already offers several methods for the equivalent problem of censored quantile regression, but these methods are either unstable such as inverse probability of censoring methods, or they are bound to specific model types such as censored quantile forests.






We propose a novel model-agnostic loss function approach to learn asymptotically optimal feature-based newsvendor policies from observational sales data, and show how it can be implemented in linear empirical risk minimization, kernel weights optimization, and deep neural network models. We prove finite-sample generalization bounds for the out-of-sample costs of each of these methods, thereby generalizing earlier results for fully observable demand data to the censored demand case, in addition to providing a model-agnostic bound for any model type with a Rademacher complexity that is decreasing in the sample size. Finally, we numerically demonstrate our method’s performance on both synthetic data and the M5 Walmart retail sales dataset, where it outperforms methods that treat sales as demand.






Future research may address further challenges in censored and feature-driven demand problems by relaxing our assumptions or addressing other types of inventory problems. For example, the assumption of conditionally independent censoring may be relaxed using copula-based dependent censoring methods 
(
Crommen et al. 2025
,  cf.)
, and the analytical assumption that observations are independent and identically distributed may be relaxed using the approach by 
Han et al. (2026)
. Moreover, it might also be possible to extend our offline learning method to the online learning case by recognizing that our loss function is exploitation-maximizing, and may be augmented with an exploration-encouraging noise term as in 
Ding et al. (2024)
’s stochastic gradient descent approach. Finally, our approach may be generalized to multi-period inventory problems by using it as a reward function for a deep reinforcement learning algorithm, akin to the direct backpropagation approach proposed in 
Madeka et al. (2022)
.",cues
