2610.17113v1,"Calibrated Typed Decisions for Entity, Relation, and Event Extraction","The remaining challenge is to support user-specified schemas while maintaining extraction accuracy across domains and annotation guidelines. To address this challenge, we investigate whether reward-based training can improve a schema-conditioned extractor beyond likelihood supervision alone.","6 
Conclusion




We presented 
CalOn
, a unified 0.6B-parameter encoder for extraction under user-specified schemas. The model handles entities, relations, and events through span and span-pair predictions. Its training objective combines likelihood supervision with relative rewards on sampled extractions, favouring correct predictions over false positives and incorrect type assignments. Across 40 benchmarks, 
CalOn
 outperforms the evaluated baselines on most benchmarks and leads every reported relation extraction benchmark. It also achieves the highest average F1 for event triggers and arguments with gold triggers. Its advantages extend to tasks represented as span-pair extraction, including aspect sentiment triplet extraction, pronoun resolution, coreference, and temporal and causal relations between events. Probability correction and calibration improve confidence estimates across all three extraction tasks with little change in F1.








Limitations




Our training data and evaluation are limited to English, and transfer to other languages has not been tested. Candidate spans are capped at 32 tokens, and relations are predicted only between spans within the same input. These limits leave longer mentions and relations across inputs outside the model’s current scope. Much of the training data is annotated by LLMs. Voting across annotators and checks by judges help reduce shared biases but cannot fully eliminate them. Calibration parameters are fitted on validation data from the training sources, and the resulting probabilities may remain imperfect when the domain changes. We report results from a single seed because repeating training and the full evaluation across 40 benchmarks would require substantial additional computation.








Ethical Considerations




When applied to text containing personal information, extraction systems can make sensitive details easier to identify and aggregate. Such applications should respect privacy and the conditions under which the data were obtained. LLM annotations may also retain shared biases despite our annotation checks. Predictions and confidence estimates should be reviewed before they are used in decisions that affect individuals.








Acknowledgments




We used LLMs to assist with drafting and restructuring parts of the main text and appendices. The authors reviewed and revised the resulting material and take responsibility for the methods, experimental results, claims, and references in the final manuscript.",cues
