Conclusion




This paper has shown how to produce moment conditions free of the fixed effects
for the dynamic multinomial logit model. Our approach is based on a simple observation: permuting outcomes across time periods leaves the
contribution of the alternative-specific fixed effects unchanged, so the
ratio of a transformed-model probability which permutes the order of outcomes to the true-model probability is free of those effects. When the chosen permutation is cyclic, the transformed model defines
a proper distribution. This alone yields a valid moment condition, and contrasting two such models delivers additional ones for the common parameters. The construction requires
at least three time periods after the initial condition, and we provide an
explicit formula for the number of linearly independent moments that are generated this way.
This number grows quickly in both the number of alternatives and the number of time
periods.






Two features of the construction of moment conditions in this paper are worth emphasizing. First, it is explicit.
Unlike the abstract characterization obtained from functional differencing, our approach
delivers moment functions in closed form that can be used directly for
estimation. Second, the construction is general. The only model-specific ingredient is that
the static counterpart of the model admits a sufficient statistic for the fixed
effects that is symmetric in the time indices. Whenever such a sufficient statistic exists,
the transformed-model device can be deployed to generate fixed effects-free moment conditions for the
corresponding dynamic model with lagged dependent variables. This unifies the
multinomial logit with other results, and provides one way to proceed in
panel data settings that have not yet been studied.






We put our moment conditions to use in two ways. We establish point
identification of the parameters in our empirical specification under support
conditions that, unlike conditional-likelihood arguments, do not require
covariates to be constant across periods.
We also leverage the moment conditions to estimate a multinomial logit model with fixed effects in an application to household soft drink
purchases. Accounting for unobserved heterogeneity in this application sharply reduces the estimated
degree of state dependence relative to a model that ignores it, while leaving the
price coefficient essentially unchanged. This suggests that much of the persistence
in observed choices can be explained by stable unobserved preferences rather than genuine
state dependence.






Several questions remain open. For example, our count of linearly independent moment
conditions is supported by a proof for the binary case and otherwise for three time
periods. We verified the validity of the claim numerically more broadly, but a general analytical proof
for more than two alternatives is still lacking. Also, while the
transformed-model moments are always valid, they need not be well behaved when applied in specific settings. As the
linear panel data Gaussian example illustrates, they can be
heavy–tailed in which case they may be best viewed
as a constructive existence result that complements, rather than replaces,
model-specific reasoning about how best to eliminate the fixed effects.
Characterizing efficient or well-behaved combinations of the available moments,
and extending the construction to richer forms of dependence and to settings
without independence of irrelevant alternatives, are natural directions for
future work.








