Conclusion




We demonstrate an inversion method that incorporates Generative Neural Network (GNN) learned generative manifolds of geological velocity models as informative Bayesian priors in model space. GNNs trained on velocity models derived from geologically realistic sedimentary process simulations help preserve geological realism in the solutions. This study also investigates the extent to which prior information can be incorporated to obtain a geophysically plausible posterior by implementing a hierarchical Bayesian inversion algorithm. Experiments ranging from simple low-dimensional problems to complex geological travel-time tomography showed that the escaping prior produces better results than standard inversion techniques, such as latent space inversion and model space inversion with simple, non-informative priors. A comparative study of three GNNs (Variational Autoencoders, Adversarial Autoencoders, and Denoising Adversarial Autoencoders) provided valuable insights into the characteristics of the learned generative manifold and its role in the escaping prior. We also discuss the structural hallucinations caused by the GNNs. Finally, we conclude that the escaping prior allows us to introduce complex, geologically plausible prior information parametrised by GNNs into geophysical inversions and to find solutions that lie beyond the generative manifold.








