V.1 
Redefining realism




Bell’s theorem assumes a notion of realism, mathematically represented by the standard ontological models framework, under which no account of the quantum statistics satisfies local causality. The localic ontological models framework represents a different notion of realism, under which, in the Bell scenario, such an account exists. Its elements of reality are no longer points of a measurable space—individual units defined by answering every measurement at once. They are sublocales of a locale, the records—relational units defined as modes of restriction of the whole. Records carry the sameness of a measurement across two incompatible contexts without those pairwise identities composing into an assignment across all of them. The common cause becomes a web of such records, and the models of Theorem 
IV.2
 keep the standard local causal structure while the Bell–CHSH value runs from the Bell bound to the algebraic maximum.






A crucial insight that made the result possible is that local causality need not be tied to the standard notion of realism. The Bell bound requires one collection of ontic states to serve all four contexts, each ontic state carrying a response to every measurement. Local causality is then the factorizability condition of Eq. (
12
) over that collection, and Theorem 
II.4
 derives the bound from the condition and the single collection together. In the simple story each such ontic state was a plan: one list answering every question either party might be asked, all at once, across contexts no round can jointly realize. Yet neither relativistic causality nor Reichenbach’s principle, the two principles from which local causality is derived, asks for such a list. They demand that no outcome depend on the distant setting and that the correlation between the outcomes have a common cause in the shared past, conditioning on which it disappears. A web of records supplies the shared past without supplying the list, and both demands continue to hold, leading again to a factorizability condition (Definition 
IV.1
).






Theorem 
IV.2
 is proved by building localic models obeying specific constraints. They satisfy the three axioms of Sec. 
III.4
 (Proposition 
III.4
), which ground the sameness of a measurement shared by two contexts in a part the two have in common, and which require a point to report an outcome of the context it lies in and none of any context incompatible with that one. From the axioms a first key feature of these localic models follows: point-freeness. What two incompatible contexts share must exist and have no point (Theorem 
III.2
), which no space can provide. The second key feature is that the probability theory has to depart from Kolmogorov’s. Each context is normalized on its own and nothing fixes a value at the join of two of them, so wherever two contexts share less than the whole of their weight, no account of the weight is both modular and valued in 
[
0
,
1
]
[0,1]
. Staying inside 
[
0
,
1
]
[0,1]
 costs the modular identity exactly what the two contexts do not share (Theorem 
III.3
).






Apart from these two features, the localic framework behaves like the standard one. It applies to any operational theory, and its definition contains the standard framework as the special case in which every procedure lives in the whole locale, the weight is defined on one and the same lattice of records for every measurement, and every nonzero record has a point (Table 
1
), a case the axioms exclude wherever two measurements are incompatible. On one locale, Sec. 
IV.3
 represents three paradigmatic quantum strategies, a first indication of how a single localic ontological model might reproduce the whole of quantum theory.






The fine-tuning problem that motivates this work does not arise here. No weight and no response function takes a distant setting among its arguments, so there is no influence present in the ontology which is not reflected in the operational statistics. A realist committed to the no-fine-tuning principle now has a concrete way of keeping it. What has to go is the picture of reality made of independently individuated units, together with the classical probability theory that picture carries with it.








V.2 
The first step of a research programme




Theorem 
IV.2
 entails that the quantum statistics in the Bell scenario need not be at odds with local causality. Whether the whole of quantum theory admits of a locally causal localic model, and what properties such a model would have, is the question it leaves open. Older questions would be worth revisiting in such a model, among them the measurement problem 
[
86
]
 and whether the quantum state is ontic or epistemic 
[
66
]
. The present work is therefore the first step of a research programme whose ultimate aim is a reformulation of quantum theory that provides a realist account free of fine-tuning, and with it an unambiguous picture of the world the theory describes.






A localic ontological model of the whole of quantum theory would provide one locale, with a weight for each state, response functions for each measurement, and a representation of each transformation, fixed once and returning the Born rule in every scenario in which they appear. The main obstacle we see at present, and the one this work circumvents, is composition, namely how two localic models combine into a model of the pair and how the composition structure of quantum theory is then recovered. Quantum theory builds a bipartite system by the tensor product of Hilbert spaces, and a localic model needs a rule building a locale for the pair from a locale for each part. The rule we expect is built on the localic product, for two reasons. Composing systems that are spatial can produce parts with no points 
[
72
]
