So far we have used the localic framework to reproduce the kinematics of an operational theory, by considering prepare-and-measure scenarios. The key ontological move has been to take relationality as primary, giving centrality to parts, defined as modes of restriction of the whole. The framework will also have to reproduce the dynamics of an operational theory. One option is to follow the recipe of the standard framework, representing a transformation by a map carrying weights to weights and leaving the ontic state space untouched. A second, which we find more promising, is to make dynamics internal to the elements of reality. The move is of the same kind as making relationality internal to them: a record is then not defined and individuated independently of what comes before and after it, but as an interconnected part of a process. This calls for a sequential composition of parts, one recorded after another, which a locale cannot supply, since its only product is the meet, and a commutative and idempotent product keeps no order. A natural candidate is quantales 
[
5
