V.3 
Morals




In a late paper titled “On the impossible pilot wave” 
[
18
]
, John Bell wrote a short history of impossibility proofs for hidden variable models of quantum theory. It is the paper where he recounts how the de Broglie–Bohm pilot wave theory made him realize that von Neumann’s proof of the impossibility of hidden variables was flawed, and how the pilot wave being nonlocal led him to ask whether 
local
 hidden variable models are possible, the question his own no-go theorem answered in the negative.






Bell’s theorem is one of the main examples of how the methodology of no-go theorems has been key to making progress in the foundations of quantum theory and to identifying its genuine nonclassical features. As argued at length in 
[
36
]
 and 
[
35
]
, that methodology consists first in articulating what, precisely, one takes to be the principles defining classicality, then in formalizing these principles mathematically, and finally in proving a rigorous no-go theorem that demonstrates a contradiction between the formalized principles and the aspects of the quantum phenomenology in question. In this work we complement this with the methodology of 
go
 theorems. Once the no-go theorem is proven, one keeps the principles defining classicality, such as the absence of fine-tuning 
[
130
, 
37
]
 or the principle of Leibnizianity 
[
115
]
, and replaces the assumption concerning the mathematical framework in which they are expressed, with one equally general and equally precise, so that the contradiction dissolves. Bell himself left a remark pointing this way, singling out the impossibility proofs that concern local causality and expressing the wish that they too may one day be “illuminated, perhaps harshly, by some simple constructive model”.






Bell called the closing section of that paper 
Morals
. He discusses there the lessons to take from this history of impossibility proofs and from his own theorem. One arrives at the end of it knowing what quantum reality cannot be, without feeling any closer to what it is. And one is left with these words, as famous as they are empowering:




Long may Louis de Broglie continue to inspire those who suspect that what is proved by impossibility proofs is lack of imagination.




This work is an attempt to compensate for that lack of imagination, by rethinking what an element of reality is and turning an impossibility proof into a possibility proof.






