2610.15976v1,Embedding Information in Policy Randomness: Distribution-Preserving Implicit Communication in MDPs,"Abstract


This paper investigates a new role of randomness in stochastic policies—as a medium for embedding and communicating information.","6 
Conclusion




This paper studied how to embed information into an agent’s behavior without altering the action distribution prescribed by a stochastic control policy. We introduced the idea of 
randomness modulation
 and formulated the problem as DPIC. The key idea is to view the MDP environment as a communication channel, with the agent’s action serving as the channel input and the resulting state as the channel output. Unlike conventional channel coding, DPIC imposes a stronger per-message distribution-preserving requirement. To address this challenge, we introduced common randomness shared by the encoder and decoder and developed the PRAM scheme. By modulating action realizations, PRAM establishes message-dependent couplings between the action and the common random variable, enabling information recovery from the observed state trajectory while preserving the prescribed action distribution. We proved the per-message distribution-preserving property of PRAM and established its asymptotic reliability. Extensive experiments demonstrated reliable message recovery, robustness to model mismatch, and the applicability of PRAM to tasks including multi-bit policy watermarking and trajectory provenance. Extending DPIC to partially observable settings, where the observer has only incomplete or noisy state information 
(
Krishnamurthy, 2016
)
, is an important and challenging direction for future work.",cues
