2610.15907v1,Mind Your Own Role: Reducing Role Overstepping for Effective and Efficient Multi-Agent Collaboration via Dynamic Role Activation Steering,"More recently,  
Liu et al. (2026)
; 
Zhang et al. (2025)
 extracted activation shifts associated with user personal information to guide personalized generation. In this work, we investigate whether role-specific behaviors exhibit identifiable directional representations in activation space that can be leveraged for role steering in multi-agent collaboration. These studies demonstrate that activation space not only contains directional representations for behavioral steering, but also encodes information that reflects the model’s current behavioral state.










3 
Uncovering Role-Related Patterns in Activation Space




Building on the insights of activation steering, we investigate whether role-related information exhibits structured and identifiable patterns in the model’s internal activation space.","7 
Conclusion




In this paper, we presented Dynamic Role Activation Steering (DRAS), an inference-time activation intervention approach to effectively reduce role overstepping in multi-agent collaboration. Based on our observations of consistent role-induced activation shifts and distinct activation patterns between role-adherent and role-overstepping behaviors, DRAS jointly uses role steering vectors and lightweight role-overstepping detectors to dynamically control steering strength during generation. Extensive experiments demonstrate that DRAS reduces the number of role-overstepping trajectories by up to 86.6% compared with Vanilla MAS and consistently outperforms other SOTA methods. By reducing role overstepping, DRAS further improves task quality score by 10.0%–11.1%, achieves a 1.50
×
\times
–1.61
×
\times
 task-completion speedup, and reduces token consumption by 26.8%–38.7%. Moreover, DRAS has much lower offline preparation cost than LoRA-based fine-tuning, making it much easier to deploy in practice. In contrast to post-hoc methods that rely on detection and regeneration after response completion, DRAS performs timely detection and correction during the initial generation process, which enhances both task performance and system efficiency. These results collectively establish DRAS as a lightweight and effective approach for reducing role overstepping in multi-agent collaboration, thereby improving end-to-end task performance and efficiency.






AI Use Statement




In this work, we used the GPT-4o model as an LLM-based role-overstepping evaluator to assess interaction trajectories in multi-agent systems. The corresponding evaluation procedure, prompting strategy, and manual validation process are described in detail in this paper. We did not use generative AI tools to formulate or refine research hypotheses, implement the research methods, clean or reformat datasets, or interpret experimental results. Tasks related to mathematical claims, mathematical proofs, and qualitative or thematic data analysis are not applicable to this work. Additionally, we used generative AI tools to improve the readability of the manuscript and assist with formatting. All AI-assisted manuscript edits were reviewed by the authors. We take full responsibility for the final content of this work, including the text, research claims, experimental data, and artifacts produced with the aid of generative AI.








Ethics Statement




This work focuses on improving role adherence and behavioral controllability in LLM-based multi-agent systems. All experiments are conducted on software-development benchmarks and model-generated interaction trajectories. No human subjects were recruited, and no sensitive or personally identifiable information was collected or processed. The manual validation of the LLM-based evaluator was conducted by the authors and did not involve external participants. The datasets used in this work are existing research benchmarks and are used solely for research and evaluation purposes.",cues
