Currently, voice-related privacy risks are studied under the assumption that the attacker has access only to spoken audio.","Conclusion




Using the PSO framework, we built a semi-synthetic dataset pairing real
anonymized speech with census-consistent geographic attributes, and introduced
a conditional reference that separates candidate-set reduction from the
within-block voice contribution. Geographic side information increases
singling out risk, especially at fine granularities and short recordings,
where signal-only evaluation appears safest.






These results show that voice-privacy risk is not determined by the speech
signal alone. We therefore recommend reporting both signal-only settings and settings in which the attacker has access to side information when evaluating released audio. Future
work will relax the signal–geography independence assumption and also extend the
framework to other forms of side information.








