2610.14639v1,Defending Consensus-Based Cooperative Sensing Against Conformity-Driven Byzantine Attacks in 6G UAV Swarm Networks,"Conventional fusion models, which assume node errors are fixed and independent, are therefore severely mismatched. This article argues for a different defensive perspective: instead of hunting for bad nodes, we seek to understand how good nodes turn bad, characterizing the inducement dynamics with evolutionary game theory and handing this law to the fusion center so that it can dynamically correct the true error level of normal nodes.","Conclusion




This article studied the security of consensus-based cooperative sensing in ISAC-enabled 6G UAV swarms. A conformity-driven Byzantine attack was identified, in which malicious nodes exploit the neighbor update rule to manufacture a false consensus, so that the induced errors of honest nodes evolve dynamically rather than staying fixed and independent, rendering conventional independent fusion severely mismatched. To counter this, the inducement process was modeled via evolutionary game theory, and the resulting law was embedded into a conformity-aware fusion mechanism that dynamically corrects the true error level of normal nodes. Simulations confirmed near-perfect situational inference across diverse attack intensities and network sizes, clearly outperforming independent fusion at low decision latency. These results suggest that understanding the collective evolution of induced nodes, rather than merely detecting individual attackers, is an effective route to secure cooperative sensing in open-airspace 6G swarms.",cues
