Conclusion




This paper proposes a generalized propensity score for treatment effects under interference when
social ties, like the treatment, depend on covariates that affect outcomes. The score is built
from a model for treatment assignment and a model for network formation, and average potential
outcomes and treatment effects are estimated by augmented inverse probability weighting.






The main conceptual point is that treating the network as a component of a joint assignment
turns the choice between conditioning on the realized network and averaging over it into a
choice of estimand and assumptions, in the same way as the choice between effects on the treated
and average effects. Conditioning requires less and is robust to latent homophily that does not
affect adoption. Averaging requires network formation to be unconfounded given observed
covariates, and in exchange delivers a population estimand, effects at exposure levels that
poorly connected units cannot reach, and, where both routes target the same estimand, a weakly
smaller variance. Latent-variable network models let one take a position in between.






For practice, the paper’s advice is specific. Choose the estimand that matches the question,
whether what the program did among the units who could have been exposed or what it would do in
a population whose ties have not been observed, and then choose the score that identifies it.
Check the fitted assignment models against the observed exposure distribution, and re-estimate
with the latent node effects of the network model added as covariates, which in the application
left the direct effects intact and halved the spillovers.






Monte Carlo studies examine the finite sample properties of the estimator, and an application to
the adoption of a political communication technology in Uganda compares it with regression.
In the application the direct effects agree across estimators, while the spillover estimates
differ because the regression imposes linearity in the number of adopter friends. The weighting
evidence on direct effects is thin because adoption is rare, and the spillover estimates are
sensitive to the latent node attributes recovered from the network.






The method has limitations. It focuses on multiple disjoint groups rather than a single
network. Section 
5.2
 shows that identification, positivity and estimation of the
score extend to a single sparse network, but the inference results there are a sketch, and a
variance estimator that is valid when only the score is correctly specified remains open. It
relies on parametric forms for the treatment assignment mechanism, the network formation process
and the exposure mapping, and misspecification of any of these biases the score. In the
many-small-groups regime it cannot accommodate latent node attributes that affect outcomes, and
the plug-in of Section 
4.3.1
 handles them only heuristically in large groups. It is
more computationally demanding than approaches that estimate the score directly from a
parametric model, which limits it to moderately sized datasets. And it is designed for
cross-sectional data in which the network is random but static. Extending the framework to
feedback among treatment assignment, network dynamics and outcomes in longitudinal network data

(
Snijders, 2017
)
 is left for future work.






