Each preregistration fixes a decision rule, called a gate: a complete, valid result below its thresholds is a KILL (the
hypothesis is not supported), and a result that fails its protocol’s completeness or validity conditions is
INCONCLUSIVE.","8 
Conclusion




The original and follow-up DGM comparisons preserved fixed-history endpoints.
Seven original drift cells did not reproduce on a second host; rebuilt images changed 16 nominal majorities (0.83%).
The offline policy rejected none of 59 exposed patches, leaving a 3% risk unresolved; the extension was inconclusive.
Selected cache cases motivate testing accepted alternatives.
Future work should expand exposed samples and replay adaptive search.








Limitations




Coverage and generalization.




Both preregistered gates returned negative within their tested scope. Study 1 covers one benchmark and published runs
from a few model families at one time. Its policy contrast is Go-only: 59 of 281 evaluable candidates, on 25 tasks,
faced a configuration that the offline policy changes. The motivating 3% rejection risk remains unresolved.
Study 2 covers one system’s archive on one benchmark, with cells from three repositories
(Appendix 
E
). Broader benchmark and archive coverage would allow future studies to test how these
findings generalize, including the effects of intermittent failures and adaptive search. The extension remains
inconclusive because its registered control failed; its tally is descriptive.








Acceptance-based evaluation.




Official test acceptance is the operational correctness criterion in this study. Our claims concern acceptance
losses, the quantity a reward or leaderboard consumes; semantic correctness requires separate validation.
Officially unresolved candidates are negatives under this test oracle. The baseline emulates the networked contract
with a file mirror, and candidates needing resources beyond that emulation enter attrition. The reconstructed
gold-bound cache matches the needed module versions in the upstream builder’s cache; file identity was not checked.
Semantic review and file-level cache comparisons would extend the evaluation of accepted and unresolved patches.








Replay controls and selection.




Study 1 uses three repetitions per arm in fresh containers, baseline reproduction of the official verdict, eligible
controls and a network-unavailability signature. The extension retains the controls but counts any failed restricted
run, a broader rejection rule. Selection requires baseline reproduction and eligibility.
Study 1’s 31 unevaluable candidates, including out-of-memory kills, enter attrition rather than the rejection count.
A preregistered baseline stability screen could address intermittent failures in future replays, provided the change
in the eligible population is reported. Replaying excluded cases with larger resource budgets would help characterize attrition.








Statistical resolution.




The preregistered gate decisions are the confirmatory claims. Post-hoc bounds and secondary analyses are exploratory;
the Clopper-Pearson bounds assume independent units. A tally below a gate does not supply an upper confidence bound,
and repeated runs do not increase the number of independent tasks. Future sampling plans should target enough
independent, policy-exposed units to resolve the chosen tolerance. Characterizing rare intermittent failures requires
more than the three runs per profile used here. Network-isolated replays fail network-dependent
tests by construction; we classify these differences as drift, separately from instability.








Prevalence and causal attribution.




The gold-bound cache rejected 6 of 15 accepted candidates in a deliberately selected feasibility stratum. These cases
establish that the configuration can flip an accepted verdict; prevalence estimation requires a representative sample.
Study 2’s original drift count describes one execution, and the second execution reproduced only part of that drift.
Controlled host, image and pytest substitutions would help attribute these differences; the present comparisons
identify neither a host-specific cause nor which verdict is correct. The suite-incompleteness rules are diagnostic
heuristics, and the test-infrastructure intervention leaves edits already present in an image intact
(Appendix 
I
). Causal tests of those edits require separate image interventions.








Version coverage.




Claims about external systems apply to the tested versions. Appendix 
H
 pairs these pins with upstream
releases checked on 2026-10-07. Study 1 tested SWE-bench Pro v1 with its v1 evaluator. The maintainers’ V2, released
2026-09-22 and now the default configuration, uses a different harness, a networked verifier and new images;
evaluating it is a next step. Study 2 used DGM’s pinned 2024 grader commit and September 2025 evaluation images.
Exploratory follow-ups cover a declared log adapter for SWE-bench 5.0.2 and a 2026-10-05 snapshot of rebuilt images,
with reference labels held fixed. Extending coverage to current harness, label and runtime combinations would require
new evaluations beyond the original gate. Appendix 
H
 preserves the dated upstream inventory;",cues
