VI.1 
Prospects for Detecting Low-Mass Objects at Very Wide Orbits




In this paper, we have presented the observations and analysis of the microlensing event OGLE-2016-BLG-0523, which was identified in a search for very wide-orbit binary-lens events among OGLE-IV and KMTNet data. We limited our search to these events because earlier surveys generally had lower observing cadences, making it difficult to distinguish bumps caused by microlensing from other variability based on light-curve morphology or color information.






The upcoming Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope 
[
44
, 
40
]
 and the Earth 2.0 (ET) Microlensing Telescope 
[
21
, 
14
]
 will be more sensitive to short-duration events, and thus to low-mass objects, thanks to their continuous and stable photometry. Their survey fields overlap with the primary fields of OGLE and KMTNet, enabling these space-based telescopes to detect additional low-mass objects on very wide orbits. Cross-matching Roman and ET events with historical ground-based detections will be crucial for constructing a complete census, as well as for verifying whether candidate FFPs are truly free-floating. Moreover, as OGLE and KMTNet continue their observations, additional similar events from their datasets are also anticipated.






An advantage of the Roman Space Telescope is that, when it detects the signature of a low-mass object, it may also resolve the host star through high-resolution imaging. Because the microlensing signal from the host occurs many years earlier and the host has since moved away from the source, Roman has a higher probability of resolving the host star in such events than in typical Roman planetary events. This enables a direct measurement of the lens-source relative proper motion, from which the angular Einstein radius can be derived via 
θ
E
=
t
E
×
μ
rel
\theta_{\rm E}=t_{\rm E}\times\mu_{\rm rel}
. Combined with photometric measurements of the host star, this information can be used to infer the lens mass and distance (e.g., 
Bhattacharya et al. 5
).
For ET, its comparable photometric precision and overlapping observing window with KMTNet and OGLE enable simultaneous space- and ground-based observations to measure the satellite microlensing parallax 
[
43
, 
18
]
. When combined with finite-source effects in the light curve, which are commonly present in planetary-induced bumps, this provides a way to determine the lens mass and distance 
[
10
]
.








VI.2 
Difficulties in Detecting Low-Mass Objects at Very Wide Orbits




From the search for very wide-orbit binary-lens events and the analysis of OGLE-2016-BLG-0523, we identify three main difficulties in detecting such events. First, robustly distinguishing both bumps from other types of variability requires high-cadence, multi-band photometry. In the present case, the event occurred in the KMTNet and OGLE prime fields, which provided sufficient coverage in both 
I
I
 and 
V
V
 bands. By contrast, two other events that were initially identified as having short-lived bumps were ultimately shown to be cataclysmic variables.






Second, the 2L1S/1L2S degeneracy can present a significant challenge. The fundamental difference between the two models lies in the origin of the bumps: in a 2L1S model, both bumps arise from the same source star but correspond to two different 
t
E
t_{\rm E}
, whereas in a 1L2S model, the two bumps are produced by two different source stars but share the same 
t
E
t_{\rm E}
. To rule out the 1L2S model, both the timescale and source brightness of the two bumps must be well determined. In the present case, we are able to rule out the 1L2S model using this approach. However, it is generally rare for both bumps to be significantly magnified (i.e., small impact parameters). For a bump with a large impact parameter (e.g., 
