Several questions remain open. It would be useful to replace the boundedness assumptions on the feature map and innovations by suitable moment or tail conditions, thereby covering unbounded state spaces and systems with sub-Gaussian or sub-exponential additive noise. Sharper control of the lower-order concentration terms, as well as extensions to misspecified models, vector-valued states, and more general initial distributions, are also of interest. More fundamentally, a systematic understanding of the relation between contraction in regularized kernel-induced geometries and classical spectral or Wasserstein contraction could provide criteria for choosing the kernel and the regularization parameter adaptively from data.








