We have formulated exceptional-point dynamics directly in terms of repeated quantum channels and experimentally accessible probabilities. In this framework, no effective non-Hermitian Hamiltonian, Liouvillian generator, or continuous-time description is required. A nontrivial Jordan block of a CPTP map generates polynomial–exponential contributions under repeated applications of the channel, while the appearance of these terms in measurement statistics depends on the overlap between the chosen preparation, the Jordan directions, and the measured effects. Exceptional behavior is therefore an intrinsic property of the channel, whereas its experimental visibility depends on the complete preparation, transformation and measurement scheme.






