Spanish audits [11].","5Conclusion
We evaluated calibrated typed-decision models on Indonesian regional languages with a pre-registered,
parallel design. Zero-shot Jev is usable: on NusaX sentiment it matches encoders fine-tuned on all available
labels, and classical models need more than 500 labels per language to match it in 9 of 10 languages. Its
accuracy,however,followstheclosenessofeachsentencetoIndonesian,andtheIndonesianoriginalrecovers
much of what is lost in distant languages, whereas machine translation mostly does not. Its probabilities stay
calibrated on regional languages, contrary to our prediction, but they rank its own errors less well, so
confidence thresholds transfer less than calibration suggests. A cascade that returns Jev's least confident
decisions to a local classifier is at least as accurate as either model and, for emotion classification, better than
both (+2.9 macro-F1 points). Frontier Claude and GPT models are clearly more accurate than Jev, but at
severalhundredtimesitscostwherecostisreported;Jevistheeconomicalfirstlayerratherthanareplacement
for them.
Thestudyisboundedbyitstasks(sentimentandemotion),bytranslatedratherthanspontaneousregional
text, by one dated snapshot of a commercial service, and by the low power of the label-budget test (Sect.
4.10). Future work should extend the evaluation to other decision types and to naturally written regional text,
test whether confidence thresholds can be recalibrated per language with a few labels, and measure cascades
in a deployed setting with real traffic and latency.
