Conclusion




We present the first heavy-traffic analysis of the hybrid-abandonment tandem queue. We characterize the scaled second queue length by finding its asymptotic mean and showing it is asymptotically exponentially distributed and asymptotically independent of the first queue length. We do this by analyzing countably infinite Markovian arrival process (IMAP) queues, a class that includes our original hybrid-abandonment system. We provide sufficient conditions for the scaled queue length of an IMAP queue to be asymptotically exponential and asymptotically independent of its phase process and verify these conditions hold in the case of a hybrid-abandonment queue. Further, we show empirically that our heavy-traffic results are useful approximations beyond heavy traffic.






A promising area of future research is similar analysis of queues with infinite Markovian service processes (IMSPs), either in lieu of or in addition to IMAP arrivals. We believe IMSPs will be slightly harder to analyze due to unused service occurring when the queue is empty. Another promising extension is to provide an analogous bound on the visitation matrix 
𝑹
\boldsymbol{R}
 and the steady-state distribution of the phase process 
𝝅
\boldsymbol{\pi}
 for phase processes beyond birth-death processes. Of particular interest would be bounds for queueing networks to extend the heavy traffic analysis from two-stage systems to more complex queueing networks.








