Conclusion




In this work, we presented a comprehensive comparative analysis of explainability across three distinct deep learning paradigms: CNNs, ViTs, and ViM, evaluating their alignment with human visual attention. By leveraging ground-truth gaze data from fine-grained natural image classification (CUB), medical diagnosis (CXR), and facial emotion classification (KDEF-FGD) tasks, we quantitatively evaluated model attention beyond qualitative visual inspection. Methodologically, we formulated the comparison as the estimation of the alignment functional of Definition 
3
 under an explicitly specified normalization pipeline (Eq. (
21
)), and we characterized the invariances of the three similarity estimators (Proposition 
1
). This apparatus is what turns several of the observations below from qualitative impressions into statements with a determinate meaning. Our results show that architectural inductive biases strongly affect explainability: CNNs exhibit more spatially distributed attention patterns, whereas ViTs display a more localized and fine-grained focus, a difference whose upper bound is fixed by the 
14
