In this paper, we presented PSCMIA: a power side-channel-based membership inference attack against embedded ML models. Unlike conventional MIAs that rely on prediction probability vectors or predicted labels, PSCMIA infers membership solely from physically measured power traces collected during model inference. Our key observation is that restricting access to model outputs does not necessarily eliminate membership leakage, since the data-dependent computations that produce these outputs are physically executed by the underlying processor and can be reflected in its power consumption. We demonstrated this leakage across multiple datasets, different ML models, and embedded MCU platforms. PSCMIA achieves ROC-AUC values of up to 
0.9065
0.9065
. Moreover, PSCMIA outperforms label-only MIA in most evaluated configurations while approaching the performance of the shadow MIA in several configurations.






Our analysis further provides insight into when and why PSCMIA is effective. Target models that exhibit stronger vulnerability to probability vector-based shadow MIA generally show greater susceptibility to PSCMIA, indicating a relationship between software-visible membership behavior and physical membership leakage. We further show that target-model overfitting and prediction correctness influence attack effectiveness, and that PSCMIA can transfer across related datasets. These results demonstrate that physically accessible embedded ML systems introduce a membership-privacy attack surface that is not captured by output-based threat models. Therefore, protecting sensitive on-device ML deployments requires considering not only what information is exposed through the software interface, but also what information is unintentionally revealed through their physical side-channels.








