) suffices for all practical purpose.","Conclusions




We introduced 
past redundancy
---a framework inspired by QD that conceptually generalizes it in two important ways. First, as the name suggests, it accounts for redundant records of past events, i.e., redundant past-present correlations of an evolving quantum system: this is not captured by any previous definition of redundancy, even when applied sequentially at different times. Second, past redundancy can treat multiple events at multiple times, and the definition of these events does not rely on any system-environment tensor product structure.
10
10


10

            
            
            
          The last point was not emphasized until now to keep the discussion closely aligned with the QD literature. However, past redundancy is defined with respect to event and record random variables, not with respect to reduced density matrices. In retrospective we thus notice that no distinguished open “system” was ever needed (in fact, systems can and often do disappear throughout evolution 
[
3
, 
21
]
).
 Past redundancy also does not rely on conditional independence (like the SBS or branching form) and directly asks for classically accessible records.






Importantly, we showed that past redundancy keeps three key features of QD. First, single records do not need to be ideal and they can reveal little about the event—what matters is an extensive amount of redundant records. Second, the emergence of a redundancy plateau in the 
classical
 MI puts the notion of past redundancy on a firm information-theoretic footing similar to QD—without the problem that quantum MI can contain many correlations that are practically inaccessible. Third, while QD is linked to the pointer basis resulting from environmentally induced decoherence, past redundancy is linked to the existence of decoherent histories. For Markovian open quantum systems one naturally expects that these paradigms can be merged 
[
86
, 
65
]
. Finally, past redundancy contains QD as a limiting case.






What is gained from this conceptual generalization is a more realistic account about the formation of redundant records in a quantum Universe. In fact, as discussed in Sec. 
II.3.1
, records are never used to infer something about the present but always about the past. Perhaps the most important consequence of this insight is then that many models which support only temporal QD support 
eternal
 past redundancy. The emergence of past-redundancy is not restricted to unitary circuits of generalized CNOT-fan-out structure [eqn (
4
)] or to fine-tuned Hamiltonians 
[
14
, 
15
]
—as we showed explicitly, it emerges for a wider class of physically relevant models. In particular, thermalization is no longer opposed to the emergence of redundancy. Testing this in more complex quantum systems, such as those in Refs. 
[
36
, 
16
, 
45
]
, is an interesting research direction.






We can also identify two different mechanisms responsible for the generation of past redundancy. The first relies on the mechanism of QD, which Zurek summarized as an “environment […] relentlessly cloning the information about the einselected pointer states” 
[
33
]
. This mechanism also leads to past redundancy, but it is not the only way to generate redundancy. In particular, this QD mechanism relies on a well-defined pointer basis and it can lead to fragile records, which formally contribute to the redundancy plateau of MI while remaining practically undectable (see also the discussion about the photonic environment below). Moreover, there are important examples which clearly do not fit this “relentless cloning of pointer states” paradigm.






For instance, the abundant number of photons in the cosmic microwave background were released during electron-proton recombination events happening 
≈
13.5
\approx 13.5
 billion years ago. It was clearly not the case that there was any relentless cloning of photons involved—in fact, energy conservation clearly forbids this (in general, conservation laws put severe restrictions on the redundant cloning of information). Another example are fossils such as dinosaur bones. Dinosaur bones encode relevant biological information about an extinct species unrelated to their pointer basis. Also here there is no relentless cloning involved, rather the opposite: the fossil recovery rate of 
Tyrannosaurus rex
 is in between 1 per 16,000 and 1 per 80,000,000 individuals 
[
87
]
. Finally, consider “destructive” quantum measurements such as photo detectors: via a metastable cascade structure the absorption of a single photon triggers a macroscopic electric signal. At the end of the measurement there is no photon left anymore (the quantum MI between the photon system and the environment is zero for practically all times) and the pointer basis of the system plays no role here.






All these examples point towards the fact that “macro-events” are able to create redundancy without the help of QD, i.e., without any relentless cloning in the pointer basis. This can be also anticipated from the example of Sec. 
III.2.3
: one needs a macroscopic superposition 
