In that context it is particularly important to pay attention to the question in which degree of freedom redundancy is encoded. While QD can generate redundant information in the phase of a photon, redundant information is also encoded in the color or brightness of an object. Yet, QD in the sense of a “relentless cloning of information” can not be related to the wavelength or intensity of light owing to energy conservation.






To conclude, that many photons contain redundant information about the object they scatter from is out of question. The mechanism for this can be QD or the macroscopicity of the object (or both). Yet, whatever the mechanism is, it is a logically distinct question whether that redundant information is relevant to humans: this is not decided by quantum MI or the SBS alone. Objectivity relies on redundancy, but it requires more than that. Past redundancy emerges as a flexible, unifying framework in which all these different mechanisms and questions can be studied. 







