Conclusion




We tested whether the gains from mask-and-replace training depend on inference-time token revision.
When each token remains unchanged after first being unmasked, DeMaR retains its WER advantage over mask-only diffusion, with WER increasing by only 0.29 percentage points for CosyVoice3 and 0.14 for FlexiCodec.
Noisy-context augmentation improves WER without replacement supervision in all three seeds for both tokenizers, under both revisiting and commit-only sampling.
Replacement supervision provides further gains in two of three CosyVoice3 seeds and all three FlexiCodec seeds under both policies.
These findings show that noisy-context augmentation and replacement supervision can improve generation even when generated tokens are never revised.








