2610.09168v1,Low-Order Inclusion Probabilities Do Not Determine Asymptotic Normality,"Abstract


Central limit theorems in survey sampling are typically established
under specific sampling designs rather than from generic characteristics
of a design. Motivated by the search for more general conditions,
we investigate whether low-order inclusion probabilities can provide
such a basis. Does such finite-order agreement impose sufficiently strong
restrictions to determine their limiting distributions? We conjecture that it
does not: for every fixed 
r
r
, agreement of inclusion probabilities through
order 
r
r
 alone is insufficient to guarantee a common limiting distribution.","6 
Discussion




The counterexample in this paper reveals a limitation of low-order
descriptions of a sampling design that appears not to have been widely
recognized. First- and second-order inclusion probabilities occupy a
central place in survey sampling: they determine the expectation and
variance of the Horváth–Thompson estimator and enter directly into
variance estimation and many asymptotic arguments. Nevertheless, our
result shows that even exact agreement of these quantities with those
of SRSWOR does not preserve asymptotic normality. Two sampling designs
can give estimators with exactly the same mean and variance at every
stage of an asymptotic sequence and yet have fundamentally different
limiting distributions.






The example also helps explain why central limit theorems for complex
sampling designs typically require information beyond low-order
inclusion probabilities. Successful results often exploit additional
structure of the sampling design, impose conditions on higher-order
dependence, or assume an appropriate HT central limit theorem as part
of the asymptotic framework. Our result shows that such additional
information cannot, in general, be replaced by conditions involving
only first- and second-order inclusion probabilities.






More broadly, Theorem 
2.1
 shows that agreement with
SRSWOR through any fixed order 
t
t
 arises naturally from block designs
of strength 
t
t
. This observation suggests that the phenomenon
demonstrated here for 
t
=
2
t=2
 may extend much further. We conjecture
that, for every fixed 
t
t
, agreement of all inclusion probabilities
through order 
t
t
 is insufficient by itself to determine the limiting
distribution of a sampling estimator. Establishing or disproving this
conjecture would help clarify what information about a sampling design
is genuinely needed for general finite-population central limit
theorems.








