2610.09037v1,When the Governor Becomes the Disturbance: Control-Generated Disturbance and Cost-Aware Backoff in Governed Tool-Using Agents,"Our hypothesis is that a governor’s own intervention cost can interact with
its retained action history to sustain failure, and that feedback-conditioned
backoff can interrupt this process.","9 
Conclusion




A supervisory governor can sustain task failure when an intervention-induced
tool error is retained as a reason to block subsequent attempts. In the
controlled environment studied here, this interaction produces block storms
and lower completion rates. Feedback-conditioned backoff reduces blocking and
can restore success on both a stochastic-policy agent and a Gemini agent.
On the stochastic substrate it also outperforms an approximately rate-matched
fixed weak governor; on Gemini the highest observed success occurs at an
intermediate tested backoff strength. These findings motivate evaluating
governors together with their intervention costs, error memory, and recovery
opportunities.








Data and code availability




The ancillary files accompanying this preprint contain the Gemini episode
checkpoint and the stochastic aggregate summaries used for the reported
tables, together with a script that regenerates their numerical summaries.
The governed-agent framework, induced-cost agents, experiment harnesses, and
analysis scripts are maintained in the CASAgentGovernor repository at

https://github.com/CriticalAttentionSystems/CASAgentGovernor
.
The Gemini checkpoint records episode-level metrics; it does not contain the
complete model responses or tool trajectories.",cues
