Conclusion




In this paper, we study the 
M
/
M
/
1
M/M/1
 queue with infectious and susceptible customers. We analyze a model where susceptible customers can become infected if they overlap with an infectious customer for more than 
ℓ
\ell
 units of time in the queue. We provide new formulas for the probability of infection and expected number of infections an infectious customer might cause while in the system. These new formulas and expressions provide new insights for disease mitigation in the context of service systems.






There are many areas for further research on this topic. The first would be to extend this model to many server queues. This is more challenging as the overlap time for even two servers is more complicated because the ordering of departures is lost. The second extension would be to extend this current model to one with time varying arrival rates or even analyzing the transient behavior of the model. This is challenging since we need to know the transient or time varying behavior of the 
M
/
M
/
1
M/M/1
 queue. Fortunately, work by 
Massey et al. [18]
 might help in this regard. Finally, it would be interesting to consider more complex thresholds that could take into account the amount of time that a customer spends with any infectious customer. These more complicated threshold would enable modeling of different ways that a susceptible customer could get infected from infectious customers.








