2610.08377v1,Data geometry preserves prediction but reshapes explanation,"Abstract




Across scientific domains, empirical data often give rise to positive semidefinite matrices that encode similarities, couplings or interactions and induce natural geometries. We investigate whether changing the geometry used to compare the same empirical representations preserves subject-level performance and the local structures from which explanations are derived, a question we term the prediction–explanation invariance problem.","Discussion




This study shows that comparable predictive performance does not guarantee either decision-level or explanatory invariance. Across three empirical regimes, Frobenius and trace geometries preserved broadly comparable discriminative information when applied to the same positive semidefinite matrices. However, they did not always induce the same neighbourhoods, decision scores, perturbation sensitivities or explanatory rankings. Predictive performance alone is therefore insufficient to establish the equivalence of two geometric representations. Geometry can affect the organization of the subject space, the decisions produced by the resulting classifiers and the perturbation-based relevance assigned to individual nodes. This distinction is consistent with the broader problem of underspecification, whereby systems with similar performance may nevertheless rely on different internal or local structures 
[
9
, 
8
, 
10
]
.






This result is especially relevant for explainable machine learning because many explanatory methods are local. Attribution, perturbation and neighbourhood-based analyses depend on how changes are measured and on which observations or directions are considered close to a given instance 
[
16
, 
17
, 
18
]
. Unlike conventional post-hoc feature attribution, the perturbations were not defined on the training-subject similarity coordinates used internally by the precomputed-kernel SVM. They were defined on interpretable nodes of the original positive semidefinite matrix and then propagated through the distance geometry, kernel and decision function. Frobenius geometry treats matrix differences as distributed entry-wise changes, whereas trace geometry emphasizes operator-level directions of distinguishability 
[
23
, 
27
]
. Both are natural for positive semidefinite data, but they weight the same perturbations differently. Because the kernel SVM depends explicitly on these distances, such differences directly modify the similarities to the support vectors and therefore the response of the decision function. The same empirical matrices can therefore support comparable performance while inducing different neighbourhoods, decision functions and perturbation-based explanatory structures.






The node-subsampling analysis clarified how dimensionality contributes to this distinction. In both morphometric MRI and fMRI, reducing the number of retained nodes progressively increased the concordance between Frobenius- and trace-distance matrices and the overlap between their local neighbourhoods. Lower-dimensional representations therefore produced increasingly similar descriptions of the subject space. At the same time, classification performance and agreement between out-of-fold decision scores and predicted labels declined. This apparent dissociation is not contradictory. Distance-matrix concordance summarizes the dominant organization of all subject pairs, whereas classification depends on the smaller component of that organization that carries information about group separation. Node removal can suppress metric-specific variability and make the overall geometries more similar while simultaneously eroding discriminative information.






Perturbation-based explanations followed a distinct pattern. In morphometric MRI, global and group-difference rankings showed nearly parallel trajectories: moderate dimensionality reduction increased their concordance between Frobenius and trace, whereas the strongest reduction reversed this tendency. This pattern is consistent with a regularization-like effect. When the discriminative signal is relatively robust, moderate node removal may eliminate weak or redundant components and leave a more compact informative structure that the two geometries rank more similarly. Once reduction becomes too severe, task-relevant components are also removed, performance deteriorates and explanatory rankings become less stable.






In fMRI, both global and group-difference explanation concordance decreased as dimensionality was reduced. Here the baseline discriminative signal was weaker, and node removal was associated with a progressive loss of information rather than an initial simplification benefit. Dimensionality reduction may therefore disrupt distributed and context-dependent functional relationships, making the relevance assigned to retained regions increasingly sensitive to the geometry used to evaluate the representation. The contrast between morphometric MRI and fMRI suggests that dimensionality reduction does not have a uniform effect on explanatory agreement. Its consequences depend on whether node removal primarily reduces redundancy or erodes task-relevant signal.






The EEG dataset could not provide an equivalent within-dataset test because its representation contained only 19 channels. Further proportional reduction would have generated very small systems and would not have offered a fair comparison with the MRI and fMRI trajectories. Its high subject-space and explanatory concordance should therefore remain a descriptive cross-modal observation rather than evidence that low dimensionality alone guarantees geometry-independent explanations. More generally, modality, sample size, noise structure, effective rank and the distribution of discriminative information remain partially confounded and should be manipulated independently in future controlled studies.






Taken together, these analyses refine the relation among geometry, prediction and explanation. Decreasing dimensionality can make two geometries more similar in their global and local descriptions of the subject space while making their classifiers less concordant at the level of individual decisions. Explanatory concordance may then either increase or decrease depending on the strength, redundancy and distribution of the retained signal. Geometry-dependent explanation should therefore be understood as the outcome of an interaction among the metric, the information content of the representation and its dimensionality, rather than as a simple consequence of predictive performance or matrix size alone.






These findings suggest that geometry choice should become an explicit component of model reporting. Explanations obtained under one metric may not be interchangeable with those obtained under another, even when performance is similar. Future work should compare families of geometries, distinguish random from anatomically or functionally structured subsampling, and manipulate independently discriminative signal, redundancy, effective rank and noise. Geometry-sensitivity diagnostics could then help determine whether an explanation reflects robust task-relevant structure or an implicit modelling choice 
[
39
, 
11
, 
40
]
. The trace-geometry perspective also provides a connection with quantum-information notions of distinguishability and motivates the exploration of operator divergences and measurement-inspired perturbations as tools for geometry-aware explanation 
[
41
, 
42
]
. In this view, geometry is not a technical detail of representation learning, but a modelling choice that shapes data organization, classifier behaviour and perturbation-based explanation.",cues
