2610.08203v1,Starspots on weak-lined T Tauri stars in the sigma Orionis cluster,"A rudimentary comparison between the results of the light curve and spectroscopic techniques, confirms that spectroscopy is finding larger surface coverages of cool spots. As we expect the light curve technique to underestimate spot coverage, so spectroscopy is likely to be giving a more accurate picture of spot coverage and magnetism on the PMS.","9 
Conclusions




To assess whether WTTS are best fitted by a model photosphere at a single temperature or a blend of two temperatures, we analysed the spectra of 17 members of the 
σ
\sigma
 Orionis cluster and 20 non-member field stars, Section 
3
. Based on the presence of lithium in their spectra, we split the field stars into 17 older field stars and 3 young field stars, Section 
2.2
. We divided the 
σ
\sigma
 Orionis cluster members into 3 CTTS, 4 WTTS binaries, 1 WTTS M and 9 WTTS K samples (composed from M and K-type stars respectively), Section 
4
. Our analysis focused on the WTTS K sample as our fitting technique is designed for K-type stellar spectra and the sample contained no known or suspected binary stars.






In Section 
5
 we saw a clear distinction between the WTTS clean sample and the older field stars. The field stars exhibited little or no preference for a fit by one or two temperature photospheres, while the SOC members showed a clear preference for a fit by two temperatures.
The best fit two-temperature models of the WTTS K sample had cool areas covering 30–95 per cent of their visible stellar surfaces, with hot to cool temperature differences of 1200–1800 K.






In Section 
6
, we compared the model predictions to independent mid-IR photometry by flux calibrating the model spectra using 
Gaia
 DR3 
G
RP
G_{\rm RP}
.
The single-temperature models showed an underprediction of the flux in the IR, while the two-temperature models were a significantly closer match with only a small underprediction of around a tenth of a magnitude.
This confirmed the presence of mid-IR excesses that are modelled by the cool components of the two-temperature models. 
The small underprediction could indicate that our models are too simplistic. It may be that three photosphere temperatures are needed to model the WTTS, an unblemished photosphere combined with both cool and hot components. Alternatively, there may be weak contributions in the IR from the warm inner regions of protoplanetary discs, not accounted for by our models 
(
Paolino et al., 2024
; 
Paolino et al., 2025b
)
.






In Section 
7.1
 we investigated double and binary stars as a potential cause of the blended temperature photospheres. As the field stars did not exhibit any preference for blended spectra, we ruled out chance alignment double stars as the cause of the blended spectra. By comparing the WTTS to models of coeval binaries, we demonstrated that the cool surface area was inconsistent with binarity for 8 of the 9 WTTS K sample and also the WTTS binary sample.






Gravity darkening was explored as a potential explanation for the blended spectra, see Section 
7.2
. The hot to cool temperature differences created by gravity darkening were too small by a factor of four to six to explain the temperature differences. We considered whether accretion could be the cause, see Section 
7.3
. However, we demonstrated the WTTS clean sample were not accreting.






In Section 
7.4
, we showed the two-temperature models for 8 of the 9 K-type stars are consistent with starspots. At such high surface coverages, magnetically induced cool areas may be a more appropriate description. The other star had a hot to cool temperature difference a few hundred kelvin higher than is found for starspots on K-type stars. This star has an extreme cool surface fraction of 95 per cent. It could be consistent with starspot like cool areas if the two dominant components are cool starspots and hot plages, see Section 
8
. Thus magnetically induced cool areas are a plausible explanation for the two-temperature photospheres found for the WTTS K sample. The rotational photometric variability of 4–17 per cent found by 
Smith et al. (2023)
 for four of the stars, combined with our measured cool area coverages suggests the cool areas are relatively evenly distributed in longitude across the surface of the stars.






The inferred cool components cover 30–95 per cent of the visible stellar surface. While these are high fractions for starspots, they are not unprecedented as 
Gully-Santiago et al. (2017)
; 
Cao and Pinsonneault (2022)
; 
Paolino et al. (2024)
; 
Cao and Stassun (2025)
 find similar spot coverage in TTS.
If starspots are the cause of the blended temperature spectra, then our two-temperature models may be approximations of multi-temperature photospheres. Hence, the temperatures and cool component fractions should not be treated as an exact description but indicative of large cool areas.






In Section 
8
, we used the SPOTS models 
(
Somers et al., 2020
)
 to show that a typical K-type star in our WTTS clean sample would have its age underestimated by a factor of 3.0 and its mass by 1.4 when fitting an unspotted model compared to a model with 68 per cent spot coverage.






The dispersion in cool area coverage of 30–95 per cent at an age of around 3–4 Myr, could explain the dispersion in Li EW found amoung coeval low-mass ZAMS stars by 
Jackson et al. (2025)
.






The temperature difference between the hot and cool model components exhibit a tight range without a trend for the WTTS K sample with 30–75 per cent cool surface fractions. This implies the same physical mechanism could be maintaining the cool areas. If the larger temperature difference for the model with a 95 per cent cool surface is due to plages, then this implies the same mechanism is responsible across the 30–95 per cent cool surface areas.








