Conclusion




This paper shows that intra-monthly information improves the prediction of retail default. By using functional representations of three key credit behavior variables, the functional logistic regression significantly outperforms the standard logistic regression on both a test and OOT-dataset. Moreover, the functional logistic regression had on-par performance with an XGBoost model, indicating that the performance gap between standard logistic regression and machine learning models can be closed by using a richer representation of the available data. The similar performance is remarkable since the functional logistic regression is interpretable, allowing for the possibility of its use in a regulatory setup.






Functional data analysis provides a solid natural framework for representing credit behavioral data. The relative time construction of Section 
3.2
 places the recurring events at a common time, and the functional representation is indifferent to whether a month contributes 20 or 23 observations, so neither padding nor truncation is required.






This paper highlighted that credit behavioral variables used in practice are not separate pieces of information, but different functions of three underlying quantities: the type of each account, its balance, and the size of the credit limit. The representation opens several directions for future work. A natural extension of this paper would be to develop a functional model in which the quarterly cycle of credit behavior variables is captured in the model design. Furthermore, the representation naturally extends to other IRB models, in particular the Exposure at Default (EAD) model. Another large area of interest is behavioral monitoring, early warning systems, and potentially the generation of synthetic behavioral data. Lastly, we note that the representation can be used to learn what changes in borrower behavior over time, as a given borrower deteriorates towards default. It would be natural to expect broadly similar credit behavior at loan-origination, when correcting for loan-origination variables and the size of the credit obligation. After loan-origination, the borrowers defaulting will show signs of deterioration, creating potential flags for early warning and borrower monitoring systems.








