Conclusions




We have presented a model for calculating the galactic habitable zone from a hydrodynamical galaxy formation simulation. The model takes into account star formation, stellar main-sequence lifetimes, habitable planet probabilities, and five sources of cosmic catastrophe: core-collapse supernovae, Type 1a Supernovae, gamma ray bursts, star-star interactions and quasar XUV radiation. Catastrophes are classified into extinctions (which make a planet uninhabited) and razing events (which render a planet uninhabitable). The number of observers depends on the probability of life forming on a planet, which is unknown. Thus, we focus on the habitable time created on planets per unit baryonic or stellar mass, and its time derivative (the habitable time rate).






With the fiducial parameters in Table 
1
, we find that cosmic catastrophes cause the habitable time 
rate
 to decline with time for the last few Gyr of cosmic history. The habitable time flattens as it approaches its current value of about 0.02 Gyr per solar mass of baryons, or 0.3Gyr per solar mass of stars. As a function of galaxy mass, habitable time peaks between about 
10
11
10^{11}
 and 
10
12
10^{12}
 
M
⊙
M_{\odot}
 total mass, whether measured per unit baryonic or stellar mass. Our galaxy, and our place within it, is typical of habitable planets. Habitable time does not show a particularly strong preference for galactocentric radius.






The most dangerous cosmic catastrophe in the early universe is core-collapse supernovae, while star-star interactions take over for the last 5 Gyr. Gamma ray bursts and Type 1a supernova are much less significant. The most dangerous galaxies are those that most efficiently form stars, during their initial burst of star formation. Smaller, compact galaxies of about 
10
8
10^{8}
 and 
10
9
10^{9}
 
M
⊙
M_{\odot}
 total mass are also dangerous. Only one star in a thousand in the present Milky Way can expect to have experienced an extinction due to a cosmic explosion.






While cosmological simulations allow for a more detailed and precise modelling of habitable zones, our conclusions are dependent on uncertain parameter values. For example, the rates of extinction and razing by cosmic explosions scale with their radii cubed (the volume), but are calculated via simple models. Our calculations here can be expanded and refined in a number of ways: more detailed models of local habitable zones and cosmic catastrophes, including the effects of cosmic rays, a higher cadence of simulation snapshots, greater resolution of galactic substructure (including stellar clusters and molecular clouds), and general improvements in simulation physics. We leave this for future contributions.








