9 
Summary and Outlook




In this work, we have presented a newly developed event-driven KMC model for H
2
 formation on spherical silicate and carbonaceous dust grains. The model follows individual H atoms from their arrival at the grain through adsorption,
surface migration, desorption, and H
2
 formation via the LH and ER mechanisms. In contrast to previous microscopic models, we explicitly include systematic gas–dust drift by sampling the incident atoms from a shifted Maxwellian velocity distribution. This allows both the enhancement to the collision rates and the anisotropic bombardment of the grain surface to be treated consistently. We further compare constant sticking with a drift-dependent sticking prescription in which the increased impact energy modifies the local adsorption probability.






The main findings of this work are:




•




gas–dust drift introduces a strong anisotropy in the surface occupation and H
2
 formation rate, even for spherical grains. At large drift velocities, H
2
 formation becomes concentrated in a ring perpendicular to the velocity vector.




•




For a constant sticking probability of 
𝒮
=
0.5
\mathcal{S}=0.5
, increasing drift generally enhances H
2
 formation because of the increased rate of H atoms colliding with the grain. However, this enhancement disappears when the effect of the increased impact energy on sticking is included.




•




With drift-dependent sticking, strong drift substantially suppresses H
2
 formation efficiency to 
ϵ
H
2
<
0.03
\epsilon_{\mathrm{H_{2}}}<0.03
 at drift velocity 
U
=
10
​
v
th
U=10v_{\mathrm{th}}
 compared with efficiencies of 20–50% at low and moderate drift.




•




Carbonaceous grains maintain efficient H
2
 formation to considerably higher dust temperatures than silicate grains because of their stronger chemisorption binding. Nevertheless, strong drift suppresses the efficency for both materials.




•




ER reactions dominate H
2
 formation across the investigated parameter space. Strong drift further suppresses the LH contribution because reduced sticking lowers the surface population available for diffusion and subsequent LH reactions.




•




Consequently, the H
2
 formation rate cannot in general be determined from gas and dust temperatures alone. In dynamical environments, the gas–dust relative velocity and its effect on sticking constitute additional parameters that should be considered in H
2
 formation prescriptions.






The present model provides a first step toward coupling drift-dependent surface chemistry to the dynamics of interstellar grains. This model can be applied to rotating and irregular grains
to investigate how grain morphology, shadowing, gas–dust drift, surface chemistry, and grain rotation jointly determine H
2
 formation and the dynamical evolution of realistic interstellar dust.






