2610.06784v1,How to scale your HEP ML models: A recipe for robust architecture comparisons at scale,"For the latter, we predict, to the best of our knowledge for the first time, the jointly optimal model size, training horizon, learning rate and batch size under early stopping.","10 
Conclusions




We have presented a systematic procedure for deriving robust compute-optimal scaling laws, and demonstrated it for multi-task jet-tagging transformers on the 
∼
11
\sim 11
 billion-jet JetSet2 dataset. The methodological result is that reliable scaling laws require the training dynamics to be scaled together with the model and dataset. Predictive hyperparameter transfer removes a source of bias that would otherwise be absorbed into the fitted scaling exponents and floor, allowing meaningful comparisons at scale, where each configuration must be given its own compute-optimal allocation.






Applied to the GN family, the scaling laws show a near-equal 
C
\sqrt{C}
 dependence of model and dataset size. Auxiliary objectives consistently improve the primary jet-classification loss and shift the compute-optimal split toward larger models and smaller datasets, while richer input representations lower the loss but leave the scaling exponent approximately unchanged. The onset of the power-law regime occurs only after a substantial amount of data has been seen, and fits based on smaller datasets can return biased scaling exponents even when the resulting curves appear well described by a power law. Large full-simulation datasets such as JetSet2 are therefore a prerequisite for establishing how HEP models behave at scale, and the ability of LHC experiments to produce and retain such samples is a major asset for machine-learning research in HEP.






This suggests a natural progression from small-scale studies to large-scale, full-simulation benchmarks. A method should demonstrate that its advantage persists as model size, dataset size, and compute increase, that it remains competitive when given the full input information available to the experiment, and ultimately that it holds up on real collision data. The framework presented here can serve as a practical blueprint for future
scaling studies.






The full study required approximately 97k A100 GPU-hours, substantial by HEP standards but modest compared with industrial-scale models. The observed scaling behavior suggests that considerably larger budgets could be productively invested, particularly if the field moves toward generic foundation models. Rather than training separate models for individual applications, one could pretrain a single model at scale, and then adapt the resulting representation to different experiments, detector configurations, and physics tasks through fine-tuning.






