Part of what makes zeros in cases interesting is that they force us to be clear about the interplay between local transmission, importation, and surveillance processes. In many situations, and often for good reason, researchers approach that issue with a mechanistic mindset, imposing structure on all three elements that they hope strikes the right balance between being interpretable, flexible, and tractable.






One consequence of that thinking, since local transmission has a biological foundation and since population movement is potentially measurable, especially these days, is that we often encounter models with a lot of intricacy in the transmission and importation components. The surveillance process then has to be relatively rigid to compensate.






The alternative line of thinking we’ve tried to articulate in this paper is that simplification of the interplay at a larger population scale effects the possibilities at finer scales. That observation meaningfully changes what’s tractable, and it relaxes our need for structure. As a result, in the historical example we considered, we uncovered a set of estimates that highlighted interpretable dynamics in the surveillance system, which could be an important, neglected factor more generally and in modern contexts. It seems to warrant further study.






But as a concluding thought, it’s worth noting that the two lines of reasoning aren’t actually opposed. Depending on the details, if we have good measures of any of the components, they could be included in 

                
                  P
                
              
 or as lens to interpret the estimates, a more intentionally structured alternative to Eqs. 
9
 and 
11
. It’s not all or nothing, and if we’ve learned anything, it’s that we might benefit from thinking across scales more often.






