4 
Obtaining and Evaluating Name Lists




In this section, we briefly discuss how to obtain and evaluate group-specific name lists.






4.1 
Generating name lists




There are different ways to build name lists. First, lists can be curated from domain knowledge or existing records. For example, they may be constructed from prior surveys, censuses, or genealogical records; membership rolls of group-affiliated organizations; obituaries associated with group-specific funeral homes 
(
Chasalow et al., 2026
)
; curated Wikipedia pages 
(
Chintalapati et al., 2018
)
; or the activities of identified users on social media 
(
Wood-Doughty et al., 2018
)
. Curated lists can be highly distinctive, but their coverage depends on the source. Sources tied to a particular place may violate the no-geographic-selection assumption (Assumption 
3.1
) that the geographic prevalence recovery method of Section 
3
 relies on.






A second approach, which makes 
ℓ
\ell
BISG applicable even when no frequency table or curated record exists, is to prompt an LLM to generate surnames typical of a target group, thereby leveraging the world knowledge encoded during pre-training 
(
Dasanaike, 2026
)
. This approach is broadly applicable across many types of groups and targets. Because the model returns the names it judges to be typical, the resulting lists may be common but also include surnames that are not distinctive.






To improve distinctiveness, one can apply a 
within-group exclusivity filter
 by removing any surname that appears on more than one group’s list. This is necessary, though not sufficient, for the geographic prevalence recovery method described in Section 
3
. In addition, one can prompt an LLM to generate surnames that are distinctively associated with the group of interest. The generated list can then be refined by asking a possibly different model to assess the distinctiveness of each name and retaining only those that are judged to be truly distinctive.








4.2 
Desirable properties of name lists




What makes a good name list? Ideally, a name list contains names that are both 
distinctive
 and 
common
 within the group of interest. Distinctive names have high positive predictive value for the target group (i.e., 
ℙ
⁡
(
R
=
r
∣
S
=
s
)
\Pr(R=r\mid S=s)
 is high), whereas common names are held by a large share of the target group (i.e., 
ℙ
⁡
(
S
=
s
∣
R
=
r
)
\Pr(S=s\mid R=r)
 is high).






In addition, a name list should ideally cover different subgroups within the group of interest. For example, a list of Asian names that entirely excludes Japanese names may have distinctive and common names for other subgroups, like Chinese, but not capture the full diversity of the group. If the excluded subgroup is concentrated in particular geographic areas, the list also violates the no-geographic-selection assumption (Assumption 
3.1
) required for the geography prevalence recovery method discussed in Section 
3.1
.






For illustration, we generate a name list for each of the four racial groups in the Florida voter file analyzed in Section 
5.1
 using an LLM, Gemini 3.0 Flash. This corresponds to a realistic setting in which researchers must create lists on their own without relying on name frequency tables. To cover the subgroups within each racial group, we use separate prompts for different national origins. For the White list, for instance, we use three prompts, each requesting surnames of individuals with English and Irish descent; German descent; and Italian, Polish, and other European descent. We split Asian into Chinese; Indian; Vietnamese and Filipino; and Korean and Japanese. Appendix 
A7
 provides the exact prompts.






For each of these prompts, we make six separate model calls at temperature 
1.0
1.0
, and request 
300
300
 surnames per call. With three prompts each, the White, Black, and Hispanic groups have eighteen calls and 
5,400
5{,}400
 requested surnames, while the four Asian prompts give a total of 
7,200
7{,}200
 surnames. Within each group, we pool the results of all calls across prompts, rank the unique surnames by the number of calls that returned them, and then retain the top 
1,000
1{,}000
 surnames. This means that a surname ranks higher when the language model produces it consistently. Because that score is a count over eighteen calls, or twenty-four for Asian surnames, many surnames are ranked in a tie. Since the top 1,000 threshold often falls inside a tied band, we break those ties by the order in which the model first returned each surname.






