5 
Empirical Validation




We empirically validate 
ℓ
\ell
BISG in three diverse settings with known ground truth. First, we use voter files from Florida and North Carolina, where self-reported race is available. We compare the performance of 
ℓ
\ell
BISG with that of the standard BISG based on Census name frequency tables. We show that 
ℓ
\ell
BISG performs as well as the standard BISG despite not using the name-race frequency data.
Second, we use the full-count 1900 U.S. Census to predict Chinese and Japanese subgroups within the Asian population. This evaluates the performance of the methodological extension proposed in Section 
3
 when geographic prevalence is not available for subgroups of interest. This analysis also corresponds to a level of granularity for which no name frequency tables naturally exist. Third, we consider the prediction of religious sect in Lebanon, illustrating the applicability of 
ℓ
\ell
BISG outside the conventional race-based BISG setting in the United States.






In each application, we evaluate the calibration of the predicted probabilities, their discriminatory power, and the bias and RMSE of downstream disparity estimates.






5.1 
Comparison with standard BISG using US voter files




Setup.




We obtain Florida and North Carolina voter files from L2 Inc., a leading national non-partisan firm and the oldest organization in the United States that supplies voter data and related technology to candidates, political parties, pollsters, and consultants for use in campaigns. These voter files contain the self-reported race of each registrant alongside their geocoded residential address, turnout in recent elections, and political party registration.






We collapse the race field into five categories, White, Black, Hispanic, Asian, and other, the last of which includes American Indian and Alaska Native. We use the Florida file of March 7, 2026 and the North Carolina file of April 11, 2026. We exclude registrants whose race is not recorded in the voter file and those without Census geocodes, comprising 
494,443
494{,}443
 and 
627,520
627{,}520
 of Florida’s 
15,017,471
15{,}017{,}471
 voters and 
649,911
649{,}911
 and 
318,860
318{,}860
 of North Carolina’s 
7,249,018
7{,}249{,}018
, respectively. Lastly, we draw random samples of one million registrants from the remainder in each state.






We apply 
ℓ
\ell
BISG and standard BISG using the published 2020 Census block-level race shares as the geographic priors. For registrants whose blocks have zero population, we use the tract prior instead.
