5.2 
Predicting Asian subgroups using US historical census




We next turn to the US historical census, which provides population-scale, individual-level records decennially from 1850 to 1950 
(
Nelson et al., 2025
)
. We focus on the 1900 census, which includes, among other information, individual name, race, and country of origin, and enumerates Chinese and Japanese as separate ethnic groups. We validate 
ℓ
\ell
BISG estimates against these two groups as enumerated in the race field of each individual record, regardless of birthplace.






We consider the challenging setting of Section 
3.3
, in which the geographic prevalence of the target groups (i.e., Chinese and Japanese) is unknown while that of their coarser group (i.e., Asian) is available. This is the setting where name lists are required because we recover the subgroup geographic prevalence from the rates at which the people in a given county have surnames on each list. We do not assume that it is known who is in the Asian group, so we also include a group for each of the other races. This validation set-up mimics a relevant contemporary 
ℓ
\ell
BISG application: Standard BISG is not applicable for more recent Censuses because neither surname frequency tables nor geographic prevalence information exists (or has been made public) for Asian sub-groups.






Setup.




We use the de-anonymized (restricted access) 1900 Federal Census. By 1900, approximately 
126,000
126{,}000
 Chinese and 
94,000
94{,}000
 Japanese Americans were enumerated living in the US, concentrated in the West and in Hawaii. No other Asian group numbered more than 
1,000
1{,}000
. We therefore use the Chinese and Japanese populations as the two target subgroups, and the Asian population as the coarse group. The published tables of the 1900 census report the size of the Asian population in every county but do not decompose this by country of origin. Therefore, if we were using only public information, we would know the geographic prevalence of the coarse group but not of the subgroups, which corresponds exactly to the setting described in Section 
3.3
. We apply 
ℓ
\ell
BISG to all 
75,790,807
75{,}790{,}807
 records of the census, of every race. The groups are Chinese and Japanese, within the Asian coarse group, the other coarse racial groups are white, Black, Native American (“American Indian”), and a residual group. Like today’s Census, the 1900 Census published tables reporting the size of each coarse group in every county.






We conduct two analyses. First, we consider “oracle 
ℓ
\ell
BISG” as a benchmark. Specifically, we treat the county geography of the two subgroups as known by constructing the geographic prior from the census records themselves and apply the 
ℓ
\ell
BISG method. Second, we conduct a more realistic “coarse prior 
ℓ
\ell
BISG” analysis from Section 
3
, where we treat only the geographic prevalence of the coarse groups as known (the Asian, white, Black, American Indian, and other residents of each county). Here, we recover the subgroup shares in each county from the list hit rates by applying the method described in Section 
3.3
 under Assumption 
3.2
. Then, we apply the main 
ℓ
\ell
BISG method to an LLM-based name list for each group. Finally, for both analyses, we conduct a downstream analysis to estimate each subgroup’s socioeconomic standing at the same county level.






The additional assumption (Assumption 
3.2
) for coarse prior 
ℓ
\ell
BISG is that a person has a surname on a subgroup’s list only if they belong to that subgroup (see also Assumption 
A6.1
). Chinese and Japanese surnames largely do not overlap with each other (only five surnames appear on both lists), but many white Americans carry a surname on the Chinese list, so this assumption does not hold exactly here. The validation against oracle 
ℓ
\ell
BISG gives us the opportunity to see how much this matters.






For both versions of 
ℓ
\ell
BISG, we construct a name list for each subgroup by prompting an LLM. As in Section 
5.1
, we also use an LLM, Gemini 3.0 Flash, to assist in writing these prompts. For each subgroup, we ask the model to propose three short descriptions that identify subgroups within that population, then substitute these descriptions into the same skeleton, which asks for surnames “spelled as they were written in American census and immigration records of that period.” For example, for the Chinese population, the descriptions are “Cantonese families from the Pearl River Delta settling in California and the American West,” “Taishanese and Sze Yup laborers migrating to the sugar plantations of the Hawaiian Islands,” and “Fujianese and Hokkien merchants and sailors establishing communities in Atlantic port cities like New York.” We form lists for each of the white, Black, and American Indian groups in the same way, with each prompt referring to its population “in the United States around 1900”; the residual group of other residents has no list. Appendix 
A7
 lists the descriptions generated for every group.






For each prompt, we make six separate model calls at temperature 
1.0
1.0
, requesting 
300
300
 surnames per call, rank the unique surnames by the number of calls that returned them, and retain the top 
1,000
1{,}000
, breaking ties by the order in which the model first returned each surname. To make Assumption 
3.2
 as credible as possible, we then apply the exclusivity filter between the Chinese and Japanese lists, dropping the five surnames that appear on both, and between either of them and the white, Black, and American Indian lists. These three lists are allowed to share surnames with one another because the geographic prevalence of their groups is known and does not need to be recovered.






