Surnames are more informative about religious sects in Lebanon than about racial groups in the United States because families tend to be confessionally endogamous. However, that informativeness is unevenly distributed. The least distinctive names belong to the smaller Christian sects, whose surnames come from a Levantine Arabic ancestry shared with larger neighbors.






Unfortunately, the last census in Lebanon was conducted in 1932 under French supervision, so the geographic distribution of sect from external sources is not available. Therefore, for the purposes of this validation exercise, we form geographic priors using the voter file itself. Specifically for each voter, we calculate the share of each sect among the registrants of the voter’s “locality”, which is a formal administrative term referring to towns and villages, aggregated from the self-reported sects in the registry itself (there are 
