6 
Concluding Remarks




Bayesian Improved Surname Geocoding (BISG) and its variants have become the standard method for predicting individual race in the US. However, the requirement of race-name frequency tables has severely limited the applicability of BISG beyond this specific context. Often, these methods have been inapplicable beyond the coarse racial categories defined by the Census Bureau and outside the US, where such information is rarely available.






In this paper, we overcome this difficulty by developing list-powered BISG (
ℓ
\ell
BISG), which only requires group-specific name lists that can easily be obtained from an LLM. Importantly, when geographic prevalence information is available, the only new assumption is a rank condition on geographic prevalence that will often be satisfied if the groups of interest have at least some geographic variation. That is, 
ℓ
\ell
BISG only imposes this single additional assumption beyond what is required for the standard BISG method.






ℓ
\ell
BISG works by using a previously unrecognized consequence of the standard BISG assumption that surname and geography are independent given group membership. If a variable 
B
B
 predictive of group membership (such as surname, surname embeddings, or embedding clusters) is not directly related to geography conditional on group membership (and if group membership has a geographic pattern), then 
B
B
 will also exhibit a geographic pattern that indirectly reveals how 
B
B
 relates to group membership. By setting up a system of equations, we are able to recover that relationship, which can in turn be used to produce BISG probabilities.






Building on our earlier work of embedding-powered BISG 
(
Dasanaike and Imai, 2026
, eBISG;)
, 
ℓ
\ell
BISG uses a proxy task of predicting list membership from name embeddings followed by clustering of those predictions to construct such a variable 
B
B
. Under additional assumptions on list quality, we have also shown how to extend 
ℓ
\ell
BISG to settings in which prior information about subgroup geographic prevalence is not available.






Our empirical validations show that the performance of 
ℓ
\ell
BISG is comparable to the standard BISG in terms of precision, accuracy, calibration, and downstream estimation performance — more so when lists are of higher quality. This is even though the former only requires group specific name lists. The case where geographic priors are unavailable is much more challenging, but when lists are distinctive enough, we are able to achieve comparable performance to standard BISG. Ultimately, 
ℓ
\ell
BISG represents a substantial improvement over using a geographic prior alone when standard BISG is not applicable.






Because one can use a large-language model to generate an informative name list essentially for any group, 
ℓ
\ell
BISG extends probabilistic inference to fine-grained ethnic groups, to settings outside the United States, and to subgroups within a coarse racial group. We expect 
ℓ
\ell
BISG to considerably widen the applicability of probabilistic prediction methods that estimate group disparities when their membership is not observed.






Future research could improve 
ℓ
\ell
BISG in several directions. One direction is to extend our approach to recovering unavailable subgroup geographic prevalence to settings where additional prior information about list quality and overlap is available. Another is to explore alternatives to our combination of list-based prediction and K-means clustering for constructing the low-dimensional summary variable.






As we noted in Section 
2
, in the settings where geographic priors are already available, the 
ℓ
\ell
BISG framework can be applied effectively without lists so long as embeddings are spatially clustered by the group of interest. However, when geographic information is not available, and for diagnostics, lists are essential. In some of our applications, we find that list-guided clustering only moderately improves on direct clustering. Still, it remains an open question how the added value of lists varies across application types and embedding models.






Broadly, this paper offers a general strategy for incorporating information from (LLM-generated) lists and name embeddings into well-calibrated probabilities that support valid downstream inference while accounting for uncertainty in unobserved group membership.",cues
