Motivated by these recent observations, this work revisits the modelling of GRB offsets with controlled numerical experiments focusing on the dynamical effects of the second SN event. To achieve this, we construct an open-source Monte-Carlo tool, 
GRB-offset
, tailored for our purpose, which samples the direct progenitor system of the second SN event, (pre-SN)He+NS binaries (cf., Fig. 
1
), computes the mass ejection and kick velocity associated with the SN, and integrates the post-SN trajectory in galactic potentials using 
galpy
. This treatment allows us to separate the uncertainties in the upstream binary evolution from the dynamical effects of the second SN event. However, we do not consider the systems disrupted during the SN and the DNSs with 
τ
M
\tau_{\rm M}
 exceeding the local age of the Universe, and our calculation cannot give the absolute numbers of merging DNSs or realistic merger rates.






With 
GRB-offset
, we have investigated the effects of the following physical ingredients, the properties of the host galaxy (virial mass, types of potentials, and star formation histories), supernova physics (kick velocities and ejected mass), and the orbital configuration of the progenitors of DNSs.
We find that the offset distributions of bound and unbound DNSs sensitively depend on the investigated physical ingredients. In particular, for tight NS+(pre-SN)He binaries, the mass ejection during the SN can produce large space velocities, even if the momentum kick imparted to the newborn NS is small. In addition to the gravity of the host galaxy, the star formation history can also strongly shape the expected offset distributions by altering the fractions of DNS of different merger times. For the GRB events occurring at high redshifts, the bound and unbound population are not distinguishable any more, as the merger time is limited by the local age of the Universe.






These combinations of physical ingredients introduce substantial degeneracies when interpreting observed offsets. Therefore, an observed offset alone can hardly constrain the properties of the DNS progenitor or the likelihood of a proposed host galaxy. A more robust host galaxy determination should combine the offset information with the chance-coincidence probability and independent constraints on the proposed host.




