2610.04322v1,Generalized Dunkl Quantum Systems with Energy-Dependent Interactions: Exact Solvability and Thermodynamic Properties,"Abstract


This work investigates a class of exactly solvable quantum systems described by a generalized Dunkl–Schrödinger equation with energy-dependent interactions.","5 
Conclusion




In this work, we have developed a generalized Dunkl quantum framework incorporating energy-dependent interactions through a two-parameter deformation of the Dunkl differential operator. This extension modifies the underlying algebraic structure and naturally leads to a generalized continuity equation together with a modified probability density, conserved current, and normalization condition, ensuring a consistent probabilistic interpretation for energy-dependent quantum systems.






As an exactly solvable application, we investigated the harmonic oscillator with a linearly energy-dependent potential. Exact analytical solutions for both the eigenfunctions and energy spectrum were obtained in closed form. The spectrum exhibits several distinctive features arising from the interplay between the generalized Dunkl operator and the energy-dependent interaction, including parity splitting between even and odd states, nonlinear dependence on the quantum number, and increasing level spacing at higher energies. The limiting cases correctly recover the generalized Dunkl oscillator, the conventional energy-dependent oscillator, and the standard one-dimensional harmonic oscillator, confirming the consistency of the formalism.






The thermodynamic behavior of the system was analyzed by constructing the canonical partition function within the Euler–Maclaurin approximation. The corresponding entropy, internal energy, and specific heat reveal that the Wigner parameter, deformation parameter, and energy-dependence parameter significantly modify the thermal response of the system. In particular, the Wigner parameter increases the energy spectrum and enhances the internal energy and specific heat, the deformation parameter strongly affects all thermodynamic quantities, and the energy-dependent interaction provides an additional mechanism for controlling both spectral and thermal properties.






The present model establishes a unified framework combining generalized Dunkl operators with energy-dependent quantum interactions while preserving exact solvability. The results enrich the family of solvable quantum systems possessing reflection symmetry and may provide a useful foundation for future investigations of more general potentials, higher-dimensional Dunkl systems, relativistic extensions, supersymmetric formulations, and quantum statistical applications.








