2610.04183v1,Language Model Activations Inhabit Privileged Error-Correcting Basins,"We hypothesize that this is accomplished via an error-correcting dynamic, wherein
the model layers distort the latent space to funnel activations toward a set of attracting basins [Hopfield, 1982,
Khona and Fiete, 2022, Sussillo and Barak, 2013, Hänni et al., 2024]. Results
from Section 3.2 show that natural activations inhabit separate attracting basins that tile the activation
space. Our hypothesis predicts that model behavior is resilient to interventions like steering because successive
layers error-correct the perturbed activations back into natural basins. This observation suggests an improved method for calibrating steering strength. We hypothesize that
steering works by moving activations from one basin to another.","5 Conclusion
Our investigation began with an intuition about how geometric constraints in activation space—like biome-
chanical constraints in living organisms—could contribute to robust and desirable behavior in language models.
Based on this premise, we employed probes to investigate the activation space geometry. Our observations
allowed us to characterize the activation space geometry as a set of attracting basins that exhibit error
correction, forming privileged regions where natural activations reside. Armed with this understanding, we
were able to gain insights into model steering, observing feature-specific basins in refusal and multilingual
steering directions, whose structure suggests hierarchical representation within the model. Hypothesizing that
ideal steering moves activations between feature basins, we develop a basin-aware, parameter-free steering
method and show that it improves over fixed-strength steering in the English-Chinese steering setting. This
result establishes the role of these feature basins in model behavior, and recommends analysis of activation
space geometry through layer dynamics as a fruitful and practically useful lens for interpretability.
This work is largely exploratory, and as a consequence, we leave many open questions and ideas for future
work. For instance, we anticipate that the geometric picture becomes more complex when considering very
similar activations, e.g., activations for the same token in slightly different contexts, which may occupy a
low-dimensional manifold. We also anticipate that feature basins could play a role in circuits and more complex
computation in models. Lastly, we are excited by the prospect of exploring the activation space without relying
on data. Our work focused on feature directions and activations obtained from data, but new methods [e.g.,
Mack and Turner, 2024] could automatically find new basins or other consequential geometric features within
the space to predict how the model will behave and how it computes.
