2610.04033v1,Gated Graph Neural Networks for Learning Hidden Independent Cascade Dynamics,"In this work, we aim to estimate a separate spreading probability for every node from indirect observations made only after the cascade has terminated.","4 
Conclusion




We studied heterogeneous parameter inference for spreading processes when
only the seeds and noisy terminal symptoms are observed, while the infection
states and transmission paths remain latent. We first formulated the HIC model
mathematically for this hidden-observation setting, including the latent
spreading process, noisy observation mechanism, and observed-data likelihood.
Building on this formulation, we introduced SACF, which summarizes repeated
hidden-cascade observations into graph-aware node features, and SAGE-HC, a
gated graph neural network that learns an amortized mapping from these
observations to node-level spreading parameters without reconstructing
individual latent cascades. We also adapted Dynamic Message Passing to the HIC
observation model, providing a likelihood-based benchmark for the same
inference problem.






The experiments reveal a clear dependence on network structure and observation
quality. DMP is particularly effective on the tree, whereas SAGE-HC performs
better on heterogeneous, loopy networks, especially under noisy observations.
The stability and sensitivity analyses further show that observation noise
increases prediction variability, while additional parameter assignments,
cascade evidence, and repeated feature realizations improve estimation with
diminishing returns. A model trained with an observation-rich cascade budget
also remains effective at substantially smaller test-time budgets.






The present framework assumes a fixed known network and known observation
parameters. Joint inference of spreading and observation parameters, transfer
across related network structures, and applications where the latent spreading
parameters are unavailable remain natural directions for future work.






