Conclusion




The halo model is a ubiquitous tool for modeling the cosmological structure in our Universe, spanning kiloparsecs to gigaparsecs. The halo concentration is a key ingredient in this model, particular on length scales smaller than a few megaparsecs (i.e. the largest halo scales). Most analyses only consider the mean concentration at fixed halo mass, while some also include the scatter in this relation. Other analyses, such as the modeling of strong lenses, depend on the full distribution of the halo concentration and model tend to model it as a Gaussian in log concentration with a fixed cosmology-independent scatter. In this work, we quantify the higher moments of the concentration–mass relation using a normalizing flow model and extract the moments’ dependence on both cosmology and astrophysics. We study the connection with cosmology in a dark matter-only (DMO) simulation, as a reference, and then extend to simulations with the IllustrisTNG galaxy formation model.






The main results from our work are as follows:




•




In DMO simulations, the scatter in halo concentration is nearly independent of cosmology for higher-mass objects but increases by a factor of 1.6 form simulations with higher 
Ω
m
\Omega_{\rm m}
 and 
σ
8
\sigma_{8}
 (Figure 
2
). This is connected directly to the increased presence of active mergers and tidally stripped, low-mass systems.




•




The higher moments of the concentration in these DMO simulations are non-zero and show a clear dependence on halo mass and on cosmology (Figure 
2
). At higher masses, the concentration is skewed low by active mergers causing more diffuse halos, while at lower masses, tidal stripping results in more concentrated objects.




•




In simulations with galaxy formation, the mean relation shows a dependence on cosmology and astrophysics that is consistent with existing 
Camels
-based work (Figure 
3
). The scatter has a significant dependence on the baryon fraction and on the SNe feedback strength (Figure 
4
), as increasing the baryon fraction causes the concentration scatter to be dominated by astrophysical processes rather than by mergers and tidal stripping. The astrophysical effects result in a non-monotonic increase in the scatter at Milky Way-mass scale.




•




The skewness in the concentration, when the simulations include galaxy formation, is qualitatively similar to that that measured in DMO simulations. It shows a large sensitivity to the baryon fraction but is insensitive to the other parameters (Figure 
5
).




•




The behavior in the kurtosis follows largely from that of the skewness (Figure 
6
) as the two are linked by an inequality condition.










In general, we show that the distribution in halo concentration (after subtracting out the mean relation) is sensitive to cosmology in the DMO case, and to both cosmology and astrophysics when including galaxy formation physics. The skewness and kurtosis of this distribution is non-zero in all cases, with the non-Gaussian nature of the PDF being dominated by the skewness. The importance of this behavior in models that use the halo concentration depends entirely on the chosen model and its sensitivity to the mean concentration compared to the full distribution of the concentration.






Throughout this work, we have focused on the IllustrisTNG model of galaxy formation. However, the properties of baryons in halos vary when considering simulations with different galaxy formation models 
(
Farahi et al. 2018
; 
Chua et al. 2019
; 
Anbajagane et al. 2020
; 
Chua et al. 2021
; 
Lim et al. 2021
; 
Anbajagane et al. 2022a
; 
Stiskalek et al. 2022
, 
e.g.
, )
. The distribution of halo concentration can therefore have sensitivity to the exact underlying model. We have briefly checked that the behavior discussed above is not qualitatively different when considering one another model provided in the 
Camels
 suite, based on the ASTRID galaxy formation prescription. However, future work will need to study these simulation variants in more detail to generalize our results. Given the behavior in the skewness mimics that of the DMO case, we hypothesize that its behavior is fairly general and will be present across different galaxy formation models.






As analyses of the ultra small dark-matter halos become increasingly prevalent, for example in studies of strong lensing systems or stellar streams, the importance of the full distribution of the halo concentration will only grow. Our work presents a useful characterization of the relevant dependencies of such a distribution, and also a robust methodology for characterizing these features. Future efforts will need to utilize high-resolution, large-box simulations to revisit these behaviors (even for the DMO case) and build a model with adequate precise for use in cosmological analyses of these rich and varied observable systems.








