We develop an interactive scoring-based preference-learning framework, based on discrete-time Predictable Forward Exploratory Reward (PreFER) processes, to infer the client’s risk aversion. By modeling the client’s indecisiveness and investment preferences through relaxed stochastic control, we propose an interactive learning procedure in which the client’s evaluation of a recommended investment prospect is modeled through an acceptance probability, which serves as a latent satisfaction score. We then analyze the properties of this acceptance probability to justify the scoring scheme and to show how the robo-advisor uses the observed feedback to update its estimate of the client’s risk aversion. Our results show that PreFER processes provide a tractable framework for learning client preferences through interaction and carrying the learned preference forward to generate personalized recommendations under evolving market conditions.
At each new time point, conditional on the available information, we identify an acceptance interval from which any recommended strategy achieves a score above the prescribed threshold, even as market conditions evolve. This enables automated updating of the investment recommendation with minimal human intervention.






An important direction for future research is to study the effect of market-model misspecification on the implementation of learned preferences. In particular, while preference learning in the present framework is conducted conditional on the market outlook communicated by the robo-advisor, a robust extension could account for uncertainty in translating the learned preference into investment recommendations. Another natural direction is to extend the inverse-learning analysis beyond the scalar CARA specification to richer or multidimensional preference classes, for which one-to-one identification may no longer hold.








