Conclusions




We studied a distributed learning scenario in which agents’ data are correlated. This generalizes the federated learning scenario where agents have independent data. To allow agents to learn from each other, we propose cordial learning, where agents send their embedded outputs to others. The agents train layers to learn how to extract valuable information from the embedded outputs of other agents. For a linear model where the label of an agent is a linear mixture of the inputs of all agents, under mild assumptions, we proved that cordial learning converges to models that minimize the sum of losses of all agents. Experiments on multi-digit MNIST with deep neural networks show that cordial learning performs well even beyond the theoretical model we analyzed.






Our analysis focused on asymptotic convergence to the optimal models. The question of analyzing and optimizing the convergence rate remains open. The related question of proving optimal regret bounds in online learning settings is also open.






Cordial learning is a first step towards studying distributed learning scenarios with correlated data. A challenging open question from this perspective is designing a cordial learning scheme where agents learn how to efficiently compress the information they send based on the loss functions of the receiving agents.








