This paper studied the secrecy performance of a cooperative SWIPT-NOMA system with AN-aided relaying under practical impairments. In the considered system, the source employs OSTBC transmission, legitimate users adopt RAS, and the EVE applies MRC. The effects of CEEs, FBD, ipSIC, and imperfect AN cancelation are incorporated into the analysis to reflect practical transmission conditions. 
Analytical SOP expressions and high-SNR secrecy characterizations
 are derived and secrecy throughput and SEE are also characterized.
The analytical results are verified by Monte Carlo simulations, showing a close agreement between theory and simulation. The results demonstrated that the proposed scheme benefits from both transmit diversity provided by OSTBC and receive diversity provided by RAS. Increasing the OSTBC order and the number of receive antennas at the legitimate users improves the secrecy performance, whereas increasing the number of EVE antennas degrades it due to the enhanced combining capability of EVE. It is also shown that the AN power allocation factor has a significant effect on the system performance. An appropriate choice of 
λ
\lambda
 improves the secrecy performance by degrading the EVE link while maintaining sufficient power for AF transmission.
Under ideal 
CSI
 conditions, the SOP decreases with increasing transmit SNR, and the asymptotic results accurately characterize the high-SNR behavior. In contrast, under practical impairments, the SOP curves converge to non-zero error floors, which indicates that residual interference and channel uncertainty limit the achievable secrecy performance. In particular, ipSIC causes noticeable degradation for the users performing SIC, while imperfect AN cancelation and outdated/erroneous channel information further reduce the secrecy reliability. The results also showed that proper allocation of AN power can alleviate the performance loss caused by additional EVE antennas and improve both secrecy throughput and SEE.
Overall, the considered OSTBC/RAS-based SWIPT-NOMA system with relay-generated AN provides an effective framework for enhancing physical-layer security in energy-constrained cooperative networks. The obtained results highlight the importance of jointly selecting the OSTBC structure, the number of receive antennas at the legitimate users, the PS ratio, and the AN power allocation factor, while accounting for the combining capability of EVE and practical impairments.








