We studied intent-hiding jailbreaks through an information-theoretic formulation in which a harmful target task is combined with benign auxiliary tasks. Our model expresses perfect bundle-level concealment through prior–posterior matching. We proved that exact matching under a bundle-size constraint is NP-hard, derived a water-filling solution for fractional weights, and characterized the smallest bundle satisfying a one-sided safety threshold. We extended the formulation to natural-language queries, jointly considering intent concealment and target preservation. We also carried out a broad empirical evaluation across bundle sizes, query generators, and responding models from the Gemma, Qwen, and Mistral families, measuring both whether a target could be reached and how fully the resulting response carried out that target.






Several key insights emerge from this work. First, composition substantially changed model behavior: targets that were refused as direct requests could often be reached once even a small number of benign auxiliary tasks were added. Second, these effects transferred across model families, and the large differences observed on direct requests became much smaller after composition. Third, increasing model size did not lead to a consistent increase in robustness over the range we evaluated, while differences in post-training appeared to matter more. Finally, query realization itself mattered: when the same bundles were written by different query generators, the resulting attack success could differ substantially for the more robust responding model. At the same time, increasing the number of auxiliary tasks reduced average target preservation for several models, showing that stronger composition can help bypass safeguards while also making the original target harder to carry through faithfully.






