2610.01667v1,Conditioning LLMs on Social Value Orientation improves behavioural alignment in a sequential social dilemma,"Abstract


Large language Models (LLMs) are increasingly used to simulate human decision-making, yet their outputs often under-represent human behavioural heterogeneity. We investigate whether conditioning LLMs on Social Value Orientation (SVO, a measure of how individuals value their own outcomes relative to others’) can better reproduce human behaviour in a sequential social dilemma. We expect that, in the latter case, the outcome distribution at the population level, obtained by aggregating all behavioural profiles according to the prevalence of their respective SVO values in human data, is more closely aligned with the diverse human reference than the default distribution. In particular, we hypothesise that this increased diversity stems from the fact that a prosocial conditioning leads to higher-payoff outcomes in the CG, while an individualistic conditioning shifts the LLM’s behaviour towards the rational but sub-optimal outcome in the CG.","3 
Conclusions




We investigated whether SVO can be used to condition LLMs to generate more representative behaviour in a sequential social dilemma.
Using human experimental data from two variants of the CG as a reference, we compared the default behaviour of eight LLMs with behaviour obtained by conditioning the same models on SVO profiles observed in the human sample.
First, we show that the default behaviour of LLMs differs substantially across models.
While some models follow almost deterministic quasi-rational strategies, others exhibit varying degrees of cooperation.
Within each model, however, strategies remain relatively stable across the two game variants and prompt types.
This contrasts with the human reference, where individuals are influenced by the different terminal outcomes of the two games.
In addition, LLM behaviour is considerably more deterministic than human behaviour, resulting in default outcome distributions that remain relatively distant from the human reference.






These differences motivated us to investigate whether conditioning LLMs on a relevant source of individual variation (here the SVO) could provide more representative behaviour.
Our results show an important distinction between eliciting social preferences from LLMs and using those preferences to condition their behaviour.
We found that SVO elicited directly from the models is sensitive to prompt framing and option order, suggesting that it should not be interpreted as a stable, human-like preference.
In contrast, when models are conditioned on the SVO profiles observed in the human sample, their output systematically becomes more aligned with the human reference.
In particular, we showed that the models reproduce the association between social preferences and strategic choices in the CG found in human data:
higher induced SVO values generally lead to more cooperative behaviour in the CG.
This distinction suggests that the usefulness of SVO for behavioural simulation does not depend on LLMs possessing stable social preferences themselves, but rather on their ability to map preference profiles onto corresponding strategic behaviour.






Despite these promising results, several limitations remain. First, our analysis is restricted to SVO and two variants of the CG.
Whether the same approach generalises to other psychological dimensions, strategic interactions, and behavioural domains remains to be established.
Second, the effectiveness of SVO conditioning depends on a human reference distribution from which the profiles are drawn.
Third, substantial differences remain across models, and improved alignment is not universal.
In some conditions, SVO-conditioned models remain further from the human reference than a simple random agent baseline.
Future work should investigate when behavioural associations transfer across tasks and models, and whether combining multiple empirically grounded individual characteristics can reproduce richer forms of human behavioural diversity.",cues
