V.3 
SMG formation mechanisms: merger, environment, or secular evolution?




We find that even among the CO/[C 
ii
] spec-
z
z
 SMGs sample with the loosest criterion category (Category E), only 71.15% are associated with overdensity structures. We also find that, when examining the brightest SMGs (
S
850
​
μ
​
m
S_{850\,\mu\rm{m}}
 within the top 16th-percentile, 
≳
5.3
\gtrsim 5.3
 mJy), their overdensity fraction is even lower than the bulk of SMGs in Category E.






This naturally raises the question of how these exceptionally bright SMGs trigger their extreme starburst activity in non-overdense environments. Since high gas accretion rates at the overdense nodes of LSS cannot fully explain our statistics, we suggest that major mergers may serve as an alternative mechanism for triggering these starburst SMGs in less-dense regions.






We analyzed morphologies of all 449 SMGs using JWST images from the COSMOS-Web survey. Because SMGs are intrinsically very red, they are typically faint in the shorter-wavelength F115W and F150W bands. Consequently, only the F277W and F444W bands were viable for our study. Between the two, we selected the F277W band because it offers superior angular resolution. We therefore performed the morphological analysis using 
Statmorph (Version: 0.6.0)
 
[
87
]
 on the JWST NIRCam F277W images for the all SMGs. This choice of redder, high-resolution NIRCam filters (e.g., F277W) is a well-established strategy in recent COSMOS-Web morphological studies to effectively trace rest-frame optical structures while minimizing biases from severe dust attenuation 
[
50
]
. After running the program, the Gini-M
20
 
[
36
, 
77
, 
76
]
 parameter results for 447 sources were finally obtained.






To evaluate the morphological properties of our sample, we adopt the empirical Gini–
M
20
\rm M_{20}
 classification boundary from 
[
75
]
. The Gini–
M
20
\rm M_{20}
 diagram is a widely used method for distinguishing galaxies with potential merger activity from non-merging systems based on two metrics. The Gini coefficient (
G
G
) quantifies the inequality of a galaxy’s relative flux distribution; 
G
=
1
G=1
 indicates that all light is concentrated in a single pixel, whereas 
G
=
0
G=0
 represents a perfectly uniform distribution. The 
M
20
\rm M_{20}
 parameter measures the normalized second-order spatial moment of the brightest 20% of a galaxy’s pixels. Galaxies featuring bright, clumpy, and spatially dispersed fragments exhibit higher 
M
20
\rm M_{20}
 values, while those with less dispersed light distributions yield lower values. By combining these two metrics, this empirical boundary effectively distinguishes highly disturbed, merger-like candidates from relatively undisturbed systems (Figure 
12
). Based on this diagnostic, the brightest SMGs exhibit a substantially enhanced merger fraction compared to the general population. Specifically, we find that 47.2% (17/36, red circles) of the brightest SMGs residing in overdense regions and 50.0% (18/36, blue diamonds) of their non-overdense counterparts are classified as mergers. In contrast, the underlying, fainter SMG population (
N
=
375
N=375
, light gray points) displays a lower merger fraction of 28.8%. This baseline fraction is broadly consistent with typical sub-millimeter samples. For instance, 
[
29
]
 analyze 125 SMGs in the COSMOS field using a similar Gini–
M
20
\rm M_{20}
 approach and report an elevated merger rate in their bright group (27.5%) relative to their faint group (21.3%). Although they adopt a simplified power-law relation (
S
850
​
μ
​
m
/
S
obs
=
(
ν
850
​
μ
​
m
/
ν
obs
)
3.8
S_{850\mu\text{m}}/S_{\text{obs}}=(\nu_{850\mu\text{m}}/\nu_{\text{obs}})^{3.8}
) to extrapolate fluxes across different observing bands, their observed trend of higher merger fractions in brighter sources is well aligned with our results. This morphological dichotomy suggests that the triggering mechanisms for extreme starbursts scale strongly with sub-millimeter luminosity. For the general SMG population, the 
∼
\sim
29% merger fraction indicates that their star formation is likely sustained by a diverse combination of physical processes, potentially including secular evolution, minor interactions, and steady cold gas accretion within large-scale environments. Conversely, the high merger rates (
∼
\sim
49%) observed among the brightest SMGs, regardless of their large-scale environmental density, suggest that the most extreme dust-obscured starbursts are predominantly driven by local major mergers or violent disk instabilities (
Dekel et al. 44
).






In Figure 
13
, we present JWST RGB images of the 30 brightest SMGs (
