2610.00907v1,STEER: Reducing Inference Cost in Relational Foundation Models through Semantically Informed Sampling,"Starting from the seed node, the sampler traverses the graph along edges representing foreign-key links and adds the rows encountered during traversal to the sample. The process continues for a number of steps until a stopping condition is met, with the assumption that the resulting neighborhood provides informative context for prediction.","10. 
Conclusion




We presented STEER, a semantically informed sampling approach for reducing RFM inference cost. STEER uses an LLM to assess the relevance of schema edges to a prediction task and converts these judgments into traversal probabilities for sample construction. The resulting relevance configuration is reused across predictions, amortizing the cost of the LLM calls. Across three RFMs, STEER substantially reduces inference context size while preserving, and in several cases improving, prediction accuracy. In contrast, uninformed random pruning causes substantial accuracy degradation, showing that effective context reduction requires reasoning about which parts of the relational structure are relevant to the task.






Several directions remain for future work. First, integrating STEER into multiple RFMs highlights the value of a common SQL-based sampling abstraction that can be shared across models. Second, the optional column filter suggests that semantic guidance can extend beyond edge traversal, although its mixed results indicate that more robust methods are needed, potentially combining schema semantics with lightweight data statistics. Finally, task-specific probability profiles can sometimes outperform the default profile, motivating inexpensive methods for adapting traversal probabilities without costly search. More broadly, applying semantic guidance to other RFM architectures and sampling strategies may yield further reductions in inference cost without sacrificing predictive accuracy.",cues
