Conclusion




We present DWS, a structured workload abstraction for dLLM serving.
Unlike scalar proxies such as output length or total denoising steps, DWS preserves the heterogeneous computation structure across output blocks and denoising steps, enabling a more 
faithful
 estimation of request inference cost.
We further design an accurate prompt-only DWS predictor for each request’s 
fine-grained
 denoising structure, whose lightweight design enables 
efficient
 prediction even on a single CPU core.
By 
decoupling
 request-dependent execution behavior from deployment-specific cost factors, DWS can adapt across deployments through lightweight profiling without retraining its prompt-only predictor.
Experiments show that DWS consistently improves both cost-modeling fidelity and cost-prediction accuracy, yielding better serving performance with strong generalization.






