In conclusion, heterogeneity does not simply attract traps toward regions of
low diffusivity. The optimal distribution reflects a competition between
where particles accumulate and where they are initially supplied. This
competition leads to arithmetic averaging in the weak-trapping regime and
geometric averaging in the strong-trapping regime. The equilibrium
preparation 
ω
=
μ
\omega=\mu
 is exceptional: these two principles coincide,
yielding an optimal trap distribution identical to the particle distribution
and a spatially uniform MFPT for arbitrary trapping strength.






