Within its stated numerical accuracy, the calculation establishes the central
point: a parameter that leaves every deterministic equilibrium and stability
type unchanged can alter the extinction barrier, and hence, subject to the exit
estimates of Section 
0.4
, the mean extinction time by a factor
exponential in the population size. Eliminating the fast signal before constructing the action
removes the joint cell-signal history that produces this difference. Rare
events are therefore not determined by the deterministic landscape alone, and a
reduction that is harmless for the equilibria of a network need not be harmless
for its exit times.






