Conclusion




A novel multi-dimensional consecutive background suppression technique was developed. Its performance and validity were verified with the two-dimensional sWeight procedure applied to kaon identification in CLAS12 cascade-baryon analyses. This technique reduces the complexity of the original 4D problem by factorizing the joint distribution into sequential single-particle components, thereby avoiding the need for a full 4D unbinned fit, diminishing computational cost, while retaining the statistical advantages of an event-by-event weighting approach. The method provides effective signal-background separation while retaining the statistical advantages of an event-by-event weighting approach. A clear demonstration of its performance is shown in Fig. 
15
, where both the ground-state and first excited cascade signals are preserved while the sub-threshold background is eliminated completely. The approach relies on approximate factorization assumptions and has some sensitivity to residual correlations between discriminatory variables, which are treated via iterative fitting and bootstrap procedure discussed in previous sections.






The method was applied to multiple CLAS12 RGA datasets, consisting of the Spring 2019 dataset with the torus magnet in 
inbending
 mode, Fall 2018 dataset with the torus magnet in 
inbending
 mode, and Fall 2018 dataset with the torus magnet in 
outbending
 mode, under very different kinematical conditions (electrons in FD or FT). The consistent reconstruction of the cascade signal across these datasets indicates that the procedure is stable under varying detector and beam conditions and can be readily adapted to similar analyses. The results of the validation study can be seen in Appendix A.






A dedicated toy Monte Carlo study was also performed to investigate the impact of correlations between the discriminatory variables used in the likelihood fit and the control variable reconstructed with sWeights. While signal-yield closure was found to be relatively insensitive to moderate correlations, the reconstructed signal shape exhibited increasing distortions as the correlation strength increased. It is recommended that this study be performed for analyses utilizing this technique, as the effect of correlations are best treated and quantified on a case by case basis.






The improved particle identification achieved with this approach is expected to benefit future measurements of strange-hadron production, where efficient background suppression is essential for isolating low-yield channels. The framework presented here can be extended to additional datasets and reaction channels and provides a practical foundation for future applications of multidimensional sequential sWeight techniques within the CLAS12 physics program or any other experiment.








