2609.38263v2,Robust LassoNet: Enhancing Feature Selection in Neural Networks via Robust Loss Functions,"This gap motivates the present work. By integrating robust loss functions within the LassoNet architecture, we aim to improve both predictive stability and feature selection reliability in contaminated data settings while maintaining the interpretability and computational tractability of the original formulation.","5 
Conclusions




In this work we introduced robust LassoNet, an extension of the LassoNet framework (Lemhadri et al., 2021) in which the MSE loss is replaced by robust M-estimation losses: Huber, Cauchy, Nonnegative Garrote and Tukey losses, to deal with outliers and extreme observations.






We evaluated the methodology on four synthetic nonlinear regression models under Gaussian noise and four contamination scenarios, and on two real datasets. The comparisons included robust versions of Random Forest and XGBoost and, for the real data, robust (groupwise) least angle regression.






Under uncontaminated Gaussian noise, the simulations show that robustness costs little in efficiency. The picture changes markedly under contamination, especially vertical outliers generated by mean contamination. The breakdown of the MSE loss becomes more severe as the regression function moves from Model 1 to Model 4, with the exception of Model 2. In Model 1, which has an explicit linear and additive component, MSE degrades but still recovers a good part of the active set. In Model 2, the effect is milder. In Models 3 and 4, where the signal is driven by nested nonlinearities and interactions, the MSE estimator degrades much more sharply. In Model 4 it fails almost completely.






We conjecture that this behaviour stems from the interaction between the
contamination and the hierarchical constraint. In LassoNet, a variable can contribute to the nonlinear component only if its linear coefficient

θ
j
\theta_{j}
 is nonzero, so 
θ
j
\theta_{j}
 acts as a gate that gives 
x
j
x_{j}
 access to the hidden layers. Whether this gate stays open along the regularization
path depends on how strongly 
x
j
x_{j}
 is 
linearly
 associated with the
residuals.






When the regression function contains an explicit linear structure, as in
Model 1, or is close to linear over the covariate range, as in Model 2, this
association is strong and the gates open easily. In Models 3 and 4, by
contrast, the signal is driven by interactions and nested nonlinear
transformations. The relevant variables therefore have only weak linear
proxies, even when their overall effect on the response is large.






Under the squared loss, the gradient with respect to 
θ
j
\theta_{j}
 is dominated
by the large residuals of the contaminated observations, which can mask this
weak linear signal. The 
ℓ
1
\ell_{1}
 proximal step then sets 
θ
j
\theta_{j}
 to zero,
and the hierarchical constraint (
6
) removes 
x
j
x_{j}
 from
the whole network before its nonlinear effect can be exploited. Robust losses
bound or eliminate the influence of large residuals, so the weak linear
signal is preserved and the relevant variables can enter the nonlinear
component.






Variance contamination leads to similar conclusions: MSE LassoNet collapses under strong contamination in every model, while all robust LassoNet variants keep stable prediction and selection. The tree-based benchmarks also deteriorate considerably as nonlinear complexity grows, most notably Random Forest in Model 4.






The real-data applications confirm the indications of the simulation study. On both datasets, the robust LassoNet variants show a degree of stability in variable selection that the MSE-based LassoNet and the benchmark methods do not reach. The four robust losses largely agree with one another and identify a consistent core of predictors across random splits, and this core is in line with previous analyses of the same data and, for the Atherosclerosis dataset, with the known biology. This stability does not come at the expense of prediction, since the robust variants remain competitive with or better than
the alternatives in terms of predictive error.






Overall, Robust LassoNet provides a reliable tool when the goal is not only accurate prediction but also reproducible and interpretable variable selection in the presence of contaminated data.",cues
