We developed a framework for analyzing the reliability and latency of VLSF codes over hardcore-regulated MAC.
Using an inverse Gaussian approximation for the decoding delay, we obtained a maximal-delay bound that depend on the transmitter-receiver distance, the target reliability, and the number of SIC-decoded users.
Since transmitters are indexed by increasing distance, this bound grows with the decoding stage, implying that the latency budget is dictated by the transmitters decoded last.
Numerical results confirm that hardcore regulation is suffice for uniform performance guarantees.
